      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>I\I°DOUGAII'SI PATRNT SE! ,F-P A !SING FLOun INDTGITTWITE! 111-1 E FX MTN ER, FRIDAY, JULY 13 1900</p>
        <p>can be no count abota, i</p>
        <p>0.,c4i: i</p>
        <p>good of him to refnsi, reporters. He coulri</p>
        <p>"Well!" he said at length __ _ _ _ •</p>
        <p>was Alizon who incited Kelly and the rest of them°, As it is, she will appear in an entirely different light." "If they don't themselves tell the truth." "Yes, yes; I must see to that. call on them on my way back. Anyhow, Rutland seems all right." "I hate him," cried Alizon, hotly. She End not meant to have spoken, but the words escaped her -r,4ore she was aware.</p>
        <p>"Ah, well, we must love our enemies, my child," remarked the old man, "and I think you have acted very nobly. But I must get back. I hope Ritzoorn's telegram will await me at home." "Tf Father Ritzoom comes, please bring him said Lady Neville. "Thank you, I will. Oh, Ritzoorn will turn the tables entirely. He's such a clever fellow. I ?hall have no fear if he can come. I wish I had thought of him before." When Father Sheen reached his house after making a few calls, he found a telegram awaiting him.</p>
        <p>"Cannot arrive before Monday night. Meet 6.10 train from Manchester. —RiTzoom. "</p>
        <p>"Our Lady be praised!" ejaculated the old, priest, fervently. shall be able to enjoy my dinner to-day." On the Monday evening Father Sheen was early at the station, and waited anxiously for the Manchester train to appear. "Oh, I do hope nothing has happened to detain him," muttered the old man, as it drew up to the platform. "Ah, there he is!" As some of my readers may possibly have become acquainted with Father Ritzoom elsewhere, I will not attempt to describe him here. Indeed, such a task would not be at all easy, for he belonged to that class of man which defies description. Father Sheen felt. this as he caught his outstretched hand. The older man somehow felt as though he were enve- loped by a strange influence, an influence which made him hesitating and nervous. When dinner was over, however, and they sat di wn before the study fire, the Jesuit unbent --miewhat.</p>
        <p>.'Let's have a look at your newspapers for these last two weeks," he said, as he lit his fist cigar. Father Sheen looked at him questioningly. "The fellows were talking about it in the railway carriage," replied the Jesuit, answering the query in the other's eyes. "I saw the placards on the walls as we came along, too. Some announcing the debate, and others informing the public that full accounts were given of the same in the Lynford Observer. Let's see the papers." The old priest gave them to him without a word, and then waited quietly while he scanned them. In three minutes he threw them aside with a gesture of impatience. "This is the bother, I suppose?" "Yes," replied Father Sheen, pathetically. "Let me tell you how the thing commenced." "What's the use of that ?—but stay, it may be of interest. Go on."</p>
        <p>The priest told his story with a fair amount of precision. Here and there he wandered, as though the facts were not clear to his own mind; still he gave the Jesuit a fair idea of what had passed. All the time he was speaking Ritzoom sat in a large armchair, smoking steadily. He never once opened his eyes, nor did a muscle of his face move. His large, square features were apparently in repose, as though the man had given himself entirely over to the luxury of smoking. "And that is all ! " he said presently. "Yes, it is all I can remember."</p>
        <p>The Jesuit went on smoking for several minutes in silence, his oyes still closed, his face as still as that of a statue.</p>
        <p>"I hope it is well,' " said Father Sheen, with a feeble laugh. "I hope, too, that you'll take my place in the debate on Wednesday night ? " "I certainly shall not." "Then I—l suppose I shall have to—to--meet him myself, unless—that is--" He did not finish the sentence, but relapsed into silence, his chin reHting heavily on his chest. "I suppose you know what you've done?" "No—that is--"</p>
        <p>"Excuse me for speaking plainly, Sheen, but we ara here alone. You've been a consummate ass, a blithering idiot. There, I take the liberty of an old friend."</p>
        <p>"I am afraid I have," replied Father Sheen, meekly, "I am afraid I have. My speeches were not to the point, and Rutland--" "Your speeches were all right—better, indeed, than I thought you could give." "I am glad to hear you say so," said the old man, evidently much pleased. "But you were a fool to tackle him at all. Never any good resulted from a public debate yet. They have always made more perverts than converts. In this case the fellow evidently wiped the floor with you.,</p>
        <p>- "I am afraid so." "How old is Rutland?" "About twenty-six, I'm told. He looks even younger." "He seems a fellow capable of pathos, passion, numour, eloquence." "Yes, yes, all that." "Highly sensitive, of an artistic temperament."</p>
        <p>"Yes, Certainly." " "He's capable of realising the historical spirit." g "Yes," replied Father Sheen, although he hardly knew what the Jesuit meant.</p>
        <p>"You must not meet him on Wednesday night No one must meet him, he must have a clear field."</p>
        <p>"But—but that will never do. Think of what the town will say. I shall be a by-word, so will the Church." "So much the better." "I —I do not understand."</p>
        <p>"I did not think you would. But that does not matter. Let him say his hardest things about us, let him prove us to be falsifiers of history, logic, commons,.nse, morality, everything good. The stronger his statements the better for us." "I am utterly at a loss to know what you mean." "Of course you are." "But why will it be better for him to say all these things, while no one replies? " "Because before they are forgotten, he will in that very ball recant them, deny them, and pray Heaven to forgive him for saying them I Then all his sayings will recoil upon the Protestants, and tell in our favour, and there will be a large ingathering into our church." "But how can this be?" "Oh, easily!" "But that will mean that he will have to be converted ?"</p>
        <p>"Exactly." "Oh, but you do not know him. He is a Protestant, every inch of hint. He is a student of history, he laughs at our claims, ridicules the Pope, and holds up our doctrines to contempt.. And mind, it will take a clever man to prove him in the wrong. I really did not think that Pro- testants had such a strong case. You see, they will not accept the authority of the Church, to begin with." "Of course not, and that is where you made a fool of yourself by arguing with him. But it does not matter ; indeed, we will turn it. all to our advantage. He is young; he will be converted." "But who will convert him? You? "No," and the Jesuit laughed quietly. "Who, then ?" "A woman!" "A woman! What woman?"</p>
        <p>"You must be dense not to guess. You will snow soon. I must see him. But it will be all right. I have not studied men a quarter of a century for nothing ; and as surely as my name is Anthony Ritzoom, of the Society of Jesus, it will be as I say."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, A friend has purchased such a becoming hat of black chip. lam told that it has a pretty droop- ing brim, with ir3ry graceful curves, and is trimmed with black tulle and a long black ostrich feather, which falLs over the left side, with a paste buckle at the tip. There is some narrow black ribbon velvet round the crown, and tulle rosettes appear under the brim on the left side. Another friend has a white straw of the sailor shape, trimmed with rosettes of white chiffon and white wings. She is wearing it with a dainty muslin and cambric blouse and dress. Another hat, which I have recently seen is of fancy straw, trimmed with folds of white tulle, veiled with white lace edging. There are wings of tulle and lace standing up in front, with a paste buckle in the centre, and under the brim on the left side there are rosettes.</p>
        <p>Just recently I have seen a beautiful lace gown in deep cream-coloured guipure, over pleated white silk muslin, through which the white satin lining is scarcely visible. The way in which the skirt is .made commends it to the unskilled. A plain breadth forms the front, the sides being finished -with the edge of the design itself. Two breadths, cut' away at the top, form the train, with a third inserted in a number of pleats in the very centre. 'The bodice has a yoke and under-sleeves of very .line, tucked silk muslin, the fulness from which .forms the vest, the lace being shaped like a low :bodice front and back. The collar is made of the lace, with narrow black velvet ribbon run through A chou of black chiffon with pleated ends 'trims one side of the bodice. The sleeves, rather wider below the elbows than above, stop short to show the under-sleeves. The latter end in a little double goffered frill, which falls over the hands. The hat to be worn in combination with this is a line black crinoline, with green feathers and a knot oi green velvet.</p>
        <p>' A bodice a lady was wearing yesterday was a 'very pretty one, the foulard being condensed in ktucks above the fulness on the chest. With 'blouses some muslin collars are worn with groins •of three or five small tucks, then a band of trans- 'parent lace insertion, and then tucks again, till the whole of the collar is ornamented. The shape ,of the revers and of the square part of the collars Ist the back may be varied to taste, and a pattern can easily be cut out in muslin to cover the revers of any coat or bodice. Remnants of nice lace • sufficient for trimming collars can frequently be ?picked up at a nominal price.</p>
        <p>Yesterday I saw a little silk coat made in a series of close tucks, the bolero outline being con- veyed by bands of stitched cloth edged with pleated frills of either silk or chiffon. The sleeves were finished in the same.way. The collar is made of perforated white cloth bordered with frills. The undersleeves are in crepe-de-chine with lace cuffs. This little coat can be made in black or coloured silk, whether glace or taffetas. Dark green is rather a favourite colour just now, and dark blue is always in favour, especially with a skirt in cloth, cashmere, or voile of the same</p>
        <p>colour. The 1;o1ero is only simulated in the trim- ming, the coat fastening the whole way down the front and ending in a little point. A summer dress I saw recently was of silk can- vas, pale tan in colour, embroidered with green Nhamrocks, and trimmed with wide lace. The little curved bolero was worn over a lace waist- coat, down the centre of which passed a long, full scarf of embroidered net, bordered with a deep lace flounce. A band of lace simulated a poloaaise. It may truly be said that woeful is often the fate of a mackintosh cloak. We wear it for an infrequent interval, and then, to our worry, we find that it has grown stiff and rigid, and almost stands alone. And, do what we will, it crackles at every turn. To prevent such an undesirable state of affairs, we should remember these few rules :—Never to hang it up in too dry a room; 'never to let it be dried in front of a fire or in the kitchen; always hang it up on a nail on a door or wall; never shut it up in a cupboard; and never attempt to fold it up when at all damp. A little oil rubbed into the mackintosh will oftenrften it when hard.</p>
        <p>Veils may be made to last longer if, each time directly they are removed from the hat or bonnet, they are carefully pulled out and ',at smoothly round a roller. A wooden roller about 20 inches long should be covered with several thicknesses of flannel, then the flannel covered with a piece of silk over which the veils are wound.</p>
        <p>Your loving, JE2OIIII.</p>
