        <p>THE TEMPTATION OF DUNC_AN RUTLAND.</p>
        <p>BY JOSEPH HOCKING,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Birthright," "Mistress AN aney Molesworth," "All :Alen are Liars," "The Story of Andrew Fairfax," Sze.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Alizon Neville, who belongs to an old Roman Catholic 'family, pays, for curiosity's sake, a secret visit to Tudor Chapel, the Nonconformist place of worship at Lynford. She hears the new minister, Duncan Rutland, and by chance is thrown across his path. The virility of his Protestantism makes her half diAike him. She fears he will do much harm among the Catholic population in unsettling their beliefs. Events so arrange themselves that at her instigation Father Sheen, her confessor, agrees to meet Duncan for a public debate about Roman Catholicism. In the first debate Rutland easily disposes of his Catholic anteg. nist. Much chagrined, Alizon unwittingly en:, ur ,ges some Catholic weavers to trap the your'e-, minis.er, so that he shall not appear at the seconddelytte. When she is fully conscious of what is happening, Alizon hastens to save him. She succeeds and Rutland, who is falling in love with her, is overwhelmingly triumphant at the second debate, but the girl feels that she dislikes him more than ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CHAPTER IX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IX.</p>
        <p>THE COMING OF PATHEIt EITZOOII.</p>
        <p>The next day Father Sheen's failure was the talk of tho town, while Duncan awoke to find himself, as far as Lynford was concerned, famous. Towards evening it leaked out that Shan Kelly and a few others had conceived a plot against him, and that but for Mixon Neville he would have had vitriol thrown on him. Tho day after that a host of garbled stories were discussed in the mills, each of which made the young minister a hero; and when the Saturday's newspaper appeared it contained, in addition to the report of the debate, a summary of the stories concerninz, his adventure at Boggart's Clough. A reporter had called on him, but he absolutely refused to speak a word concerning the matter, and this fact was also duly recorded. Altogether the town was in a ferment. The debate was the topic of conversation in three-fourths of the homes of the town ; it was fervently discussed in pul)lic-houses, while a special song had been written and sung at one of the music-halls.</p>
        <p>As for Father Sheen, he had kept inside his house. No one had seen him. The young priest who acted as curate had to do all the pastoral work, and in nearly every case had done his best; to Drove that Duncan was an ignorant clown; if not a liar. This young man had even hinted to his Superior that it might be well if the onus of the discussion were transferred to his own shoulders, as he felt quite sure he could, to use his own words, "make mincemeat of the blaspheming heretic."</p>
        <p>But the old man had not listened to his curate ; instead, he had sat quietly in his own study think- ing much as to what should be done. When he opened the Baturday's newspapers and saw the report concerning the plot against the young minister his heart sank like lead. Much as he hated heresy he loathed the thought of such an act. He .was a kind-hearted, honourable old .crentleman, who scorned to do a deed of meanness. .wn deep in his heart, while he was angry with nut and, ho admired him for the stand he had taken. He bore him no grudge, and if occasion occurred, he would, have done him a kind action. The young minister had kept entirely free from personalities, and his language had been fair and chivalrous throughout the whole debate. The old priest had. moreover, commanded the Catholics to t Jte no violent steps, and to use no menacing ltngutwe. Ho had been dragged into this debate against his will ; but he wanted to live at peace and concord with all men.</p>
        <p>"This will do untold harm," he thought, as he read the news. "I must. talk with Alizon about it. Anyhow, I am glad that it was a Catholic who warned him of his danger." Then ho read the report of the debate and noted the editorial comments. .</p>
        <p>"I daren't meet that audience again," he thought, sadly, "I daren't. It's a subject which will be even harder to deal with than the others. 'The Right of Private Judgment.' Why, Rutland can do almost anything with it in speaking to a Lancashire audience, almost anything. Oh, I'm not the man for it 'T know I am not But what can I do ?"</p>
        <p>He thought long and carefully, then he started up suddenly. . .</p>
        <p>-"That is-it," he cried, "that is it. T.Te C 1.1: see me through this business. lam glad thought of it!"</p>
        <p>He seized a telegraph form and wrote hastily: "Father Ititzoom, College of St. Joseph of Arimathea, Dublin. "Como here immediately. Most urgent. Wire time I may meet youe—Stamm." . . .</p>
        <p>This missive he sent away hurriedly, and after- wards threw himself into an arm-chair with a con- tented smile upon his face.</p>
        <p>"Yes, RitzOom will see me through this," he thought. "Ritzoom can see a man through any- thing. He's as clever as the devil himself, and as full of plans. I'll not trouble any more. Oh, I am glad to be rid of the whole business. But he may not he at the college, he may not be able to come. I hope and pray he can. Anyhow, I'll not worry. I'll go down to the Priory and have a chat. Alizon and her ladyship." Winn he reached the Pr;ory he found Lady Neville reading the Lynford Obsentr, while Alizon stood near her, eagerly looking at the books on the library shelves. " I am glad to see you," said Lady Neville, "very glad. I wish to talk with you." "Oh, I am glad to come," cried Father Sheen, "I have been so worried. I've neither been able to sleep nor eat. That debate—that debate has been like a nightmare to me." "Has been! Why, it is not finished yet." "Oh, yes, it is, as far as I am concerned." "How ?"</p>
        <p>"I could not go on with it, Lady Neville, I simply could not. My brain is all of a whirl. I am getting to be an old man, and I am not able to bear excitement. Besides, that last subject, The Right of Private Judgment,' our doctrine is not the kind of thing that goes down with Lancashire people." Lady Neville had felt this as keenly as Father Sheen had felt it, and had thought of writing him concerning the matter. "But what are you going to do?" "I have telegraphed for Father Ritzoom to come and see me." - -</p>
        <p>"What, Ititzoom the Jesnit ?" "Yes. He's the cleverest man I know, and I I think he likes Inc."</p>
        <p>"Of course he likes you, everyone does, but 1 am not. sure that 1.-" She stopped suddenly, evidently deeming it unwise to complete the sentence.</p>
        <p>"Ife'll see me through this—if he can come." "Do you anticipate that he will take your place in the debate ? "</p>
        <p>"Yes, I expect so. I really am ill, Lady Neville. Such another night as last Wednesday would kill mu. -I can explain in Wednesday morning's paw.rs, that being physically unfitted for the work I have asked a friend to take my place. You see, it is not. as though I hadn't done my best; I've not shewn myself a coward—and— and I'm an old man."</p>
        <p>"J think you have acted wisely," replied her ladyship; "but however clever Father Ritzoom may be I'm afraid the mischief's done."</p>
        <p>"En what way?" "Well, you see, the people will say that you're been obliged to call in an outsider. Besides, that affair at Boggart's Clough will not tell in our favour."</p>
        <p>"I never heard of it till this morning. What is the exact truth about this matter?" Alizon, who had been listening intently to what bad been said, now joined in the conversation, and told Father Sheen'exactly what had happened."</p>
        <p>"It seems that some people must 6ve blurted out the story themselves," remarked the priest i+lien she had finished. "It would seem so."</p>
        <p>"Just like them, just like them. If they had succeeded, it would have porno out all the same. What a mercy Alizon was in time; for if they had harmed Rutland in any way the town would have gone wild. I must say, however, that the young fellow has behaved very well." "I think so, too," remarked Lady Neville. "For my own part, although Alin does not like him, I think he is a one, gentlemanly young fellow. He's clover, too." 'Yes, he's clever." sighed Father Shea. "(bets</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CHAPTER X.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER X.</p>
        <p>PATIIES. RITZOOM'S PLANS.</p>
        <p>Father Sheen stared at the Jesuit like one demented. Being but a simple-minded old gentle- man, and knowing little of Jesuitical sophistry, he could not see his way through the subtle windings of the other's brains, fie therefore waited, open- mouthed, for him to proceed. !Ritzoorn lit his second cigar, and azain closecl, his eyes, az though he were thoroughly enjoying himself.</p>
        <p>"Whatever your taste in other matte', Sheen," he said, "in cigars it is beyond praise. These are simply lovely." "Bother the cigars!" cried the old man, some- what testily, "tell Inc what you mean." "Surely you can see." "No more than the dead." "And yet as a Catholic priest you profest to be a student of human motives."</p>
        <p>"But give me some clue." "Clue! Why, Sheen, you are a child. Let me aek you a few other questions, and in I D doing you will see what my plane are." "Ask unestione by ell IlleltTlr. "</p>
        <p>owe% 51st of all, tell me about Rutland. Tow nay he is twenty-six?" . "Yes."</p>
        <p>"And minister of Tudor Chapel, which you say is the most important Nonconforming meeting. house in the town?"</p>
        <p>"He has the largest, the most intelligent, and therefore the most influential congregation of any church in the town. People of all sorts crowd to hear him." "He is a scholar ? "</p>
        <p>"He is an M. A. of the London University. He took both his B.A. and M. A. during his six years at the Theological College at which he studied."</p>
        <p>"A gentleman ? " - "I should say so, certainly." "And good-looking ? " "Yes, one of these distinguished-looking fellows. He is tall and lithe. His face is rather pale. He Wears no beard, and his features are what you may call clean-cut. He has a fine head of dark hair. He would be noticed in any crowd." "He's not married, you say ? " "No. I hear he has no marriage entanglements. I suppose half the girls in his congregation worship him."</p>
        <p>"Of course; but he is seemingly a pronounced bachelor ? "</p>
        <p>yes.” "Splendid. Nothing could be better. Nothing. And you say he is humorous, sensitive, and passionate ?" _ _ _</p>
        <p>- "If you had heard him you would have no doubt about it."</p>
        <p>"You say he has met Miss Alizon Neville?" "Of course. I told you of the affair of last Wednesday night. Alizon saved him from the anger of some of our people who rere--very enthusiastic in the Church's cause." "This Miss Alizon Neville is a faithful child of the Church ?" "I verily belieie she would die for it."</p>
        <p>"So I jiidge. It was she who incited you to give that series of addresses, and to accept Rutland's challenge?" "It was," replied Father Sheen, sighing bitterly. "What does she think of Rutland ? " "She hates him!"</p>
        <p>"Good. She says so, does she?" "Yes, she is very bitter towards him. Alizon. you know, thought I should be able to—to— "Wipe him out, as he wiped you out." "Yes," replied the old man, lugubriously. "And as a consequence was all the more bitterly disappointed at your failure." "That's it."</p>
        <p>"And now hates him like poison ? " "Yes. I never knew her feel so bitterly towards anyone."</p>
        <p>"How old is she?"</p>
        <p>"About twenty, I think." "Just so. I have never had the pleasure of meeting her. Of course, I know of the family. The Nevilles are among the few who remain true to the Church in the important houses of Lancashire. I should judge from what you say that she is an ardent Catholic, but of an inquiring mind. That she is impulsive, sometimes rather unreasonable, but still faithful to the Church. She almost worships you, I suppose ? " "I believe she is very fond of me, and trusts me implicitly. That is natural. I have been a friend of the family for over-"</p>
        <p>"Yes, yes. Hasher ladyship arranged fcrr her marriage I' " "No. As you know, that matter presents many difficulties. There are so very few eligible young Catholic gentlemen in these parts. And lam sure Alizon would not marry a heretic." "Just so. I think you said she was good- looking?" _ _ _</p>
        <p>"A beautiful girl." "High-spirited, well-educated, fairly widely read, fascinating,romantic ?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, all thai.."</p>
        <p>Ritzoom again sat with closed eyes for a few minutes, then he threw the cigar end into the fire and lit another. As he did so he laughed with evident enjoyment. "This is splendid, Sheen," he said. "Still—l—l don't understand you," stammered the old man.</p>
        <p>"You will in a minute. Now, just another question. Lady Neville is a devoted daughter of the Church ? "</p>
        <p>"Yon know that, Ritzoom. She has been our chief stay and support in the town. She is the only important person of our faith in the district. She pays for two nurses, she has built a convent, she gives large sums of money for our work every year. The whole Church has no truer child than she."</p>
        <p>"Yes, I know all this pretty well, but. I wished to be perfectly sure. Women are peculiar creatures, and sometimes change without apparent reason. But all is well, all is well. These are really fine cigars, Sheen," and again the Jesuit closed his eyes and blew huge volumes of smoke from his mouth.</p>
        <p>"But what—what are you driving at, Ritzooni ? Why have you asked all these questions?" "To get you out of your scrape, and to win a host of converts to the faith."</p>
        <p>"But how ? " "Well, I will tell you. Things must take theit course on Wednesday, but you must not attend. You say there is a mid-weekly paper, and you must pen a few lines, or I will for you, saying that in obedience to the commands of your medical adviser you had to stay at home." "But I shall be the laughing-stock of the town." "No more than you have been, and even if you are it cannot be helped." "Then you go and say a few words. Ask a few questions in your own peculiar way." "I had thought of that, but it will not do. I am a stranger, and Rutland will be fully primed. No, let him have his own way. He will have only Protestants to speak to, that is if he sees fit for a meeting to be held." "Oh, yes, I see." "Well, then. we must arrange for Rutland to be invited to the Priory." "But why ? " "Because he must fall in love with Miss Alizon Neville."</p>
        <p>"Good heavens, man, you must belmad! What good will that do? " "You will see presently. She must, he very gracious with him. I also will meet. him there again and again. We will show how kind we Catholics are in forgiving all the hard things he has said about us: we will say nothing about our faith, but it shall be presented to him in the most glowing colours. He shall see it at its best. It shall be represented to him in one of the finest old homes in England. Ile shall see it, through the medium of refinement, culture, and an old name. More, he shall see it in the person of a beautiful, educated girl. And she shall be very winsome, very gracious. All the conversation he shall hear, shall lead him to see ideal Catholicism. He shall be overwhelmed by the greatness of our Church, he shall see the grandeur of our purposes, he shall understand the magnificence of our outlook, and every hing shall be beautiful f.o him because at every turn he will see the beautiful girl he will learn to love." "And then? "</p>
        <p>"lie will be fascinated, bewildered. Re will begin to compare the littleness of the paltry sects with our great Church. lie will be ouLthed by our autiquity, our tremendous history. 1 will be there to explain those dark periods of that history. Not ostensibly but apparently by accident. And he will iyield, he will as surely come to us as the tradi- Aional fly walked into the spider's parlour. All arguments of the ordinary nature will fail with him. I saw that by glancing at his speech. The critical ,faculty is strongly developed in him. But he is !young, he is ardent, and he will fall under the spell of a woman's eyes." "Well, go on." "Well, as I said, ho will fall in love wi:h your Miss Mizell Neville, he will become hu: as clay in her hands. lie, in spite of his education, is p• commoner, while she is the daughter of one of tht. oldest families in England. And, mind you, thr• most democratic Englishman that ever breath.eu loves what is termed noble families. For a time he will flounder, and then, because she continuos to be kind and gracious to him, he will make is confession of his love—will ask her to be his wife." "And then ? " "Then, why, she will tell him that she cannot wed an enemy to her faith." "Naturally. But afterwards? "Then all our preparatory work will take effect, and presently, he will resign his position at Tudor Chapel. After that he will declare himself a convert."</p>
        <p>"My word, Ritzooml" "After that you will have a great day at Lynford, for this skilled oppontnt of yours, thie man who has, from an argumentative view, used you as a mop, will stand up in that very hall, before the assembled thousands, and declare himself a convert to the Catholic faith." . .</p>
        <p>- - The two men sat quietly for sonae time without further speech. "And sunnose Rutland falls In w•ith 'CM i ?Mkt</p>
