        <p>Her Majesty's aversicn to the electric light may now I rihrosecled mong events past and gone. From time to time her consent has let en gained to thii and that sea, tit rooms being illuminated in this particular way, i few parts of the Royal Palaces are lighted in any other style save the Sovereign's private apartments of course, where, as ever, wax candies reign supreme. St. George's Chapel. Windsor, is now liehtcd by electricity, also the Royal vault underneath the Albert Memorial Chapel. Speekiee of Sc. George's Chapel, by the way, at the present time. the magnificent nr in fie cernetp irra,anr6 sometbe of' theiis, stops being altered and new ones added. 'the oidesi ergan in any church is claimed to he the one in Tewkesbury Abbty, which was built in 1637, and or, which Milton played. It; originelly belonged to Magdalen College, Oxford, but it S 9 pleased Oliver Cromwell, that be had it removed from there to Hamp- den Court, vs here it remained till the Restoration. It was then sent back to Oxford,but another ergan having been presented to the College, it was in 1737 removed to Tewkesbury. The next old, at orgae is believed to be the one built by Bernard Smith in 1660 for S. George's Hall, Windsor, and whic'e is now in Ina in a London , church. Some may wonder what the badge means with the I atter* c.E " which many vinyl,' are wearing. It denotes reernbership.of the Christian Endeavour Socisty which, is boldieg a great Convention at the Alexandra Palace in London. Briefly it is a se ciety to neemete Chlistian living,' mid it is said to number 3,000,000 membess, and over 50,000 separate unions. 'the Con- vention will be attended by debleates from Canada, Japan, America, the different countries of •ed in fact from all parts of the world. Some two or three tlitusand delegates are expected fr-m EA uu rs ot Australia, America, many of whom will pals the week—July 14. h to Jay 20tb—under canvas, so that, for the time hem., the Alexandra Park and Pelace will present all the of a huge catep. Dr. Francis Clarke, the faoPuPnedaerrajoh;cthe movement.wilt of course be present, and atro bkel will help in the sirgine. The Convention will addt essed by many w ell-knnivn divinco, and leadeis of the varioua religicue movements, from all parts of tee world. The Duchess of Fife has been about rather more than usual this sesson, and it has been frequently Tema- knd bow much less nervous Her Royal Elighnese appears th,n was the case years ago. Though shorn inc sscicte func- tione, which she disl.kee,sod ese r has disliked, inteasely, the Duchess really goes about a good deal, and in her quiet, unostentatious way, does an immense amount of I gcod. And with the sick ani eorrowf el, all trace of nervousness vanishes: Eler Royal Highness, on occasions I such a. these, shows herself to ha just what she is, a truly sweet, sympatbet-c we man. The Duke ot Fite was recently made a duke in a , doubie sense. His Grace, having no con, the intention was to secure the succession of the titles Cr, his daughters. It is new discovered that certain venrcls were omitted in the announcement of the reee creation in the 's London Gazette." and according to the genea- logical " Magazine " the titles would not in any ease en to tee second daughter, Lady Maud Duff—hut to another breech of the family, Cr perhaps to titi George Dunbar of Hemptiggs. This therry may he correct, bet the credal words apnear to have hen emitted only in the " Loudm Gnette " and not in Lc tters Patent, so that it can no doubt eailly be rectified. The jute ntion was. ot course, to preserve the title, among descendants of Royal blood, hut is, is qiiite roe- eible time there may one day he two Dukes cf Fife. The present Duke might become a widower, and marry again. It he then had R son, th,t son wnul,i heeom Duke of Fife by virtue of the original dukedom. At the same time the ttle under the second and reernily created patent might descend to the heirs male of Lady Alexandra or Lady Maud Duff, and we should then have twe Dukes of File. Not a few wi men will sympathise with the remarks of a Londen Police Court Magistrate on the subject of giv- ing false characters to servants. many mistrpece. . actinv from most mistaken ideas of kindness, do not hesitate to suppress the truth when information is souget as to the character of domestic servants and in this way- PO gave preyed to be the case in the matter which riae to tip. learned magistrate's remarks—they afford direet escourarrnent to dishonesty, and other misconduct. Ir. is a great shame that any woman should have so little regard to the rights of others,and aggravate in this way, the diffieietirea—alreadY sufficiently great—of the serv- ant question. Apropos, i appears from a decision cf tits Birmingnsm County Coutt Judge, that the role" No followers allowed "which obtains in many households, camel*. re enforced. A ce, k who bad accepted the poet of "chef de cuisine 'on the express condition that she would not receive visits from her "young man," was summarily dismissed fen breaking the agreement. The breach was admitted, but the judge made no difficulty about that p No human being, he said, could expect such a ridiculous rule to be obeyed, and rewarded the cook a month's: wages and costs in lieu of notice. If this is ! good law, those householders who ti irk they ere pre- . tected by any agreement as to" no followers" may as wei give up this so-called ridiculous rule. The sash is an important feature of the newest gowns. I Some' imee this is wide, onmetimes narrow ; eorre'imes it is knotted loosely at the side, at others tied in a bow, baby h &amp;Ann. at the back, but a sash of some stvle we must have, if we wfvh to appear " up-to.date," regards the fini,bing off of the ends, which should fall quite to the horn of the sown, these ins' he either fringed, embroidered, or overlaid with lane. 'he fringed variety standing p-rhaps ',lewd in our aft ctions at the wait.</p>
        <p>CRANE AND SONS, LIMITED, LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Toe. new linens are being made up by the furlong, fo seaside and country wear, an vry attractive wear th(y promise to be. In pale' c lourings, there are charming blues, pinks, mauves, and reds, while for those who pre- fer them there are middle and quite dark tones. Grey is in considerable r, quest, also cream colour, and tan. A visit to the drl-cleaners, and a g.iun—tailer built—of this fabric will return, over and over again. to all intenta and purposes, new. It will face an amount of red lan wear 11W -,re showing any sign of the campaign it has gone through. Thoac who dread partinc! with their youthful looks may do much to ward off the evidence of advancing ve.rs by trying to slap as calmly as possible. Some folks 7 takes th,. trouble.a of the day o bed with them, and con.equPntly the restraint over the facial muscles. which do,‘ so much to contort the features into wrink as to a. me extent continu-d during the night. We should therefore try to go to sleep, if not exactly with a beatific smile, with at least a serenity of mind which does not distort the countenance. Another great secret in preserving a smooth face is t akvass endeavour to keep the eyebrows in their proper place. These natural aids are open to all, and they tend to preserve one's temper as well as one's appearance.</p>
