      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>Warrington Drayman in Trouble at Northwich.</head>
        <p>A Warrington Drayman in Trouble at Northwich.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. J Wrst ,n and J W Deakin, st Northwich Police Court, last ninny, Frede.rick san- key, a brewer's dravmari, tr.,,ttl, was charged with being drunk in charge of two horses and a lorry, in til-qcha-ir lane. Marston, on Tnurs- day.—P.C. Mason said he saw tne lurry go pct, hi station. Prisoner was fast asletip, They drew up in front of the New Inn. and prisoner fell between the horses, and witnesa had great trouble in re-cu- ing him. Be was quite helple:4*, end Sereeant Prinion and himself harl.to carry him to the Polies Station —A fine of 15s 6d including costs was imposed, which was paid by his wife Proposed Explosive Stores.; at Bold. At St he en. Ccuory Petry 6ession, i•n I uesdiy, Mr J G :hompson, ninn.ger of the C llins Or. en Colliery. submitted plans to the ma2ist rates (Alder- man A Sinclair and Mr A Welmeley (loam) of t!,e proposed explosive stores to be erected in the town- ship of Bold. Ihe present stores were to be moved on account of come property being erected near. Superintendent Baxendale, the in,pector of elves for thedir tric , off-red no objection, and said it wa abetter poeition for the stores than the prc- sent one.—The aingiArates raact!oned the plane • THE MOST NUTRITiOUS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>E PS'S</head>
        <p>E PS'S</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>BREAKFAST AND SUPPER.</p>
        <p>broke in Father Sheen presently. "Suppose efery- thing turns out according to your plans, what is to become of him?" • "Impossible to tell until I've seen him. That is a mere detail. As you know, with us Jesuits the man is nothing, the truth is everything. Rutland is only a pawn on the chess-board. For the time te may seem a knight, a bishop, or what you like' in the game we are playing. in reality, however, he is enly a. pawn, and a chess-player pays but little. att.ntion to the pawns." "Still--" "Oh, yes, I quite understand you," said Ritzoom, as though he had fully divined what was in the other's mind. "From what you say, "- should think he has the making of a priest in him. Once let us get him to renounce his faith, well, then we've got him. Of course, it will be impossi- ble for Miss Alizon Neville to marry him. would never do. Nothing would be gained, and much lost, by such a course of procedure. Sha will, she must refuse him, and then I should not be surprised if he, crushed, bewildered, heart- broken, does not seek to solace himself by entering the priesthood." "If that could be done it would be a wondroue triumph," exclaimed Father Sheen. "The more so as, up to now, we can point to no single instance where a Nonconformist of anY standing whatever has joined us. To convert eb pronounced heretic, a Protestant champion, and to make him a priest—well, it should be paragraphed in every newspaper in England.. I would see to' that. "Yes, yes," said Father Sheen, fairly carried away by enthusiasm. • "It is a splendid scheme, splendid. All the more so because it is so natural, so simple. If well worked out it will atone for tuf shortcomings. Yes, Rutland must be invited to, the Priory. where Lady Neville and Alison must be kind. lam sure they will gladly, eagerly agree to it. And Rutland, he will be fascinated, charmed. Exactly. But you will have to stay here a few weeks." "I can give the matter just six weeks," said Ritzoom. "Six weeks! Can this be done in six weeks ? " "My part of the work can, easily. But under- stand I appear in your midst ns a Catholic layman. As such, too, I go to hear Rutland on WednesdaT night." "What, are you going to hear him ? " 4SCertainly. That is I shall go if he decides tc bold a meeting." Lifter this the two men sat a long time talking, euring which Father. Ritzoom smoked many cigarF. (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>SEAMY SIDE OF A PUBLICAN'S LIFE.</head>
        <p>THE SEAMY SIDE OF A PUBLICAN'S LIFE.</p>
        <p>LONG hours arid the many aLstieties attending the proper control of a licensed huuse help to make .a pair lican's life something more than an easy career, to BO nothing of the usual worries of mstrimony. At Droit' wich, the quaint old Crown Inn is held by a Mr. Trust whose wife is a buxom lady of thirty-seseu years. an the mother of seven children. Four pare ago,so a report in the " Worcester Advertiser " statoa, the wife v 7116" suffering very bad health, as she had eeffered for three. or four years previously. That this was a constant anxiety to her husband je evident from an observation made to a reporter of tbel' above paper on a recent cccasion. "1 was so weak,' Mrs. Truss said, "that I hadn't the strength to watig* Various docters in Droitwich, Worcester, and Sivas' grove were consulted, and they all said I was sufferiog frrm general deb.lity and chronic dyspepsia. For SilL weeks I was kept on a diet of milk, without even at atom of bread in it, and I fell into such a melanchOlY and exhausted state that I felt convi. eed I was pill to die. I could hardly drag myself act ,es the room was unable to eat or elect), and my health seemed ttl."'" utterly shattered. I was nursing a baby then, but tbe doctors told me to wean it at occe,or it would kill rae•ee " Customers at the house bad noticed the feeble staw I bad fallen into, and from time to time spoke of vellous cures effected by Dr. Williams' pii k pills for, ted, people, and urged me to try them. At last I consee - and sent for a box. When I had taken only halftlo ha_ contents I found 1 could eat better. Then I sent another box and when this was empty I could sile heartily and sleep so well that for a time I took no inart Since that time I have had nothing whatever to con3P,IBll of, but desiring to make the cure permanent, I 511174 querVly took two more boxes., I now feel strong:l7e- erjoy-the very beet of health. lam sure Dr. Willia`beri pink pills saved ray life, for I told the doctor OtlCe I get I went to see him that I feared I would die befere,everal home. I constantly recommend the pills for " weakness" stler- Worn out, haggard, unhappy, and continua—, ing • is tbe lot of too many women, and in the inalu`'_;, of eases this unenviable (x,st,,nce arises from OS weakness. When the system is revived auct the bios enriched by the. tonic influence of Dr. Williams' .pr‘ pills, digestion beccmes possible and nourlsbeiew derived from the food taken,while the [lt-Ives are sootb„., and regular sleep obtained. These pills are univers.lia.' recommended for the cure of ar malls, general wcak4rie lo,s of appetite, palpitatisn, shortness of breath, ee, decay, all forms of female weakness, hysteria. pasal,nts. Incomotor ataxy, rhi umatism, s&amp;at ice , scrofula, tic erysepelas, and consumption of the bowels and 100 Z., Rot thry are genuine only with the full fialnet Yo;, Williams' pink pills for pale people, and are v01;14, Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn Viaa'gw London, at two shillings and ninepence a box, of - boxes for thirteen and nine post free.</p>
