        <p>.</p>
        <p>Cemp,uy's works they were outseie toe borough where Mere were manufacturers whose valuations had not been inreased. The committee decided that the assessment was a fair one, and could not see their way to reduce it. MESSRS. GREENING'S WORKS. Mr. HATTON a'Bo appealed on betielf of Ma:srs- N. Greening and Sons azains the increased assesement of the Scotland-road works. at £496 grese an £397 rate- ohle ;an i the Crown-street works at £lB7 eress and £147 ra;eable. He stated that the firm was building a new works at the corner of Bowsey-road and Levels-lane in cons-crence of the present premises being unruitable for the requirements of their business. The pr mite's which ha i been re-assessed were on the point ot being ab,ndonel and in • hat case it seemed more a question of larking for a reduction. In reply to the Clerk Mr. Herron said the firm hoped to he able to remove to the new premises next year. The committee decided that as the firm only intended to remain a year longer on me present premises, to reduce the assessment to the previous figures. MESSRS. RYLANDS' BROS. WORKS. Mr ARTHUR BROWNE appsaled against the increeeed assessment of Messrs Rylands' Bros. works to £2.750 gross and £2.150 rateable. HA said the only portion of the works which was old wae that fronting Church-Ft The where were additions which had been assessed hy Mr Owen from time to time and had &amp;name el to £9OO or £l,OOO within the last 15 years. The firm did not wish to be unfair with the committee. Hs was able to tell them that the works were valued by Mr Rushton. who was an authority on the value of wire works, and his figures were lees than those fixed by the committee. He was in a position to make an offer to the cemmit ee that the firm would agree to an assessment of £2,500 gross and £2,000 rate 'his. The committee considered this a satisfactory offer and altered the assessment to that amnunt• Mr. A J JOLLEY : Evan on the firm's own showing they have been under-ossessed in the past. THE ASSESSMENT OF PUBLIC HOUSES. Mr. BROWNE said he represented something like 35 licensed victuallers who ware appealing against their increased assessments. These assessments had been increased without any valuation being taken and with out any reason or cause on the face of it except on the common knowlelge that they were bringing in a large amount of money and rent.; at the present time. He understood that the committee had decided to assess the houses according to the number of barrels sold. It was true that one house might he a very large house and do a small trade williet a small home might do a very large business, but it seemed an absurd decision to assess property according to the number of eustomers. They might just as well apply the same rule to a solicitor's office or a doctor's house. He was aware that the decision of the Rouse of Lords on the point was that the authority "may"assess according to the number of barrels used in the house. and he understood thse the Clerk interpreted the word "may" into the word "shall." That had been done in other cases, as for instance in the action of Longshaw v. the Warrington Water Works Company. Seeing that the Committee had agreed to 1189459 the houses according to toe barrels used, he wmild sugeest that a sub-committee he appointed together with the Clerk and Assistant Overserer,to meet the representatives of the Licensed Vieuallers and go into the subject. The CLERK : Do you mean that you would be willing to produce to that sub-committee details of what business wan done in the houses? Mr. Bnowsts : I think so. The CLERK: It would be useless meeting them unless they had that information put before them. If the information were given to the sub committee they could report on what they thought would be a fair valuation. There were certain hrus-s where spirits were largely sold and the number of barrels used would not be a fair criterion in such a case. A report could then be presented to the Assessment Committee. After discussion it was riftided that a sub-committee be appointed. consisting of the Chairman Vice-Chair- man Mt. Elickmen, Mr. Halton, with the Clerk and Assistant Overseer.</p>
