      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PRESENTATIOt-T1 TO MR ZIBA ARMITAGE, J.P.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATIOt-T1 TO MR ZIBA ARMITAGE, J.P.</p>
        <p>An interesting presentation took place at Cockhrdge Mills last Thirreday. The event was occasioned by the eomnletion of 40 years' connection with the mills of Mr. Z. Armitage, J.P., who, with the late Mr. Samuel Rigby became associated with Cockhedge on July sth, 1860. Alter the usual morning business had been trans- acted the Managers. Meeirs t.) H Hibbert and J Aine- worth. along with Mr P T Winskill, entered the office and in the presence of Mr S R Armitage, J.P., the presentation took place. Mr. RIBBER? said that he had been appointed by the workpeople to offer to Mr. Z Armitage their heartiest congratulations on his long connection with the M. 113. The workers desired that he might have continuod good health and that the Liminess would be even more succes.ful in the future than they bored an i-elieced it had been in the past. (Hear, bear.) They eelemed the opportunity the occasion gave to show their aohrt, Elation of his high character and business integrity. trusting that for many wars he might be able to intend that large concern. On hr half of the 1400 work people he asked Mr. Armitage'e acceptat,ce (4 a solid silver rose-bowl, beautifully worked, and two silver tern stands, all of shich had been supplied by Messrs. Eustance and Co., of S ink-5 -itreet. (Appian e.) The howl bore the following inscrirt•on :—" Presented to Ziba Armitage, Eq., J.P.. in cemmernoration of 40 ears' connection with Cockliedge Mills. and •FA a mark of esteem from the workneople. July sth, 1900" On each of the articles Mr. Armitage'e monogram had been engraved. In replying, MT. ARMITAGE said he wsj completely taken by surprise. but he ace,frpted the gifts with very great pleasure. Be had received other expressions of goodwill, when he reached his majlrity and on his merrine, articles which he highly v.lued and they had always been in prominent posit'ons in his home. These last gilts would not he less esteemed and would receive equal prominence with the others. Be and his co - directors desired to conduct the works in the interest of all concerned and always endeavoured to do what was right and fair to everyone. Be was glad to aelmowledge the kindly feeling displayed and desired the deputation to convey to the workpeople his sincere th.,nks for the Irelashis gifts. and No good wisher, expressed that mem (antatin.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 13, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 13, 1900.</p>
        <p>PLEAASNT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>CAN a pretty woman be plainly seen? CARVEES of their own fortune—butchers.</p>
        <p>TiEs of affection should not be woro in public. EVERY smrpeuter has a plane duty before him. IT is often the ease that poor whiskey is the reel thing. FEW people annoy their enemies, but nearly every one annoys his friends. A MODERN philosopher thinks that early rising is well enough as far as bread is concerned. As long as a man has lots of money there is something about him that other people like.</p>
        <p>A FATAL COMPLAINT.—" Wht did the black- smith die of, Johnny?" "Shoeing a mule." LAWYER—"And what is your last name?" Divorcee—" I don't know. I haven't married my last husband yet." "It does a man good to be rejected by a girl." " Why ?" "It makes him pitch in and marry a more attractive girl." "THAT whiskey is twenty years old Pat." "Is it? bedad I thin its mighty small for its age Eor.</p>
        <p>A man may fall in love a dozen times, but it is a question whether he ever sustains any perma- nent injuries by the falls. "SHE told me I was her king—that I had crowned her life." " Well ?" "And then she turned round and said she would never speak to me again if I didn't atop smoking." " WELL, Annie, how do you and Charlie like your new flat ?" "Oh, it's just heavenly." " an- deed ? So you are living in the seventh heaven ? That's nice.</p>
        <p>DESPERATE.—" Anything wrong ?" asked the manager of the show as he encountered an attendant running for a doctor. "I should say so. Sword swallower's swallowed a pin." WITH REASON.—" Your extemporaneous talk on art was grand, Miss Goggles." " Well, it ought to be; I put in three solid weeks getting it up."</p>
        <p>OCCUPATION FOR THEM.—" I wonder mhat the Arctic explorer will do after the North Pole has been discovered?" "Well, they can make trips to see if it is still there."</p>
        <p>THEIR OCCUPATION. When lovers spend the evening This programme is the rule: The fair maid plays the banjo, The young man plays the fool.</p>
        <p>TOMMY—" Mother, what is an angel ?" Mother —" An angel is a being that flies." Tommy— But, mamma, papa calls my governess an angel." Ntjuther—" Then, my dear, she is going to II), im- mediately.' FENSHAW-44 I hear you are to wed Colonel Swinger, Mrs. Grasse? He's a noble fellow, every inch a soldier, born to command" Widow GraHse —" H'm, we'll see about that, Mr. Fenshaw ; we'll see about that."</p>
        <p>PATIENT "Are you sure, doctor, that this health tood that you have recommended is nourish- ing ? " Doctor—" Sure? Why, I know it. The man who introduced that health food not only lives, but supports a large family on it." PASTURE.ISING.—Araious mother (to farmer) : "I suppose, ot course, you Pasteurise your milk !" Puzzled Farmer: "Oh, yes, marm ; leastways we pastureise one cow." IT MUST B.—Teacher: " What do you call a little child that has never had a mother to care for him?" Tommy: "An incubator-boy, ma'am." A CONFIRMED SHOPPER.—Aggie : "Why, Ethel, you are going dreadfully out of your way. You can match that ribbon right across the street." Ethel: "Yes, I know; but let us try in all the other shops first." Too MUCH PROGRESS.—Mr. Chipps (looking up from the paper) : "The doctors have discovered another new disease." Mrs. Chipps : "Well, I wish they'd stop looking for new diseases long enough to find a cure for my old rheumatism."</p>
        <p>SUE: "I know there's something I've forgotten to buy." He: That's just wat I thought." She: "Why did you think so?" He: "Because you have some money left. AN EXTREME CASE.—Twynn : "They say that young Ten3pot is deeply in love with himself." Triplett: "There is no doubt about it. He sends himself an expensive valentine every year as a mark of his undying affection." " DOBBIN'S wife is going to make him go in for geology." "What's that for ? " "She can't make him keep himself tidy, and she thinks if he is scientific, it won't be noticed." ON HER MErni.—Magistrate : "The evidence shows that you threw a stone at the man ?" Mrs. McDuff: "And it shows more than that, yer wor- ship. It shows that I hit him." Emma: "I am not going to that female bar- bers shop again. There is a wude girl there, don't you know." Algie "What did she say?" Bertie "Why, she looked at my mustawsh, and awaked me if I would have it spunged off or rubbed in"</p>
        <p>WAITING FOR THE FllN.—Daisy Bly (in a loud whisper) : •` Is dis de new French tutor, mamma ?" Mamma: "Yes, pet. Come and speak with him." Daisy Bly (hanging off): "When is he going to begin to toot ? " As TO the familiar problem, "What is home without a mother ? " a boy of our acquaintance says that it is pleasant enough if she leaves behind her the key of the cupboard where the jam is kept. "TALKING about happiness " said the matter- of-fact one, "it all cornea down to this—that nearly everybody's for locking up their house and u-searching the world for that contentment they've thrown away in their own lumber room." Son years ago a French horse won the Derby. Immediately after the race a Frenchman rushed down the course shouting. "Waterloo avenged." "Ye," remarked a bystander as the enthusiast passed, "you ran very well on both occasions." PUTTING, INTO PRACTICE.--Mamma " amOtily, tio you know who ate my raisins ?" Dorothy (i.urhing over the leaves of her book more rapidly): "Mamma, you told me yesterday some things are better left unsaid. Isn't that one of them, don't you s'pose ?" AccouniNo TO CAPACITIL—Mrs. Berry (glancing across tt:e table): "I'm Ifraid my little girl isn't e:,j)ying her dinner." Rachel (who has left her replding half eaten, with a sigh) : " Yes, mamma, io much as I can; hut, of course, if I was bigger I'd enjoy more of it. THE SEQUEL —"No," said the tortoise, haught- ily ; "I have won the championship, and I shall retire. I shall never, never race again." And the Welters of the hare departed mournfully. They were willing to bet fifty to one on the result of a return maielt.</p>
        <p>STICKLERS POR ETIQUETTE.—Excit•ecI lady (on the buKcii): " Why isn't soinething done for that 144 in distress? Why don't, sotos of yon-" Coastguard (hurriedly): " NVe've sent theni line to conic ashore, mum." Excited Lady: "G cat gracious! Were they waiting for a formal in</p>
        <p>ITS A DVANTAG E TO GEorAE.—The Washingtons had unexpected company for dinner, and the pumpkin pie was rather scarce.—" George, would fike another piece of pie?" asked the head of iha family, who was certain that his well-bred son would refuse a second helping. "Yes, father. I would," answered the truthful boy. " You ought to hnow by this time that! cannot tell a lie."</p>
        <p>How To GET SERVED WELL.—Mr. P.: "How do you alvolys manage to ha Ve such dolicious bat ?" Airs. B. : "1 select a good, honest but- ci,er, and then stand by him." Mrs. P.: "You rri:eti that you give him all your custom?" Mrs.&amp; : •‘ ; I moan that I stand by him while he is citaing of: the me, t."</p>
        <p>NOT EXACTLY MiTHETlC.—Mistress (to servsnt girlo no apparently takes an interest in the 01.jects of art in drawing-room): "Well, Mary, with one of those figurei do you like best?" Ei,.-evant (pointing to armless figure of Venus de N 1110) : "'['his ono, mum." Mistress: Ant' why do you like Venus best?" Servant: "Well, it's the ai:iieet to doost, mum." AT an auction sale in Fife the antioneer was try- ing to Bell a number of domestic utensils, including a p•irridge pot. As usual, he was making a great fuss. Finishing, his keen eye caught a well-known worthy, the beadle, standing at the back of the crowd, and he shouted out—" Master Weeluinson, make me an offer for this pot. Why, it would make a splendid kirk bell." "Ay,' replied the beadle, "if your tongue was in it."</p>
      </div>
