      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MESSRS. WALKER'S EXCURSION.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. WALKER'S EXCURSION.</p>
        <p>Beautiful Weather and a Successful</p>
        <p>Outing.</p>
        <p>Once more the employes of Messrs Peter Walke and ous, tne well-known firm of brewers, have had their alinual excursion. The place visi:ed this year was Morecambe—" the Naples of the North "—and the outing pas-ed off As successfully as any chat has yet been 'meld. In accordance w:th the usual custom each emplq6 was presented with a railway ticket and also received an allowance for pocket money. xcellent preparations had been made under the superin eulenca of Mr. J Fulton Smith, and all that was necessary to ensure an enj3yable trip, was fine weather. The climatic con ditions on Saturday, could no have been more favourable. The suu shone radiantly hroughout the day, whilst the heat was tempered by an exhilarating breeze. The first train left Bank Quay Station at 5.40, whilst the second fo!lowed at six o'clock. A saloon was provided for the heads of the firm, and refreshments were partaken of en route. After a pleasant journey Morecambe was reached shortly before eight o'clock. Most of the trippers at once proceeded to restaurants &amp;c., for breakfast,and afterwards dispersed to spend the day as they wished. Some visit ,d the historical town of Lancaster, whilst others went to the ancient village of Heysham and to Bare to see the artillery camp. Sea trips afforded pleasure for many, and the railway tickets admitted of frets access to the Central Pier and Pavilion where Mr. Mel. B. Spurr and a first class company provided ent-rtain- went. The niggers on the Beach also received their share of patronage. At seven o'clock in the evening the managers and a number of invited guests adj urned to the Sings Anne Hotel where dinner was served. At the close the usual toast list was gone through. Mr. Nlouro pre•ided and the vice- chair was occupied by Mr. J. Fulton Smith.</p>
        <p>Tb toaAs of the ''Qt-ieen " a 1 irfh Prince of Wales and the rest of the Royal Family " were proposed by the Ch irman and musically ho loured.</p>
        <p>In propoising "The Army, Navy and Reserve Forces" the CHAIRMAN Bald that 'he war in Soleil Attica had exposed some of the shortosminks of the Army, which, however, could be overcome hut it had also shown the spiendid calibre of on: fighting forces. The soldiers had done what had been expected 01 them but the Volunteers had exzelled themselves and proved themselves worthy to be associated with the regular Army, which had always been considered one of I hegra ndest fiehti..g forces al the world. In many ways the Volunteers had proved themselves to be superior, he believed, and if they were willing to volunteer to go out to tight, surely, if thee were called upon at any time to defend their bnlxtr s would they not show a still stronger spirit of sactifice and bravery. Mr. Monro al;c• reforr(d to tne reservists who he said, if rr quired would j An the colours in thousands and produce a force that would be absolutely unconquerable by any nation of the world. be Navy were always liked. They were ready and steady, and tearless and jolly. He hoped that the great confidence that had always b4n reposed in them would continue to xist and that the Navy would give them turther proof that they were as good and better than the r forefathers were bider, them. (Applause.) Mr. Bitooxs sang "The flag that braved a thlusand</p>
        <p>Alderman HUTCHINSON and Quartermaster POTTER briefly respondt d. M . Fur:rote SMITH proposed" The town and trade of Warrington," coupling with it the name of Cluncillor Lee. Re said he thought there were over fifty trades carried on in the town, not the least impor:ant teinzl that of brewing. They had been taented with the fact that the town was kept under a cloud of smoke, but if it was not so he dare say it would not b. very well for ' them. The Corporation were d )ing their best to dispel the smoke by introducing improvements which would no doubt be of benefit to the town gen. rally. A song was kiven by Mr. WOLLACOTT. C iuncillor Lim responi'ed to the toast. Mr. JENKINS sang Good-bye arre-.heart, good-bye." Alderman FRANCOMB proposed "Success to the firm of P. ter Welker and Sou.' He said they wou!.l all agree that it was a firm that deserved every success it was possible for a firm to gain. Referring to the progress of I the fi,n2 be said 'be trade had increased by leaps and bound-. There must be EOM reason why this eTantic , ousiness had grown in such a sh rt time and in such a comparatively small town as W.rrington must have been when they commenced. lie had never had the privilege ef knowing the late Peter Walk, r but he bad heard that he was a splendid bu-inees man of kind• hearted disposition and was always willing to help on a good cause. Thee the business was carried on br a very eminent man--the late Sir Arelrew Walker. They all knew him and respect him. 111 did not know he had ever beard a man utter a wrong word about him' and be had beard volumes in his favour. Since the business had been turned iato a limited ,liability company, the sam tett and ability had been brought to bear and that was the reason why such wonderful success had crowned their efforts. The suc- cess in the present day was due to the officials that Sir Peter Walker bad always possessed their honoured eh ef being Mr. Monro who was present that day. (Applause.) Down to the poorest boy whim ran errands there was one desire and that was, to do their duty and he was sure that when men were banded together with a strong desire to push on the firm, that firm was bound to be a suocesa. Another thing that had a great deal to do with its success was 'he wonderfully good treatment that was meted nut to the workmen by the proprietors of the company. Ha wag quite certain that there was net one of the managing directors who would not be willing to speak a kindly word to the poorest labourer for the firm and if mirortune befel anyone he was sure that Mr. Munro could go with the greatest ease to any of the directors and get whatever help was neceesary. He was certain that so long as workmen weretreatt d as fellow men th y would do their best to repay the firm. Another reason for the success was that the firm got the Warrington water. It was a remarkable thing that the firm of P, ter Walker and Son, was the bigge.t customer on the Corporation hooka. (Hear. hear) He was sur, that so long as they stuck to the Corporation the Cnporation wr ud s jolt t th m and the result would be good beer and silences to Warrington. ZApplause). He coupled with 'be toast the name of Mr. John Baird, the oldest member of the firm.</p>
        <p>The Customs); sang" The Diver," In which be was vigorously applauded. Councillor BAIRD, in responding to the toast. said that he was employed by the firm when the late Mr. Peter Walker f,,undod it 37 years ago. H. had also had the pleasure of working with Sir A B Walker. The firm had always studied the men who wolked for thew, and by doing so thfy were studying the interests of the people who patronise.) thi.m, and if they made good beer they trot a good sale. (Hear, hear,) Mr P. WELLs gave a sang. Councillor TINNION proposed "The Railway g7om- ponies," and in don g so said be wag surf the railway companies bad contributed very largely to the success of ..he trade of Warrington. He had always found their representatives to be most c urteeus and desirous of doing all they could to furtbor the interests of the pro- pr,etors Mr. LONGFORD gave a humorous recitation which crf eted much laughter. Metsrs Tuomrsolv, BICKFoRD and TAYLOR responded to the to,Firs.</p>
        <p>Mr PARRY, Of Liverpool. proposrd "The Press," and the rues wag duly bnni,urpd. This trims's: nf 'ha" The Gut q " prmposf.d by the CHAIRMAN', arid recpardod to by Dr. BRAIDE. _ _</p>
        <p>The toagts of " 11;i1r. Ful'on Smith " and " Th. Chair- man " were rnthusiaaticallY honoured and tho p-oeeed- inoi terminated with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>The return jonrn.y wag cnrnmonred at abnut 10 30 by the first train and 11 n'cck h thn s cnrsrf. WArlington Leing reached at 1 o'clock on Sunday morning.</p>
        <p>Successful Warrington Athletes—P.C. Robin- son Captures the 'Waring" Shield. Several Warrinki ton men competed in the Liver- pool Police Sports on Saturday. P.C. J Robinson was again a successful compe!itor, and is to be con- gratulated upen his cap, urn of the "Waring" shield. The shield was pr.sented to the society by Messrs. S .T Waring and Company, Bold-street, Liverpool, and is held annually by the Chief of any police force in the United Kingdom, whose representative scores the highest number or marks on the fore- going scale, individually. The individual winner also receives a gold medal PC. Robinson was adjudged the victor he having been accredited wi ii five points. He gained the first pr'7., in the 440 yards figt race and was second in 'he yar handicap. PLC. F Whittaker ran second in the 100 yards scratch, 3 Prince. of Sankey Harriers, was third in the handicap sprint. and A T llaintir h,War- rington, waa similarly placed in the two miles bicycle handicap.</p>
        <p>Wirral and Birkenhead Agricultural Show As will be observed front the advertising columns all entries for the Wirral and Birkenhead Show, to he held on August ;:rd, 4th and 6 h, clutto by next Thursday's post, and none will be acceptsd after- wards under any circumstances. Additional class '8 for cattle, sheep and horses are provided this ycar. and the price money in each section has heen con- siderably increased. Schedules and all information can be obtained from the Secretary, Mr A.Tthnr H ,Edwardson,6, Hamilton-square, Birkenhead.</p>
        <p>BUT BOOTS To ALL LJNfIRTaECLOI. • v.t. PiTBLIO BBNBIPIT UNDSR TEU, OLOCIC ioa BEST BOOTS 1.3 LABOASHLIX.—Advt.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING FATALITY AT WARRINGTON.</p>
