      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Girl Killed at the Cable Works.</head>
        <p>A Girl Killed at the Cable Works.</p>
        <p>An Unprotected Shaft.</p>
        <p>The inquest on the bo iy of Elizabeth (Cissie) Allen, (cotton tiraider, of 16, LiHord-street, and datuntbr of Mr. John Allen, newsagent). who wt a kilie t while at work at the St. Helens aril Warrington E ec,ric Cable Works, Bank Q lay, on Touraday was open-d on Satur day morning in the Parade Rosin at the Police Station. Mr. Brighouse was the cor ,ner. Mr. Bro woe appeared f r the relatives of the deceased. Mu ju. Roe, Factory Inspector, was present on ben tif of the Govern- ment.</p>
        <p>The Coroner asked if anyone represented the Cable Works, and Sergeant Sinita replied that there were two witnesses present,</p>
        <p>The Coroner : The represeleatives of the works ought to be here with plans and evidence. /lime they nu lawyer or manager here? Sergeant smith replied in the negative. The Coroner: Did the employers know the inquest was to be held this mornin: ? Sergeant Smith : I slid I suppose you will send someone to represent the firm, and the repiy was "There is sure to be someone there*"</p>
        <p>I,lin Allen, of Scotland-road, the father of the deceased, said his daughter was 23 rars of age last birtioday. She was empio)ed as a c tton braider at the works. He iliac saw her alive on Tuesday atter she had left her work. She had been in the empto uf tie com- pany for about five weeks. She war a dressmaker by trade,and had no:: been previ(:usly employed at 0 works. acswer to Ilr.Browne, witness said he was refused admission t. the works by Sergeant Smith, and he told him be should rl33rt he matt,r to the coroner Sergeant Smith eltnlained that he ref us,-d Allen ad- mission to the works because he wanted to go in with the jury. Tue Coroner : Quite right. He ought t 3 have seen the manager and gone in either before or after the jury bad been.</p>
        <p>Witness, in answer to Mr. Browne, ssid the manager told him that tha place was just as ne then saw it when the accident occurred. He Lela him that the place was expoard without protection. Tne Coroner : There has been clearly an offence against the Factory Act by the employers of the girl. They bad no right to have a revoivn2s,, shaft without the fencing r-quired by the Act of Padisment and the rules formulated under the Act.</p>
        <p>Furtner questioned, Mr. Allen eaid he understood his daughter's dress was caught in the machinery. The Coroner: If the einuoyers have anything said against them it is their fault tor not b ing here. Robert Gregory, in tile employ of the Electric Cable Company, said that at this particular plsco the works were not yet completed. Toe shaft was 18 inches out unprotected, and it was going at the rate of 140 revolu- tions a minute. She was turning the drum tor him and he supposi d she mu-t nave backed into ths macanaery. The girls were not undor his control, but he assisted them. If the works had been finished no one would have dreamt of leasing the shatting onprote-ted. Witness had not Ipen employed at the works long—only for a ek.—By Msj )r Re: fl c iuld not say how long the sh.fting had been in us'.. lie supposed the manager had tull authority to fence the s'aaf :mg in the room. It ilev,,r struck him that the expos,d end of the ehatting was dangerous.—ln answer to Mr. Browne witness said he was a hairdress,l by trade. The Coroner: AU the more reason why the shafting should have been protected. 13,P intoimed the jury that Mij ,r Roe had been to the woks and that be nad seen either the secretary or ass,stant-se3rertry and the man- ager and told them distinctly that they ought to draw the girls from the place or trop the machinery. It was not respectful to the jury or to himself—it was not fair to any of them—that the employers were not represented that morning at the inqu.st to give the j ary all the information they want, d. Liis view was chat they ought to adjourn the inq airy, otherwise no one would be satisfie i. Mr. Brown's client would not be satisfied, nor would Msjor Roe. A Juryman We could no get any straightforward answer yesterday when we visited the woiks. The Coroner: lam rather sorry you asked anything or that anything was said to you. I would rather you made your .inspection without saying anything.</p>
        <p>Tivinquiry was then adjourned until Weil:lf:2day morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>ADJOURNED INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>THE ADJOURNED INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse continued the inquiry in the Parade Room on Wednegday morning. Major Roe was present on behalf of the Government; Mr. Jack.qm (Oliver Jackson, Thiliton and Company of Liverpool), and Mr. Viacilonald (manager) for the company; Mr. A. Browne for the relatives. The Coroner said he had a couple of letters from the company. The first, dated the 7th, was to the effect that they were extremely sorry that their re- presentative was not presen, at the opening of the inquest. The police sergeant bad told him that be would be sent for at the proper time, but no word was lefG as to the time and place of the inquest. Toe Eecoad lever, dated the 10th, stated that the works manager. Mr. Macdonald, was instructed to represent the firm at the inquest, and had arranged with the police to let him know when his presence was required. The police promised to s.nd for him. The matter had been sally mismanaged on the part of the police, as far as they could make out. Toe Coroner said he would hear what the witnesses had to eay about the matter.</p>
        <p>Charles Beecher, thA worka enjneer, sjd the sta'e- ment in the letter was correct. Be hed been ask ,d to attend the inquest' on behalf of the firm Toe e,,-geant told him he would be sent for at the proper time. He told him the time but not the place. Mr. Maod,nald s+id he was iostructed to attend the inquest, and w.is told that the police sergeant would let him know the time and place. Sergeant Smith sa,d be told Mr. Bet cher that it was customary for a reprosentsti,e of the company to at. tend. Mr. Beecner s*id he would be there He (S-,r- Remit Smith) promised to warn a witness named Mac- donald it he aere needed at the inq lest, bu: he was not. He sent for the other witnesses anu they came from the works.</p>
        <p>Tue Coroner said his opinion was that a large firm should know without having to b) told, that in a case like tnis they should b represent d. roe jury and he wanted to have all the inform:l,ton they possibly could. The necessity in cases like this for a representative of a company to attend th iequest and p,oduc, a plan nad been mentioned in the newsp spers several times. Apart from tnat it was a graceful act if only to show their good feeling to the friends of the deceased! To keep away seemed to him to be v ry bad taite. J Allen, father of the ffeceased girl, was recalled.— In cross-ex ,Mi,latinn hy Mr. Browne he said his diughter had to work overtime until tea o'clock at night aod also worked on the two Sundays preceding the accident. th C 'toner : Row do's this eff ant the case ?</p>
        <p>Mr Browne It tends to snow that she was so over- worked that it may hive contributed to the accldent.</p>
        <p>Witness (continuing) said that his daughter was told- that if she did nt work on Sunday she neel not come to the works aL:ain. By Maj ,r Roo. Oa what date did she work on Sunday ? WqnPsa : Oa the two Sun Jays b fore the accident. Major Roe : How do yov know tnat ? Witnosa : She :old me tiersrlf. Major Roe : Di i you tire h. r going to work ? Witne:s : No : but her sister Fanny and her brothers did.</p>
        <p>By Mr. JackFon : D 1 you ear he W:l9 told by the manager, Mr. Macdonald, that ,f lb() did not work on Sunday she hPe,l not come agaio ?</p>
        <p>: I don't know whether it was the manager who told her, but some one did.</p>
        <p>H ibert Gregory was as r'-callAd and repeated his evidence. He vim+ awaro that a- thP first inquiry he said the shaf- which caught the deceased bad beeu working a week, but f.-otn what he had learned eince be understood that it had not been working more than a day or two.</p>
        <p>gy Mr. Browne. : You callod her to you didn't you ? —No.--But you asked for soatt help and this girl ceont ? —I told ht.r not to nome.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : You accPpted her aerviceg and now you want to turn round and env 6he had no right to do it.</p>
        <p>By Major Roe : You collrin't do it single handed ? Wicriesr4 : I mizht have done i.—Cut you have mtni- pulatsd two drums i'—l could have done it at tke risk ot spoiling aline work. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Jaclaon : I think it is true yon only came on the previous Manda 7 ?—Yes, tbst was so.—You 'said on Svurday that the shaft was mooing' the whole of the time pal were there ?—Ye, I have made inquiries since and am d uhtful whether that was so.-1 think as 3 rule you have an asaistant ?—Yes, bit be was away at the time.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Duwnes (a juryman) : How long bad she been svorkir.g in that p.talcu'ar spot before the accident occurred ?—Three or fo.r minutes —Had you had the assistance of the lemal,.B bufore v—Yea, one of them. Eli n Farrington sta..ed that sha Ind been en4a2ed at t e works for anent hur months as a cotton braider. Mit.s Kelly was the Frewoman and they could not leave the machiue:s w:thout her p Tmission. She di.) not know how ln the sivaf:. had been running. Questioned by the Coroner she denied that she said at thP previous inquiry that it had been running the day before the accitient and the day before that. The Coroner ; I knob the impression that is getting on my mind after hearing these witness s.</p>
        <p>Mr Browne (to witneee) : Who his b opeaking to lieu since the last inqulry.—No ene.—ln auswer to farther cmstions, the witness imid tbe hours of wlrk wen trona Witt OWOl* to sic She hod setnetiavi</p>
        <p>worked until ten o'clock bu:; had not worked on a Sun- day. Charles Beecher, s (id he was the enginrer for the works. but he practic Lily k tow nothing about this par- ticular shaft. lie did not fix it up and gave no instruc- tions for it to be fixed.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : Gsn q.l tell me how long the shaft had been revo,ving ?—Wi nem: From the day previous to the accident.—You cannot get over the fact that when toe crirl came by her death ths shift was unpro- tected P—No.</p>
        <p>Maj )r too: Who nut in the shaft ?-11Ir Finnegban : Although you are the engineer ot the works you didn't ' know that the machinery was altered P—l was working at the other end of the, wok s —Unknown to you this shaft was cowl d up ?—Yes —Who did the work P—Mr Finneghan.—Di in't you tell me on Saturday morniniz that you were the foreman ?—fslo sir, I don't think so.— Did you tell me the ehaft had to..en running for -two days ?—I can lot say I did. I don't remember because I was 80 worried.—Were you so worried when I saw you that you noel knot? what you said to me P—l cannot say that.—You lora kno 7/ what you said, so you deny it ?—I am not prepared to cPny anything. Th Coroner: You 4fe making an impression on my mind stronger thal i was. By Maj it Roe El? CV many women and girls are em- ployed ?—Abour. 30 --I pointed out to you on Saturday similar shafting winch 1 ,:onsidered dangeroua, and or- dered it to be fenced or the women removed, what have you done slime P—The machinery has been fenc-el in Is there anyone here who will be able to tell me tow long this particular shaft has been running ?—Yes, we have the man here who fixed it.—Who gave him orders to do it P—Ele did it without telling me. I dou't go through the ro ,in more '.h an twicl a week.—Tnen you don't know what is goiog on ?—Well, the time has been so short in this case.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Browne : Had this girl been hurt before ? No, not as lam aware of.—Was it never reported t.) you that shs had her fiagers caught P—l never heard of that: Mr. Roe: When was that ? Mr. Browne (after consulting Mr. Allen), said it did not prevent the girl from working and was not a serious matter</p>
        <p>By a Juryman: Des your assistant engineer often fix naacbinery witbow, voar knowledge ?—Yes.—Then ne is equal to you ?—Ye l in 13J1110 respects. He is supposei to let me Ito )4 what be does, oat he did not do so in this perricular case.</p>
        <p>Herbert Finn-ghan, aesistant engineer at the works stated that rhe shaft was c innected up plrmsnently on Wednesday h-tweea 12 and 1 o'clock. It ran from that time until tie aced e.it occurred. It was temporaril; fixed up on Tuesday morning, but he dii not think any accident could have occwred whilst it was temporarily fixed. He did not rezaro i as a dangerous shaft. By Major Pole: Wont i you have put any fencing roiand it ? —Yes, there w -mid have been fencing round eve yone of the ends.—Are you a practical engineer ? I h.va served five yeas at a cotton mill —Have von never known a person caught by a shaft ?—No, but I have hear] of them being caught by pulleys.—Who gave you orders to couple up too shaft P—N ot_e, I have been in charge of the work of erecting the braider. an] most of the machinery, and have used my own discreti .n.—You Are entirely ignorant of the provisions for fencing ?—I don't know that I have read them.—Are you responsible for this shaft having been put in and run unprotected ?—I should say BO it I hava done wrong.—lVill ou tell me who is re- sponsible if you are nit P—Mr. Beecher.—He says he knew nothini ab 'iut it ?—I didn't tell him personally that I was potting the steift on. I don't tell him every little thing IdO about the works—Supposing you dis- connected a soaft wont I you think you were justified in doing so without telling anyone 2—Providing I was doing it for a right purp,se. By Mr. Browne : Is it not a notorious fact that at these works there is a great deal of overtinv?—l ca not say that it is witerious.—But they do work uutil ten o'clock? —Yee semetimes.—Don't they work on Sundays? —Not as I know of. Igo to Manchester at the week- end.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said as far as be was concerned he had herd ennugh evidence.</p>
        <p>The jury agreed. klaj Jr Roe sill he had visited the works and un- doubtedly the seaft in qu .stion was mill gearing under the Factory A t and ougat to be fenced In his ex- perience a plain shaft was m re dangerous because ignorant peop:e did not understand its danger. The air that was carried round by the shaft was sufficiunt to draw in any light clothing and was especially dangerou to worn u 3 owing to the clothing they wore. It was a dangerous shaft aid ought to be fenced. The Coroner in addr,issing the jury said the firm Foamed to mike a poin: ef the fact that the shaft had °ivy recently been coupled up. But it was clear that it was revolving for two days. INT attempt bad been made up to that time to feeee it. It was au illegal act to let it run for two days in that unprotected state. The jury returned a verdict of •• Accidental death," and made a presentment to the effect that the accident arose in consequence of the shaft being unprotected and that it ought to have been protected before it was clupled up. Toe Foreman of the jury (Mr. Whittle) said he was instructed by the jury te point out that they tad given two half days to the inquiry an.i would be glad if they c iuld be ex-mptel from further suvice as juryman for some time to come.</p>
