      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>EX A. MIN ER ,</head>
        <p>THE EX A. MIN ER ,</p>
        <p>JULY 13TH 1900.</p>
        <p>THE fallacy of the time is Strong Government. that a large majority</p>
        <p>means a strong Govern- ment, whereas it often means the reverse. A strong Government must be compacted of Minis- ters who are zealous, competent, and in general agreement on certain lines of policy. It does not necessarily man that the PREMIER should be masterful, dictatorial, able to assert his will on all possible occasions. It implies that there shall be co-equal power, constantly consulting, and in direct touch with supporters and the country, un- moved by the cliques, interests, and sections which so often compel an Administration to do indefen- sible acts. The worst of a Government which is the reflection of the acts of one man, or which is left to its own devices because the head is weak and co-ordination is lost, is that it loses touch with the vital spirit of the country and makes con- tinual mistakes. All Governments make mistakes because they do not conduct public business in the way that private business is managed. They want to reward clamourers, and they desire to avoid the financial or political results of their own folly. Tried by the best tests the present Administration is anything but strong, as it conshinee all the tendencies which are debilitating and destructive. Thera is the frequent absence of a controlling head, there is the more or less independence of the Foreign Office because it is under the PREMIER'S control, and consultatien may be avoided until emergencies arise, and there is the tendency in some other departments to go their own way. Government is, or should be, a consultative affair, a matter of free discussion, and not the casual sort of thing it is apt to become when there are Cabinet Ministers who are votes and not voices, and a Premier who prefers, as a rule, to let matters slide. The dominance of foreign affairs for the past four or five years has given to Lord SALISBURY an almost unchecked direction, and the result has been that decisions have lacked force, and domes- tic affairs have been considerably mismanaged. It would have been better for our foreign policy if it had been made the subject of unfettered discussion, and the Minister in charge of it had to state and defend, in open Cabinet, what he proposed to do, with the controlling veto of the PRIME MINISTER at critical times. But what we have seen is a free hand by Lord SALISBURY when matters were fer- menting, and a reference to the Cabinet as a whole when they became critical. In other departments of the State there have been similar eccentricities. Deviations, sudden changes, and grave mistakes have followed. We cannot describe such things as strong government. The lack of touch was dis- played over the original Education Bill, and quite recently over the South African Hospital Corn mittee. Followers were annoyed and startled,aud had to rebel. The dummy supporter, who is a mere item, encourages lax administration, and here again we have an explanation of weak rule. Ministers who know they can command the votes of mere items are tempted to be lax, illogical, and inconsistent. In quiet times these conditions are less important. Of late, when affairs have not been quiet and one crisis has succeeded another, they have tended to become disastrous. In China as in South Africa, we have had examples of what has been anything but strong government. We are reaping the results, and they are not to our liking. The consequence is that the politicians most hopeful as to the renewal of confidence in the Ministry by the country, hope the Ministry itself will be reconstructed, and that duplicating offices will come to an end. They look forward to some closer correspondence between the House of Commons and the Cabinet. They are right. In this country, government by a Yamen will not do at all. Its inevitable result is weakness and de- moralisation.</p>
        <p>IT Will be irepossiblo to Ecclesiastical keep out ecclesiastical politics Politics</p>
        <p>from the General Election, and it may also be undesirable. We cannot make everything turn on foreign and colonial affairs. The country has had a long spell of agitation en these subjects, and that agitation cannot be inde- finitely prolonged. It would be bad for trade,bad for national expenditure, and bad for the temper of the country. The Britisn Empire is based upon peace and commerce, reform and progress. It could not long exist as a fighting empire, straining its resources in the work of conquest, working for the partition of decaying countries, and grabbing at ports, settlements, and special privileges. Attention to home affairs is imperative, and eccles- iastical questions are urgent and vital. It would be better to protect the Protestantism of those islands than to allow Romanising to proceed un- checked because we were bent on partitioning China or making the Empire a Militant Power, full of tumults and ambitions. Two distinct eccles- iastical questions must C 01320 up. The first is the establishment of a Roman Catholic University in Ireland oat of Imperial funds. Half the money spent in Ireland on political agita- tion during the last twenty years would have splendidly endowed a Catholic Uni- versity. Such an institution nisy be wanted in Ireland, and we know it is desired. But the choice would be between a purely Roman Catholic university,with out any restriction and such a university as could he endowed w ith public mono y, in which certain chairs only would be maintained by the State, while arrangements</p>
        <p>would have to be made for non-Catholic students, and for some kind of direct or indirect control over the governing body. The first would meet the wishes of the Catholic Hierarchy; the second would not, and it would, moreover, greatly offencl Protestant electors and taxpayers. Hence we say that the complete is better than the partial solu- tion, and that no State-established Roman Catholic University is desirable nor can be sus- tained by the country at large. The second ques- tion is that of the preservation of the Protestant- ism of the Church of England. Two organised sections in the Church are now at war, and they will marshal their adherents at the polls and endeavour to impose coLditions on Parliamentary candidates. They are well within their rights. third section in the Church, with a large amount of support outside, is in favour of selt-governeient in tke Church and freedom from State control. That is, it prefers Disestablishment and the organisation of the two sections into distinct religious bodies. But it is not highly organised, and some sympath- isers, are Protestants and others Ritualists. The active, legislative-seeking Prostestants believe in the State as the only power able to secure the maintenance of the Faith of the Reformation. Their position has been sapped of late years, and the effort to strengthen the foundation is obvious and general. The Liberationists are also active, ready to take advan- tage of the present conflicts of opinion, and willing to leave forms of faith to the free, disendowed, and surviving bodies. The Liberator, surveying the whole field, remarks in its current issue: "The time is, indeed, eminently favourable for out- spokenness and for firmness, seeing that public, opinion, both in and out of the EAablishment, is in a state of flux; resentment towards sacerdote- lism on the one hand, and impatience of State con- trol on the other, having the effect of preparing the public mind for changes which at one time apreared to be as dangerous as they were remote." Hence we are certain to be involved in the conflict from one side or the other, and it will be well to arrive at clear views before the time of decision arrives. The country cannot afford to lose its hold on Protestantism, or to permit unhindered thein- sidious attempts to undo the work of the Refor- mation under the plausible plea of esthetic or doctrinal restoration.</p>
        <p>Judging from the forecasts in commercial journals the Chinese crisis will not effect our im- ports, though it has already very seriously affected a part of our export trade. Mincing-lane admits that there will be no rise in tea conequent on the disturbances, and if Mincing-lane had the smallest ground for hoping that an increase was possible it would not be so candid. What may be missing from China will be supplied by India and Coloo9 and this in the smoothest possible manner. Tile English consumer will not suffer. There will be e' diminution in the supply of the Chinese palit, straw is also made up in other countries, and the dislocation of business will be temporary. direct sufferers in England from the rising will be the proprietors of the cotton mills, and the opera- tives there. Already some of these works ere cicsed, and a. the time approaches when would have been forthcoming for cargoes now be' back, but in ordinary circumstances due for de' livery at this time, the workers will beWail thef blindness of all the Powers to the beginnlog.°4 this rebellion. The German shipping col:14)4'11e% who have been taking our custom blw,heir Shanghai and Singapore will also lose must °:",ilat, freightage. But the worst prospect of all IS " if China be partitioned between the nations IN!, enterprises may be developed at a rate which WIJI mean the end of our export trade to that couotr,l in a very short time. The Chinese labourer 04" do the work cheaper.</p>
        <p>Tolstoy's experiment in Socialism, which hase,,r cited so much amazement in Russia, has had to"; broken in an important particular. Every9L3, knows that his particular ambition is exactly opposite of that of the average cz.pable man of tcrs day. While others desire to make money, alviale with the respect of their fellows, but still to 0111 D money, his aim has been te free himself from s interest in lucre. He lives in a single room, westro the plainest garments, and reduces his other scal3nd to the smallest possible compass. He annoollew-ci seme time since that he should receive no reir3r,,, for his works, but should give them freely tort, people—that is, subject only to the cost of prlll,l, ing and publication. The persecution of t,"e Doukhobortsce, however, aroused the old dosle for wealth as a means of usefulness. These PO s objected to military service on conscienti°lls grounds, and all that they desired was the inea,l3,, to enablethern to flee the country where conscriYi tion existed. Tolstoy saw in the publication of tit! famous novel, "Resurrection," the means of lOW ing them to find freedom in the British colooleisi; He therefore copy-righted the volume, and "he with the proceeds rendered excellent servicAtotew„r cause. The fact has somewhat distressed bun,lvt, it has proved the value of gold as an instrunl9ni, It has justified, under wise control, the mone!,; making taste—now become through the pract''t of our ancestors an instinct. He has learnt 0;1,1 the evils of 'currency are to be realised not in 'ordinary use, but in its abuse.</p>
        <p>The most important event of the present cric-be season happened on Wednesday,when the hithe% undefeated Sussex eleven sustained a reverse at tt.:t hands of Yorkshire. With only two wins to credit Sussex defied defeat, and, by virtue of iblig much abused method of calculating the positl_,°o of counties in the championship table remainea 0 all practical purposes on a par with the tv/of shires of the rose, who had each won two-third,° their matches. The northern tour of Frlll,-6 Ranjitsinhji's men promised that defeat which necessary to put them in their proper Against LaLicashire, the water-God came to, 0 aid of the Sussex men and rendered another orro inevitable; but against the equally forlsihill Tykes they were not 60 favoured, and had to accefir an innings defeat. All hopes of ultimate 11011°130 now lie between Lancashire and Yorkshire, are on level terms and who try conclusionB---4'eo mite conclusif-ms we hope—at Manchester week. 'Twill indeed be Greek versus Greek, rA, the combat should be an exciting one between ;vie so well matched and such keen rivals. The fai!lik.io of Sussex and the decline of Surrey prove that Pty only by the aid of first-class bowling that a 00t113,0 may keep at the top of the tree. Good batth* essential, of course, but the bowling is more o°'</p>
        <p>the There is some hope that the condition 0- 450 Ship Canal may, after all, be improved in o,7tet, of time. The Mersey and Ifrwell Joint are determined to make Manchester, the prineWer polluting authority, move in the matter or Oiler penalties which will not be at all agreeable eiter for the Corporation or the ratepayers. Mtilledge ter has been shilly-shallying with se" i# schemes since 1888 and the probleniote nearly as far from solution yet, th 000 two schemes are under consideration. oil Corporation is willing to adopt, but the ote Government Board will not sanction it, e„,c3 sob other which fulfils the requirements of the is rejected by the Corporation. A trial of strunbe an patience will fellow, we suppose, wit:vor ultimate triumph of the Board,which has 5'13-34ese• ful ally in the Mersey and Irwell Joint Conotxsl Too Committee is persistent in its endeavucl stir the fethat gic Mancunians into action' stlJ year ago fined them £5O. Result, te„oe' intents and purposes, nil. Another cution has just terminated with t,heoc, of fiction of the more substantial pc'n' £:250 and costs, and the promise of "fl</p>
        <p>L'o-re to We ober. of dv0069,4%00 1116 60f</p>
        <p>low" if nothing is decided in Septel have rather more faith in the persnai this last penalty than in anything thl before, and Manchester's eupabity for</p>
        <p>' r; ItA4 ; 1</p>
