      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>logic of it need not be underrated. In the mean- time, Warrington, Runcorn, and other authorities along the great waterway will get the undesired benefit of some 25,000,000 gallons of sewage, re- lieved only of its solids. No love will be lost be- tween Manchester and these other authorities if nothing is done by the end of September.</p>
        <p>It would appear from the proceeiings before the Committee of the House of Lords that the Lon- don 2..,nd North-Western Railway Company are more concerned about the projected light railway schemes between Warrington and Northwich and Runcorn than the Corporation's Tramway Scheme. The light railway was the dark horse of which they were afraid and they saw clearly that if the Corporation Tramway Scheme were approved they would have no farther oppor- tunity of opposing the light railway proposals. They therefore took occasion to make the most of their opportunity and have fared very badly. It is quite true that the tramway scheme as it at pre- sent stands is confined to the borough but we see no reason why this should always be so. The circular route between Stockton Heath and Latch- ford which was proposed by the promoters of the ill-starred Warrington and Nnthwich Light Rail- was an excellent idea, and one which the Cor- poration might do well to consider. The difficulty of crossing the swing bridge over the Ship Canal ought not to be insuperable, and it would be far more preferable for the Corporation to have the scheme in hand than a number of speculators from outside the borough.</p>
        <p>By the small majority of twelve votes to ten the Warrington Town Council has sanctioned the expenditure of £6,62.7 4s. ltd. on improve- ments in the existing baths and the erection of Turkish baths. With the first portion of the scheme we have no fault to find what- ever; improvements and extensions at the batns being an absolute and pressing necessity. But we are no more in favour now of the outlay of some £3,000 on building Turkish baths than we were when plans were before the Council in February last. Turkish baths are not needed in Warrington; they are a good deal in the nature of a luxury at any time; and moreover the town is not in the position to spend £3,000 in this direction. If there were no huge schemes like electric lighting, tramways, and water already on hand then there would be some excuse for investing money in Turkish baths. These schemes are very much on hand however, and in addition Councillor A. J. Jolley warns the Council of further I•eavy expendi- ture likely to he incurred shortly, so that it is perfectly clear t us and to probably the large majority of ratepayers that the spending of this £3OOO is a piece of unwarrantable extravagance. Even if the scheme has been on the carpet for ten years that is no reason why it should be hurried forward now. Tne present time is no more con- venient than any period since the matter was first mentioned, and it was the duty of the Council to wait—another ten years if need be—till there was money in hand and more of an equality between work accomplished and that to be done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>The Prince of W ales, either alone or accompanied by the Princess and their eldest daughter, has done much in differ.nt parts of the provinces during the Past three weeks or so to d,serve the distinc:ion he long since earned 01 being the hardest worked Public man in England. Everywhere he and the Princess have been congratulated on their recent miraculous escape from assassination. But this week the public have appeared mystified in reading that Sipi o, the w.,uld be assassin, had been ac- quitted after trial, and was living with his family in Pane. The facts were all admitted at the trial, the attempted murder not being denied ' • but he Was fouad by the court too young to b 3 held legally responsible for his sell' in. Anyhow, it valoutit id ordered to be to this, since he waq simply tent to a reformatory until 21 years of age, but was „4,ll°lved to escape. Of course, those who have been 4',_ultiiring the Princess end cangratulalng her and 'tie Prince in the provincial towns they have been visiting, are in high dudgeon over the matter; more so, probably, thhn the Prince The latter asked at the time that the prisoner should be dealt with as lightly as p.3ssible, but the public none the less feel that such leniency as this is a direct incentive to this kind of crime.</p>
        <p>_ It is at lagt beginning to be recognised that the name " Workhouse Infirmary" is angularly and offensively inappropriate as applied to institutions where workpeople overtaken by sudden illness or Some bodily ailment are occasionally treated. Mr. T. W. Rugseil, M P., Parliamentary ecretary to the Local Government B )ard, has stated his opinion that some change in this respect is desirable, and in the course of an address the other day at the cere- Mony in connection with the opening of new wards at the Devonport Workhou.e Infirmary, the Rev. C. J. Palmer expressed a hope that some mire euphonsous and less repellent name might be adop- ted. He at first suggested the words "Local Government Hospital, but if it was desired to retain the name of the town, it could in thi instance be "The Devonport Infirmary for sick and aged poor." Other autnorities might very properly take the hint. . .</p>
        <p>It muPt be a very great consolation to a very de- Serving an I important body of men, whose lot is proverbially not always a happy one, to know how much more their bodily comfort and cnnyenience is Considered by every self-respecting Wateh Commit. tee, or other police authority, than was formerly the case. In every well regulated community nowa' days, tbe cons'abulary are in these tot July and "dog" days, provide 1 with a light waterproof to Utilise asoccasion le3quiree. The concession is very gratifying to members of "the forca." It is net long since members of nearly every county constabulary had to wear the same clothes in eumnpr as in win- ter. The old Court of Qearter s'essions, backed up by equally old fashioned chief constables of the day would not sanction any deviation from the rule of thick heavy eloth to that of light serge. But now there has been a general ening for the better. Promises of subscription to the Wesleyan million guineas fund amount to something like three- quarters of a million, and the amount actually paid to more than £3OO 000. There is still some leeway to make up be ere the full sum of a million guineas is realised, and the task towards the end will no doubt. prove heavier than that at the beginning, The United Methedist Free CrAirches which ha‘e been boding tneir annul assembly in Manchester this week, appear to have sunceeded in about the same proportion as the larger bedv heving promises of some eighty th -susend out of 100,000 guiness. in ore -r two of the smeller towns a note of die- COUrfigement has been sounded anent the proposal to celebrate in a spirit of joyfulness the expeefed announcement of PRAM "Premature p-eparations" are deprecated end it is publicly d dared that with the trouble still exi4tent in West Africa and in China the teked-of rejoicinge should be con- sidered nut of the qeestion. It is, perhaps, need- less to all that the preparations are going on all the same</p>
        <p>At the commencement of the month, having re- gard to the revenue returns tor the first quarter, it was asked if our tide of good fortune was reced- ing? The question wag euggeered by the shrinkage of no less than £1..203,144, at first sight, regarded as serious. At that rate the deli it would be enormous by the end of t'se veer. But the decline could be easily explained, and although had for the revenue, did not betoken any interruption in the trade of the country, and now, taking the exports for the Rix months as shown in the Board of Trade returns, it is found that they actually amount to more than 311 milli ms more than the, exports for the corms- Donding period of 1893. Si huge an advance is Surely noteworthy in our commercial history. Feeling in the provinces on the all-ab4orbing topic of the day is that after the Tuner:meg of het week, the later news from China. or Puzzledom, must be regarded as more belief&amp; to say the least. How the Dowager-''inpreee came to be e'eeesA, to be repor- ed as hevine Sell to her summer palace, to have been assassinated, and to have taken the eeie-seu URI with tii,appointed Orieetiel in high Places of committing enieide, and then suddenly l'*•-appearing on her thron- again, like en ordinary j'eteenehe-hee er the elewn in tee, mritemitne, Vitb his cheery" Here we are again !" is no longer rneptery or a remneee when it has nfl been ex- Plaine&amp; But there are very few of even the " knowing °nee" in this instance who tem hace had .214 RA? i.fAc.iou of saying, "jest as 1 had eurmised.' " ways that are devieue " the Heathen Chinee VII maintains the" unique "position assigned him "v N{ark Twain. After explanations tendered in the House of Commons during the week, it can no</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>'longer be fairly said that responsibility rests with the British Government for the delay in military arrangements that has occurred, any more than upon that of " Jap, the Giant-killer." It is curious to observe and speculete upon how the turn of events in the Far East may yet affect American policy both there and in other parts of the world. betever may have been the attitude of the American Admiral when the Taku forts were bombarded, American marines hive taken part in subsequent operations, United States troops are le,.ing called at the present mo- ment from the Poillipines to join with those of the other Powers, and if there should be any division of spoil hereafter, it will be too much to expect the Cabinet at Washington to stand idly by while other Governments are obtaining valuab'e concessiens. But., in that event, what would become of the boasted, venerated, and very-excellent-in-itself Monroe doctrine? Really he mystery deepens. The Grwers' Federation has been in conference at Bristol during the week.and nearly five hundred ' delegates have attended It was the tenth annual gathering of the kind, and, from the trade point of view, appears to have iven much satisfaction, There has been a remarkable development of the grocery business in recent years. and village shop- keepers of half a century ego would be astonished if they could see the variety of articles which now have a place in the price lists of large firms. As in many other kinds of business, the tendency of the age in regard to grocery trading is to elbow out of existence the small shopkeeper. The lattec ham to compete with the " stores" and the bigger shops, and generally manages to eke out a living to the annoyance of his more important neighbour, and the keeping down of p ices, of which the public in the end have the advantage. Far good or for illiand often much to the injury of and unfairness towards other traders, the stock of a modern grocery estab• lishment includes not only tea, coffee, agar and other articles of domestic consumption, but goods of almost every kind, to notepaper and envelopes. and thongh the public patronise them, they do not approve the "Noah's Ark " mPthleg.</p>
        <p>ST. MICHAEL'S ClitJaca,</p>
