      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SAD END OF A CRIMEAN VETERAN. Accidentally Drowned in the River</head>
        <p>SAD END OF A CRIMEAN VETERAN. Accidentally Drowned in the River</p>
        <p>Weaver.</p>
        <p>Coroner J.C. Bate held an iequest at the Bridge Hotel, Frodsham, on Weinesday afte,neon, on the b)cly of one-legged man weich w-is taken out of the River Weaver on Menday last, by the lock tender. Thomas Wuittaker, of the Royal Elotel, Widnesemore he ceuld identify the body as that of George, Meson, an old army veteran, aged about 77 years, and whom he believed had pirticip,t-d in toe oatdes of Aline anti Inkermann. The deceased was origin4lly a native of Eseter, but of late his abode had been unsettled, He was on the pension hat, and drew about is 10 i a day • Witness had known the deceased ale nit 30 years, and knew that when he worked it was as a stene breaker. On Friday, the 6th inse deceased had called upon him, and they had a conversation, in which deceased said he was" hard up" and tied been obliged to pawn his medals—one of which witness knew to be a three-barred one—and witeess bad given him some meney with which to find lolgings. Deceased had then left saying te would havoa look round the town, call on witness aeaintand proceed to Liverpool. Witness did not know whether deceased conk; swim, but had never heard bine say so.—Recalled, this witness said he could identify the brush produced as one belonging to deceased and one he generally carried with him. Edward Bowden, lock tender. at Froisham bridge swore he met the deceesed. of whom he had no previous knowledge, a few yards off the turnbridge on Monday morning, at abeul 20 minutes to one o'clock. Witnemi went home, but whiter at his dinner his attention was called to the man's sudden disappearance. He subse. quently went down to the canal side, and found deceased's clothes, but no oign of him. With the help of his assistant he recovered the body, which was quite naked, with a boat hook. The only sign of life %Nee that the deceased's tongue fete warm. Re sent for the p.lice, and .commenced artificial respiration. This the p,dice continued upon their arrival but deceared never gave any signs of returning viteli. y. He would think the deceased was in the watelab .ut re) minutes before he was recovered, —Questioned as to his theery as to how the deceased miehe have got into the water, witness said: When he first disherbed the body the deresh (produced'? came up to the surfece. h his he thought had been retained in the deceased's hand. His jacket, cap, and other clothing, as well as the erut hee was some &amp;stance off the canal side ; but the decease-I'Q trouser; were quite close to the wall, and he weell give it as his opinion that the deceased had been endeavoer• ing to clean himself with the brush, overbalanced, and se fallen in and drowned.</p>
        <p>Sarah Lovett, residing near to the Frodshana locks, a sister of the last witness testifi-d to seeing the MAN going to the canal side, and li:idenly dicappssr told her sister-in-!aw abott it, and she told the hat witniss.</p>
        <p>rhe Coroner, in summing up, Feld it was not very likely that the defeased cauremploted nnil fcr it %vac not generally found th4t a p 'mon so disp.s-d Avould strip himself, or ners?lf, for the purplsp, which could he equally well ace:)ropl,sled with clothing on. Ile advised the return of a vvrdiet of" found drowned," as they had merely conjecture to go upon. Th jorv, however, returned is erdict of "accidentally drowned."'</p>
