      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EXAMINER,. Fill DAY, JULY 13, 1900. RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,. Fill DAY, JULY 13, 1900. RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>BREWSTER SESSIONS AT RUNCORN. The jusdr...es ot tho Runcorn Petty Sessional Divi- sion have fired the annual licßnsinr4 day for tile 27th August, and the date for verifying jury lists the 24th September. SUCCKSS OF A WIDNES MUSICIAN. At the examination in musical knowtedge held at Trinity College, London, Miss Cissie .Nlather, nieco of Mrs. Martin, Widnui, passed most successfully with honours. The highest possible number of marks to he gained was 100, and MiS3 Mather obtuned 91.</p>
        <p>CHOIR SERMONS AT ST, MICEA.ET'S. We TTOui.l call our readers' attention to the fact that the annual choir sermons will be preached at I ISt. Michael's Church, Runcorn, next Sunday. In the morning the preacher will be the Rev G D Wray, MA, and in the evening the Rev N J Poole, B.A. A flower service will be held in the afternoon at which a ear non will be delivered by the Rev E S Oliver, B.A.—See Advt. MARRIAGE OF MR, EDWARD EIERBERT BNIVETON. Halton Trinity Wes' .yan Chapel was the seene, on Thiareday morning last, of tile solemnization of the nuptials of Mr. Edward Herbert. K.iiveton, younge*st, son of the late Mr. Benjamin Kai vecon, to Miss Emily Nancollis., daughter of Mr. J. Nancellis, Old (lefty, Runcorn. The officiating minister was the Rev Humphrey Morrison. Miss Polly Knivetom was bridesmaid, and Mr. George Hayes, acted as groomsman. The ceremony was quietly observed, and after its completion the happy couple left Run- corn Station for the Isle of Men where the hone 1/4- moon will be spent.—Numerous hands me and use- ful presents were received, am ingst which was a beautiful combination dessert service the gift of Mr Kniveton's fellow-workers at Messrs. Simpson, Davies, and Campanv'e. DEATH FROM BURNS AT WIDNES. An inquest was held by Mr S Brighouee, at the Accident Hospital, last Wednesday, on the three ' year old daughter of Andrew Shannon, 32, M (xis- street, Widnes The evidence showed that at 10 15 on Sunday night the father and mother left the child in the house getting her supper, whilst they went to the back or. The other chilersn bad beer put to bed, and one of them, bearing screams, ran downstairs, and found the child in flames. The father hurried back to the house and extingeished tbe flames, but not before the poor girl had received sufficient inju-ies to cause her death. A verdict 01 "Accidental death" was returned. SUDDEN DELT!! OF AN INFANT. On Wednt sday night Mr. R. !Mem, coroner, held an inquest at Runcorn Police Court, on toe body of the infant son of Frederick White, labourer, 14, Loch-street. Evidence was given by the mother and a neig..bour that the deceased who was three months old had been healthy from birth. About five o'clock on Tuesday morning the mother awoke to find the child in a fit and he died in a few minutes. rho child was insured but was not in benefit.—The jury returned a verdict of "natural causes probably a convulsive fit." A CAPTAIN AND HIS MATE.—WAGES DISPUTE</p>
        <p>James Corfield, mate of the "Premier" schooner, lying at Runcorn Docks, sued John Waldroe. can tam n of the same vessel,forthe recovery of ..e2 174 61 wages due to him.—The case was defended by Mr. Preston, for Mr. J, Burton, solicitor, Runcorn.—The defence was that the eemplainant had been paid off, and considerable evidence was celel to prove €43. Also, one witness for the defence (Michael Tirrole) said he met the complainant under the influ4nce of drink, and he was blasting that tie had go'. his " wages" but as" he had elven no receipt for it, he would claim it again."—This was denied by the complainant, but as he produced no evidence beyond his own word, the Case was dismissed. ALBERT-ROAD METHODIST FREE CHURCH, WIDNES. The anniversary of this S indiy school attached to the above place of worship was celebrate on Sunday, when large congregations assembled at the morning and eveniag services to hear the Rev T Campbell, of sierra Leone (a coloured stulent of the Free Church °Alive. Manchester), preach. In the afternoon a children's meeting was held. Mr fr II Pankhurst ocmp'e 1 the chair. The Rev T T Campbell delivered an inter.,sting address on mis- sion work and life on the West Coast of Africa. The musical portion of the service was excellently carried out by he children and choir. The hemna by the former "Come to the Saviour," " Heavenly " Girdi-d. Armour," " Kin's Brigade," " Hark the Trumpet," &amp;e. The choir reildered several anthems, their finest effort being "Ae the hart pants." The collections were very satisfactory._</p>
        <p>SMART AMBULANCE WORK, On Sunday eveninrs as the mernb,rs of the Irish National League, Kirkdale, were re!uming from Hale a man's hat wae blown off. A man whose name is unknown jumped off the waggonette to pick it up Another conveyance, in which were seated about 30 per,ons. wkich was foil ming, passed over the unfortunate man. His thigh was dislocated and severely cut, whilst he also snatained a compound fracture At the left leg. P.C. Clarke, of Hale, ban- daged the injuries and sent for Dr. Hutchinson. When the Doctor arrived h announced that the man's wounds had been so skilfully attendt,d to by the officer that the only thing he could do was to order his removal to the "Royal Infirmary. He was subsequently taken to this institution by P C Clarke</p>
        <p>"DRINK AT THE BOTTOM OF EVERYTHING." George Ferreet, Parson ige-road, Widnes, was on Monde,/ charged before the Mayor and Alderman J W Waring rn remand with stealing a watch end chain, value rl, the property of Giffen Young, at gale on the 5-h inst.,—Prisoner pleaded guilty.—llannah Young wife of the prose- cutor, said that on Thursday, the sth inst.' the prisoner Came to their house in company with another young man. After they had had some eheese, cake and cherries they asked for some flowers. Witnese went into the garden to get them, and when she returned she found the young men were just going She afterwar is mistel the watch and chain, and being unable to find it, gave infor- mation to the police —The prisoner said he had been working at the sit ter and c,pner wo-ke,but was :stopped on Tuesday ninht; He had had some drink. —Alderman Waring: It is at. the bettom of Avery. ' thing,—Prisoner was boiled over in the 4um of .£lO for three mlnthe. and was ordered to nen the costs SUICIDE OF A WIDNES WOMAN. On Wednesdey evening. at Mines, Mr. S Brig- house, county coroner, held an adjourned inquest on the body of a single woman named Sarah Shaw, aged 25 years. 13, Robert - vreet Widnee, who died last Fridey under some- what mysterious cireumetaneee, after taking a precipitate powder Inspector Strinkl ind repered that he had made int:pH-es in Ma'c'-e'er. where the deceased was emp'oy d as a domestic anvant and he found that she had h wise a grand charac- ter and was a gOVI girl.—Dr. Scott, who had maths a post-mortem exim'netion in (inn- junction with Dr Wbennan, said they wen both of opinion that de tth was 'he result of an irritant pnieon, the etomieh and inteetinee being acutely inflamed. The deceaeed had told her mother that ' she had taken a preeinitate p mder —Alice Shaw said her sister had stifbred frem indigestion, and had been greatly depresnsd. —The jury brought in a verdict of suicide whilst of unsound mind. FA73EWELL TO THE REV W FID01?, RIINCORE The Greenwey road Rchoelroom was the scene or a very intereeting function on W-elneeday after- noon when about 90 of the society members and friendq entertained their late motor the Rev. W. Fidoe and &amp;ire. Fidoe, to tea. The occasion was ' brought ',bout by the K v W. Fidoe being called to Lexey, Isle of Man, and was to provide an onnor- trinity in which to bid the rev gentlemen " God speed." After the tea, Mrs. Thoroesen, one of the eldest members of the society, male a presentation of a silver plated cruet. Gladstone beg and A hand- bag, to '.r. and %Ira Fidoe, on behalf of the Church and in deing so referred to the valuable ministry Mr Fdne had conducted during his stay of four ywirs amongst them In that time he had done much for the cause, and gained the respect and e;cod will of the whole of the members- litosqr, haves, R Peacock nd C Full, else testified to Mr F doe's worth, and to the etrainhtforwerd manner in which he had conducted everything con- nected with the cause—The Rev W Fidoe returning thrnks on hie own and wife's behalf, expressed the pleasure the work had given him and at the good feeling which was now being shown to him tie im- plying satisfaction with hie pa,t se vices. Th..y were very useful and appropriate presents and he Ilwould always value and use them.—On Thursday morning the rev. gentleman and his wife and Ifamily left Runcorn station for Laxey. secomnanied by the well wishes of a !erne gathering of his late congregation; and in some instances the parting was very affectinnareelhowing how truly sympathe- tic minister and laymen had been in their daily in- tercourse. LATEST DESTGITS and emianrinßs in Prints. Muslin' and &amp;Thera, for samoser. also a choice assortment of Sunshades just reftived.—iv. T. WiLttaxs, 74, Sankey street and 1, Legtestreet, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES. --- [BY A CORRHSPONDENT].</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. --- [BY A CORRHSPONDENT].</p>
        <p>One wonders whether the " Runeorn Ratepayers' Association" will accept the bait which is being held out to them by a contemporary, which practically invites them to a corre4pond- ence upia the Park Quesition. Possibly, it may come about that one or two ot the ardent "spirits of discora" will again prefer a wordy debate, equivalent in effect to" penny trumpettn4 against thunder,' upon the question of "Park or no Park r —or " Park versus Bread ?'— They are always interesting subjects to people desirous of writing matter derogatory to a much wilhi-d for consummation. But,even if they are so disposed, and the writing is dune, one fails to see how anything is to be gained by it. It has already been clearly shown in this column that the RAte- payars'Association miserably failed in i t object—so far as the park question was concerned—and that, was the avowed object that called it into exittence.</p>
        <p>Furthermore, when one Mlles to make counpari- snns, a mare microscopic examination often dis- clo-es " the motif." It is, for instance, not generally known that Public Baths for Runcorn, are not so remote as people would think. Lit me whisper to those people who are forwarding the Park Scheme, and xvno are doing their best, (in what appears to them the best way), that they are being heartily supported, and a large number of their supporters kirnw they contemplate providing baths along with the Park—if possible! low weak: it suit, if those who are willing could be got to clntribute to a fund for the erection of baths, mil allow the Park to come out of the public purse? Thus, in a few years, toe whole scheme could be completed, and as for the rate !—well it would be oaid, with just as mach, or as little grumbling an at present.</p>
        <p>It was Charleq liingdey who first told the st)ry of "rhe Three Fishers" ; but perhaps the present writer could commence a story the same way, an if not as pathetic a tale it may prove amusing. "Three fishers went out as Oe sun want down" but heir destination was not the open see. Oh no! A "big pool" was goo I enough for them. Here they "plied rod and line" with a z ;al worthy of a better cause. They were young enthusiasts, who a few days ago were desirous of being proposed as mem- bers of the Runcorn A.A. Alas, for that enthusiasm the "big pool" proved too much of a damper, and I now the would be fishers have some tackle tor did- ciaal chAap, and de,ire no more to fraternise with I the Runcorn section of the "gentle Anglera."</p>
        <p>That the dog days have areived we have hai ample evidence this week, The heat his been scorching an the discomfort in hi attire which Englishmen are c ,m- -pelted to wear owing to -he variations of the climate bs. been extremele great. Blt there is also danger in this hot weather. Our elereetic medical officir of h-alth, Dr ltd iDougall and the Urnan Distr ot C3uncii, have ot late been frequently e ,ngratulving thernselv.s, and the public, upon the fac that tho town 97a3 alm est entirely Ire° from infecious disease. If such weather that we have experienced the week co itinues I am atraid thew conzratulations will have ti give place to commisera- tions ; unless the Health Corntnit,.ee bestir ttleM3ol7,i to sorno purpose. I have this weak passed d own streets, in which there was 3 vathii:37 of stinks, that //mild have astonished even a Turk. wising from b t conditions of eanitation, to whine I have more than once referred to within the put few weeks. When a ease of inFectious disease 'is reported, there is quickly at the house where the poor patent languishes, a min carrying a bucket of chloride of lime ;and thi;, which t, a great extent is a mere deodoriser, ha dumps up and down the yard and beck-passage where ho thinks a smell is likely to arise. Tnis sav:iurs very much ot locking the stable door after the steed has been stele(' ; It is chasing away stinks tnat have already elaimrel victim In fact the disinfecting of the t:Avn is carried on in the same "patchwork principle" as the Council carry on other sanitary matters. If i fectious disease is to be prevented, as it can be pre- vented, the 0 ouncil must instruct their officers to systematically disinfect the yards and back premises ii the town week by week, and see that the work properly and thoroughly done, and the inhabitants would be well recouped for the extra expenditur,,, b, the gain in health.</p>
        <p>Ree-miking in the Urban District Counttil of Rao- corn is evidently at a disc .unt. True thi town is a hilly on, and its roads and streets pregent more diffieuLy than do other porti Ins of the county ; but surely this should be no re son for the tyro-like methods employgd. Tile rate. put•chatie as good material As that used anywhere. an th,) r,Admete are in polsession of a good steam rolleriatiu other appliances for their work, but the finished road or street—A9 they leave it—surely discloses a want of even the ordinary rudiments of the art. For there i art req tired in r) id m sking, laying the macadam upon tip etrth's surface, covering it with soil and gravel and rolling it with the szeurt • roller aftr.r it isas been watered to the consistency of a mud pud 'l. is surely not ro idmaking, and the result of en ..11 a pro case cannot in the nature of things produce anythin satisfactory, and yet this is what 13uncorn's road- makers expect, and even sometimes flatter them selves upon obtaining. Now, I use those road, daily, and also spend a considerable portion of my time upon ot'ller rods. just outside the confines of the ni— trict, and as the latter traescend, the former to a yer) high degreN I would respectfully advise those respon sible to go and see bow they are made and take a lea' out of the book."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>eltIC K ItT</p>
        <p>Warrinzton could hardly hive been expnte I to win in Saurday, wnen they had tor thLir opponents, Man- chester who him ught dawn an exceptionally powerlu zeam. The visiting eleven included nine profossionals, amongst whom were fillnld and Paul, of Lanca- stare tame. The result of the match was that Manche.iter ran out *,ianers to the extent of 103 runs te 61.</p>
        <p>Warringtm captain, winning Uri toss, batte first on a wicket affctecl by the rain, Thewlis and B S+lcock teing opposed by h1)14 and Lord. Runs we, e hoird to obtain, and with only five runs on the Belt., sheet Mold to.at Silcock with 'a break back. J Cellon cA me next and at once forced the game, a lucky snick off M•ild going dangerously near Broughton in tho slips. fnewlis drove Lord splendidly for four, but the ho NiOC tiad his reverie° a minute later, a fino c itch by P,iu! at point rending Thewlis back.</p>
        <p>Dr. Smith elm? mcxt and oponed hi inning by plac- ing \lntl fpr fur, but soon afterwards lothe company of Call)n, who was bowled oy a grand bill from Mold. E 4,1 Connor soon placd five to hie credit, when Lord 4ent him back, the score being 28 for t3O 1045 of four wick,?ta. Lunch was now taken and on resuming ther, would bo considorably over a thousand preserfz• Clarke came out with Dr. Snih to continue 1119 War- rington innings and the original pair of b)wlers again caueed tho bstsmen trouble,</p>
        <p>Ten run; were added. when Clarke was caught at the wicket. Dr. Smith continued to play a fine lefensiv- game, hut Briggs drove one into the hands of M )ld at mid-off. Mold met with nucces now, bowling J W Bibby and WI a PodrnorP at 51 and 57 rrspectivel7, and D. Smith retired at 60, having played faultless cric.ke.t for his 16. The innings chsed for 61. Manchester commerwd their innings with Pail and A F Lyon, Connor and Thewlis being the bowlers. Only tour runs were scored when Paul was taken filely at the wicket, whilst Broughton who after escaping heina tnaelPd ha Thewlis, also touched one of Connor's expresses into the hands of Rawson.</p>
        <p>Thoaiss joined his captein, and the amateur did near's/ all the ecorin,g, nlacing oab bowler for four until at 29 he tried to get ThAwiis round tw leg., missed, and roti,ed for an invsluable 20. Wright cm e next, and at 35 D. Smith relieved CJlrior. The change soon worked, for the n w bowler ** york-d " Wright. Thomas con- ..inued his st-adv career end in quick Auccesiion saw the dismissal of R K Dsin, flaw aid Lrd, whilr. Mold was also howlnd by J W Bibby when the Warrington c.c.me had te.en tied.</p>
        <p>Lees Radcliffe joined ThoIIINS and runs came freely, the new comer hitting Rinby for two fours in succe.sinn G Ciarke was tried or two o*erci, and Connor again resumed, but the century appeared, and W El Po imore was elven the ball. Thomas was speedily bowled and Radcliffe stumped, the innings closing tor 103. War- ,ingtou went in a second time and lo ail three wickets for 25 runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2ad gained a brilliant victory over Oughtriagtort Park, the totals nein!? 103 and 32 runs r.apictively. For the vrionPrq Dackwortn knocked up 28, Clot-bpi:on contzibuting 17.</p>
        <p>sioleF Wire Mills eneaged Grapponhatl St. viiilfred`,3 thd succeeded in defeating them by a margin of 24 ruoq. J. Evans and W4tBol. handled the bait well tor their r,spective teams, the fortnor taking six wizket,s for livo runs and the latter nine w;ckets tor 16 runs.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Ll:3es Englneeis IRt easilv diipneed of Ryhd Bros., running out vainoerfa by 27 rang. tVi aud Genders batted woll,sporiou 16 and 14 respectively.</p>
        <p>When asking for Cocoa, insist on having CLDBuurs so,d only in Packeto and Tins as other Goaoss are often substi- tutoi for the sake of extra prolit,—Advt (I)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>DAY TRIP TO MORECAMBE.</head>
        <p>A DAY TRIP TO MORECAMBE.</p>
        <p>With a Glimpse of Lancaster and Heysham. [Ey " QUILP 'N."]</p>
        <p>It was at a quarter to six on Saturday morning when a thousand happy looking mortals,on pleasure bent, turenged the platform at Bank Qaay Station. I was one of this multitude of holiday makers, having prevailed upon myself to leave the mammal couch at tais early hour, in order that I might sp lid a day at Morecambe, that beautiful northern watering place which has so rightly been called " The Naples of the North." I was pleased to receive the elitorial instructio3s to accompany Messrs. Walker's excursion bat I had serious doubts as to whether I should be able to arouse myself from the elualbers of, well, the moderately just at this unusual hour. However, on Saturday morning by some instinct I awoke at a quarter to five. My first impulse was to settle down for a quite snobze until about 730, as is my won't, but I suddenly re- membered the trip, and also the fact that I should have to be at the station by ten minutes to six at the latest as that was the time arranged for start- ing. With a great effort I vacated my be,d, awl at once rushed to the window to form some ides of the weather prospects for the day. On Friday the climatic conditions had been anything but promis- ing, and I should not have been at all surprised to sot) a steady downpour of rain, but no ! the sun was begenning to shod forth his rays and the clouds were dispersing, so that I began to think that after all the cierk ot the weather might favour us with a tine day. One does not take long to dress on such occasions as these, and before half past five I was on my way to the station. Warrington was yet sleeping bat I overtook several others who were going in the same direc- tion as myself. When I arrived at the s[ntion the platform was filled with a surging crowd. Every- one was in the best of humours and there was much speculation as to the weatner. It seemed to ha the general opinion that the present conditions could not continue for long. At a quarter to six the first train steamed into the station, bearing a card which denoteu that it was to convey Walker's brewery excursion to Morecambe. There was a rush for the carriages, but I, knowing that there would be plenty ot 20)III in the train to follow, did not exert myself to obtain a seat. Promptly at ten minutes to Ax the train departed on its way to the seaside, and a moment later the second steamed in. Without much difficulty I managed to secure a cmfortable seat in a fir,t class compartment and at six *lock was speeding on my way to More- cambe.</p>
        <p>Toe fi:'st place of importance through which we ~)ased V 7133 IVigan and the town appeared to be just awakening. Some of the inhabitants had al- ready commenced their day's work at the collieries, and the smoke was curling upwards from the chimneys of dwelling houses. At 6.40, Preston was reached, A snort stop was made here but the train was soon again on its way. The country after leaving Preston as-umes a more interesting charac- ter and the journey was not at all monotonous. Poe train steamed into Lancaster Station just a. the Parish Co arch clock cnimed half-past seven. A glimpse of the ancient castle could be obtained from the carriages. I no:Aced that part of the country from here to Morecambe was dotted with white tents and ascertained that it was the artillery camp at Bars.</p>
        <p>It was not yet eight o'clock when we reached Nloreeambe after a pleasant journey. Tee weather had decidedly improveu. Old Sol wore his most genial smile and there was an almost cloudless skV• Like most of the other trippers I at once made my way to the Promenade and I was favourably im- pressed with the view that met my gaze. The 81.1 n was shining on the placid waters of the magnificent hay and the azure sky was reflected in the sea. In the distance could be seen the cloud capped moun- tains of Cumberland and Westaiorelaud and the lovely hills and dells skirted the bay. I strolled along the handsome Pro eienade, which by the way is nearly three miles long, and after viewing some of the fine hotels and places of amusement that have beau erected it was hard for me to believe that eisty years ago there were only 18 hoases and 01 residents in this now prosperous watering place. rwo splen lid piers adorn the east and west ends of the town and I noticed that a new Tower is in course of construction at the east end. As I walke along I was accosted by bronatd old salts who in- vited me to go out fishing. I am not an eethusiavic follower of lz ate Walton and I did not at the time feel inc:ined for a sail on the briny. 1 at length decided to pay a visit to the histories] town of Lancaster, and accordingly made my way t, a tram which conveyed visitors to that place; and enj lyed the four miles' ride which lay through I beautilul country. Nature was fresh after the recent rains, and all the farmers seemed to he engaged in" making hey while thd sun shines." Oa arriving at Lancaster 1 at once wended my way to the ancient castle, and on approaching t' Giunt's embattled pile" I was impressed with the seleren grandeur of the fortress, which wasenany hundNds of years ago, used as a palace, bat in the reign ot Qis •ri Eliz ibeth, on the threatened invasion of toe Spanish Armada, great alterations ve!•re made and the fortress was much strengthened. Oa the break- ing out of the wars of the Commonwealth. Crom well, at the head of Parliamentary forces, success fully attacked th 9 Castle. Racing his guns on para- pets which are still to be seen on a hill near Rip- ley's Hospital, about 800 yards distant from the Castle, he was able to breach the curtain wall cen- neeting the Dungeon and Gateway Towers,and thus effect an entrance. Henceforth the castle became the county goal. I was gszing in awe upon this famous structure when I heard the notes of a bugle. On hurrying round to the east gate of the Castle I saw a hand- some carriage drawn by four splendidly groomed horses with gorgeous trappings. On either side of it were halhordiers and in front marched two heralds, blowing a fanfare Toe carriage stopped end the High Sheriff with hie attendants stepped out and entered the Castle. This eras the first day f the kssizds and the opening was being carried out with pomp and ceremony. In a few minutes the High Sheriff returned and the carriage pro- ceeded to the Judge's lodgings. I was told that his lordship would attend service in the Parish Church which is adjacent. to the Castle and I proceeded ! thither. Before the arrival of the Judge I bad time to inspect the beauties of the sacred edifice. What particularly attracted my attention were the elabor- ately carved stalls and the tabernacle work round the lter. Me beautiful tracery and carved work also excited my admiration as did the windows of richly stained glass. Suddenly the organ pealed forth the strains of " God save the Qaisen " the congregation rose, arid the proceeeion, headed by the clergy entered the church. The judge in his scarlet robes of effice was t e cynosure of all eyes and his appearance was mlst imuesinee His Lordsbip.the High sheriff and attendants were accomm elated with seats in the chancel, and the service esnamenced. It was a simple repetition of the office of Morning Prayer, but was nevertheless impressive. At the conclusion of the service the orgenistagain played the National ‘nthem and the Judge walked from the chureli to the Assize Courts to commence the business of the day. On assize days the Castle is net open for the in- spection of visitors but the public are admitted to the court. In order to see this part ef the build- ing I. entered tee Court welch is capable of RACOI32- mAatillg 1,000 persons. It is a lofty hall lighted by a dome over the barristers' table which opens to a roof of decorated oak. The judge's seat is :sur- mounted by profusely-adorned wood work aid over the B inch there is an egaestrian portrait of George 111.. Beneath the portrait I noticed three shields, b-aring the arms of George 111., John 0' Gaunt and ' Roger de Poictoa respectively. It is said that more peons have been sentenced to death here than in any other Court in the kingdom owing to the fact tint until of late years Lancaster was the only ! town at which assizes were held for the whole ! county.</p>
        <p>On leaving the Court I resolved to visit the tVil- liamson Park about which I had heard a great deal. To reach the park one has to climb a steep hill, but those who accomplish this are amply compensated for their exertions. I must canfess that this park is the prettiest that it bee ever been my privile4e to enter. Situated on a crowning eminence it com- mands a view that would take an eloquent, pen to adequately describe. Below the silvery Lune winds in and out of the valley in graceful curves whilst in the distance can he s, en its broad estuary with further beyond, the waters of the Irish sea. On the other hand the lofty mountain peaks of Cumber- land en-1 Westmoreland meet the gaze of the en- raptured visitor, and the whole scene is one cuneidered to be unsurpassed in the kingdom. On one of the highest hills hi the park is situated the Greg Observatory and irom here a view of 50 miles' radius cAn be obtained. By</p>
        <p>r paying the modest sum of one penny I was ad- mitted into the Observatory and the curator pointed out to me the principal objects of interest in the surrounding counrry and explained the use of the valuable astronomical instruments which are to be seen in this pretty place on the hill-top. He told me that the park was forty acres in extent and cost no less a sum than f. 126,000. It is named atter its generous donor the late Alderman Williamson. Its natural beauties are great. Heather grew in pro- fusion on small hillocks and a rustic bridge, spann- ing a small lake added to the delightful scene. One could hardly believe that twenty-live years ago this park was a wild mcmr. I at lengch bade farewell to Lancaster and once more ascending a tram retraced my way to More- cambe. The weather still continued beautifully fine and I intended on reaching Morecambe to go for a sail as on production of my railway ticket I would have been allowed this privilege free of charge. I. however, arrived at the Promenade just in time to find that the boat had left the Pier.</p>
        <p>As no viAt to Morecambe is complete without a peep at the pretty marine village of Heysham I mounted a tram which was running to the Straw- berry Gardens and after a short, but pleasant drive, arrived at the gardens. The village itself is about ten minutes' walk from here and is charming ia its rusticity. At the top of the hill the ancient church nestles among the umbrageous trees and a visit here is well repaid. The building is one of great antiquity and cannot fail to be of interest. Near the church yari are a number of stone coffins and I took advantage of the opportunity of examining them.</p>
        <p>I Time forebade me staying long at this peaceful I village, but had it been possible! should have liked to have spent a whole afternoon here. However, I again returned to Morecambe, and spent some time on the Central Pier to which the railway ticket again procured free admission. A young Tommy kkins was nere delighting his lady admirers, by singing a song with the refrain of "Break the news to mother," which elicited rounds of applause. At seven o'clock I repaired to the King's Arms Hotel where the heads of the firm and a number of guests were provided with a capital dinner. At 10-30 I wended my way to the station and half an hoar later was speeding on my way to "Good o/d War- rington" feeling satisfied that this was one of the most delightful outings I had ever experienced. I think others were of the s me opinion too.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>SHIP CANAL POLLUTION.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL POLLUTION.</p>
        <p>Manchester Corporation Fined.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. M. at°, Q.C., stipendiary megistrate for the Meuchester Division of the Coulty Palatme, on Wednes- day moraine. at the Strange waye Pislice Court, gave the deci.inn of him. If and other magistrates woo heard et luescley ths complaint of th M irsey and lrwell Joint Committee against toe Mincnester Corporation in respect of the continued pollution of the river Irwell, otherwise the Ship jtnal. The lesrned Stipendiary Magistrate gave judgment as follows :--We had an oppee unity after this case concluded yeiterday to cone sider our judgment, and we gave a most anxious and careful consideratien to the evidence that was given and the very able address made to WI by M. me:pies. This I Is a summons taken out by the M-reey and Irwell J 'int Com nittee, in which they aek 'or penaltlei to be imposed on the Corporation of the city or Manchester for the p-ollution by them of the Irwell. The pollution is admitted. and, minter facie, no course is open to us eut to inflict penaltes. We have, however, been urged with great earnes rules, not to inflict penalties but to alj horn the consider tion of Wale matcer until Seeeem- bet. We have thus to consider the whole question, and we ask (ourselves. W at oaght to guide us in this coo- siderati on ? We decided in May, 1898, that the answer to this qeestion mainly depended on an answer to anether qiiestien, ne.m-lv, Have the Corporation deter- mind upon any effective scheme for dealing with the s 'wage? arid w s title cten'e tnt is the real question. Now what is the position of the mater? Twelve years ago, namely. in 1888 the first inquiry was held by the Local Governme it Board at the iretance of the Corpo• aeon, and land was in coeseqeence nurthaeed for the purpose of filtration, but nothing has beea done since then. In consequence, on many occ I . ens tres matter lass ben before 121, and on %lay 12, 1838. we refused to grant any further time, and it May, 1899, nothing having been 'lore we imposed penalties of £lO a day for flee days. Niw at that time, as now, the Loesl Government. B ard, who have toe controlling voice in the in atter, ahaolately refused to permit any menet, t‘e be brewed for Bewaesi purpesee unlees an adequ.te ernouet of land was perchased and used for land filtreeion. Tnie the Corporation had refused, and apparently still refuse to do. At toe time of the hearing of these eurninousee a further i cj li-y the Local Governmeet B card was impeneing, and were told by the th in L net Mayor (Ar W H Vaudrev)— and that .rettly h.,— hat the result of that inquiry should be at onee ilehmittee (if he was L ird Mayer) to a meetinr of the Council, aid he hoped and hetievai they would act up .re it. Whet Was don'? The Cerperation instructed three very eminent gentlem-in to report oa the sewage question to the Corp oration, and at or about the same , time as t hin rep r was r(-Aced the Leal Government Board laid down tee coed tient; on svhiela alone they ecoohl allow money to he berrewed. These eond.tions eieeeed feline% hist troll the en le.re' report, and on the 21qt Fohruarr the Coon It col.liered a report of he It vers Co emit-ea of tee C orporetineen whirt these two rPoorta wer,. emb ,(Ued.arri they palaeri a rese u-ion hiniing thorn-eves o nettling Sine then every effort hes he ei made to indire the Local Government Board to alter :h er views, but on May 29, 1900, they finally refused to do se. Wa should 03 this have eepected the Ceuec;l to b So the inevit- able, hut ins out they have, nledging theineeives to do nnythine, eirected Mr. Wilkinton (ene.inesr) to q•=t out Wane. srelifitwinns, &amp;0., for both scheme-- ets !hat they know the Local Gnvernment B tail cvi not aolont.ani ore that th'y thee' are toe ,me hero-e the Ceuneil in S Wernher. Ira toe meentim 25 00000 gallons of eewaere with oily th. aolil preeipitated, are to be poured dewn noon the us- willing inhabit:anti below, and th's for an ind"ar,it • period, so far as any definite scheme of the city is con- oernee. Sheol we be deng ow duty not only to the inhabitants below the outran but to the numprousitowne awe district c uncils above, who have been enmnelled commence works at great colt, haring retard to their rateable vette', ntel who argue before us : " What is the use of 1.19 cleansing the river above when the trwege of Mencheseef eeee in below ?" if we do not inanose penalties in this case? To the question, then," Ought we to i npeee what- ties ?" I think there can be hut rine enswer. We have consi 'evil the difficu!ties of Manchester, but do they dif ir from those of other places? If there is in. greater volume of se wage there is also a much larger rateable value to spread the coat upon. Toe difficulty really is that a nropertion or the Corporation do not realte the absolute necessity of doing at once what elene the Local Geyer imen t Board will sanction, and do not rsiaFee the great injury th y are deine day by day to those below their °erten. If the Corporation had resolved to pro- need with one scheme or the other our decision would have been different. What, then, are the penaltiee we must impose? Having regard to Mr Rhodes's statement that plans and sp cifications are b 'int! Prepared by Mr. W.lkinson, arid that the whole question will be con- sidere I in September, we shall /rat impose penalties for all the days referred to in the summons, but considering that this is the second occasion on which we have to im- pose pona!lies, we think they must be Increased,and WP therefore impose a penalty of £5O in respect of each of five days mentioned in the summons (amounting to £250) and the costs. We ought to add that if nothing is decided in September we shall have no option but to impose penalties, if asked to do io,ie die in diem.</p>
        <p>HALTON. HALTON LADIES' CLus.—Tl Halton Ladies' Citib, friendly aneiety, for the last 84 years has celebrated its anniversary by "walking the village," attending divine service, and feasting and dancing on the Castle grounds- Last Monday the function was celebrated in all its nolendour. Led by the Runcorn Volunteer Band, under Bandmaster Outhwaita the club paraded hsadarl by Lady Brooke, Master Brooke, Rear S Wikineon, Rev G D *ray, Messrs J,bn D &amp;mann and T Wright ('ruqtees) and Percy R Wright(secretary). Then followed a Weas.nt tea, and dancing etc., on the green. Later th Tr.. was a pyrotechnic display, and it was not until a late hour that the company dispersed.—The financi.l statement shows the society to be in a sound position as regards funds and memherstfp. WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY .Thin event waa oh 4. rued en Sunday, when spe ial sermons were preached by the Rey. H Valentine, formerly of the Ron- corn circuit. The snholar4,asFilt.d by the ohnir,renderod nun.ber of spa,' ally selected hymns in pri ea7,3frhy msnner. The cotlections amounted to £27. which is an advance on last year's proceeds of about £5. ACCIDENT.—On Monday a yen beloneing to 3 Beasley, of Warrington, was ascencling the brow leadi-g to the Castle Hotel, when 'he horse slipped down, irjaring both knees and breaking one of the shkfte. Sercq,,ant Bennett and P.C. DIY:on happ•nied to he :thew at the time and rendered promo' angi4tance in ”.ttinc the horns , on its legs again. Beasley's wife and c'.ildren were in ' the yen, but they were happily none the worse for the minhap. INIZOLST.—On Friday evening, Mr. , Dobson, coroner, h‘ld an impost a.. the Norton Arms, Hilton, totiebOng the death of Either Ann Leach, aged seven months, daughter of JAn L ach, labourer. —The evidence showed that the child bad suff2red prom teething. On Tuesday , night it was put to bed at 7 30, and early the next morning the mother was wakened by its ' cries, and ' found it was in a lit. Death took place about halt an. I hour afterwards. The jury found tbat it died from Con- vulsions.</p>
