      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Shocking Case of Neglect at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>Shocking Case of Neglect at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>I D,tective-Sergeant S Gregory in a story as told to the Raec)rn magistrates,on Monday last. ciiecicsed how a husband and wife and two children had existed for some considereane time past. It appeeree Gregory ob- tailed " certein informacion"and ac:ing up al it visited a number of empty houses in Water-street and Back Brunswick-scree., in company with Constahe Edwarde. This chit was timed at 2 a.m. on Sunday last. Among other houses visited Ni. 1 &amp;Lek Brun2wick-street was selected, and here for a time the officers' progiees wag tarred, for the chor was locked. Kuocking, they got no answer, but on repeatiag the upera.tieu, a mares voice cad: d out " Who there ?'' Gregory's reply was the laconic" pence," whereupon the door was opened,and the constables entered, atter a short parley with the man. Further examination di,closed to the police that Michael and Ann Corcoran, and their two children Mary Ekon (8), and Anne (2) were the inhabitants of a room of4che house. which wee eight or nine feet below the level of the outside street.Tne place was but rudely tiled and extremely damp. The only furniture—if furniture it could be called—was two old chairs and a tin box. The woman was in a drunken sleep upon a mattress of. inferior condition about 2 feet by 3 feet, and the little children were le:noted upen the floor with a portion cr2 their heads on the mattress. Upon waking the ctildrere they cried forifood. Both officers had looked to the little ones and found them but scantily clothed and shivering from cold: A further detailed description the officers gave of the room, is best summed up in the words, retchedness, squalor and filth. As both ttie husband and wife were helplessly druek, and the room reeked with the smell of beer; and, furthermore, ae neither could eatisfactorily account for the right of in- hibiting there, the officers divided the coup, Sere geant Gregory taking Corcoran, and leaving Eivardg to escort Mrs. Corcoran, and the two children to the polies stitien. There the police prosecuted their examination further—as is their wont--and three pawn tickets were found upon Corcoran. One for a watch (3i. 61.) dated 6:h July; another for a coat and vest (84. 6d.) dated 8% of July ; nod the third for a pair trousers (Is.) this latter, bowever,wes of an earlier date and didn't enter into the bearings of the case. From all accounte, however, it was very apparent that the other motey had been spent in drink by the pabrentbt: On examining the children, (both of whom werepre,,ented nefore the court, after a very generous handling y police), the police found they had been very mueht ' ne.tlected,and in a manner toe that was detrimental and , injurious to their health. They were filthily dirty and ve!minoue, and truly could it be said their" lives had not fallen in pleasaut places." In the presence of their I parents, the eldest girl said they had been neglected, and had received nothing to eat for a long time ; and none of them bad slept in a bed for over six months. Naturally the upehot of the Constables' discovery was ' the preference of a char ,e against both parents for- neglect of their eMpring in such a manner as to cause unnecessary suffering and injury to health, which was heard by Messrs. Henry Reynolds and F. J. Norman, J.P.'s on Monday. B tli hrisonArs p!eaded guilty, and elected to be sum- marily dealt with. Then it was that the two cffieers of the law unfolded this story as set out above, and this too eves supple- relented by evidence of Mrs. Elzr Dunning, 20. Brans- wick-street; and Mrs. Ellen Stringer, Water-street, who had known both prisoners and who bad many a time given temporary sustenance to the children. The questioning of these witnesses, by Superintendent D. Pearson. also elicited the fact, that Mrs. Kelly the female prisoner's sister,hsd been asked by the police to cleanse and cloth the c iildren. and sustain them, which accounted for the fairly becoming appearance they male before the Court.</p>
        <p>The B.lnch committo-d both the prisoners for three months, to Knutsford Prison, without ths option of a fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. There was an averaee attendance at the meeting of this Society on Sunday afternoon. Alr.Williain Beswick presided an in the comes of his address he referred to the Government's treatment of the Bill for the prevep- tion of the Sale of Drink to Children. Although it bad been a disappointment to all temperance friends that the Bill had not been passed,let thin not be discouraged, for he believed that it would not he long before the children's charter would be placed upon the statute Proceeiling to refer to the crisis in China, he spoke of the difficulties of the missio,:arle3 and defended them again4t the attempts which had been made to make them responsible fcr the present crisis.—Mr. J. H. ll)wman, of Liverpool, d livered an interesting' address on " Character : its paramount importance." Miss Finch very sweetly sang "The Better Land." Ma service WV! alco taken part in by Mr. R. Atkinson, Mr. William Beswick, jun., and Mr. Scriven.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Latchford. oa Sunday the annual sTmoes in connection with the above place of worship were preached—afternoon and even:ag—by the Rev. John Youge, of Wycliffe Church. There were large congreeations. In th 3 afternooe the rev. gentleman took for the subjpet of his sermon WlFt.t is the truth ?" and at the evening service he basPrl his remarks on the words " We see Jesus." Special anthems—" Behold how good and joyful " (afternoon) and "Oh ! Clap your bards. " (eveaing)— were creditably rendered by the choir. In the morning: t. young eeoWe'g service was held, wh-n the pastor, the Rev. J. F. Ma' rhewe, delivered an address. The collec- Mow, which were in aid of the church funds, amounted to the sum of £IS 83 91, which was considered very satisfact o</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON Coming Events. A Palo of work aud g.rden fee in aid of Horne and Foreign NiiB9iol3B, will take place in the Rectory, Girden en Thursday and Saturday next. On Thurs- day it will be opened by Mrs Charlton Parr, and on 4aturday by the Mayoress ofprarrington. The amusements will include tennis and croquet tourna- ments and a cycle gymkana. Bands will be in attendance to play for dancing in the evenings, auo ' refreshments will be provided.</p>
        <p>Personal. Master Dyott Darwall, elder son of the Vicar or Walton' has scored another success at Rosso!' School.having just obtained (by examination) a clerical exhibition, in addition to his Previously. won scholarship. The school rules, however,forbid the holding of both, so that he will be compelled to resign the scholarship, i while holding the exhibition. \ We are pleased to bear of the success of Mr. George E. Clay, of Park Buildings, Warrington, late assistant to Mr. Percy Si!cock, F. R.T.8.A., and now chief architeeural assistant to Messrs. Rayner and Bridgland, of New Road, Gravesend. who has oucceqfully passed the 3rd and Final Qualifying Mxtetnination of the Royal Institute of Brikish Archi- tects held in London in June last. Anomalies of the Burial Laws at Warrington. At the Conference of tile Urban District Councils Association, held at Llandudno on Saturday, Mr. Baker, secretary of the association, gave a short address on the Burial Grounds Bill, The Bill was a wise one, in that it removed certain anemones in the burial laws, and was a foolish one in the way it had been drafted. Under the law as it at present stood at Warrington, although the three burial grounds attached to the three parish churches were closed, and interments took place at the Corporation cemetery, the incumbent and sex-. ton of the parish in which any person (Nonconfor- mist or otherwise) died was entitled to a fee, although no service was rendered by him and although the burial rites were petformed blyedhtios 'own clergyman or minister, and this bad serious dissatisfaction on the part of Nonconfor_. mitts in Warrington. /n the session of 1899 the 'Corporation of Warrington promoted a Bill, a clause of which, drafted by the speaker, proposed to put an end to this state of things, and to authorize Some small amount of compensation to be paid to the clergyman or sexton affected thereby, 'flie day tollowiog the passing of the clause by the Comnaittee, the promoters were informed that, the matter had teen considered of great importance, involving, in a thin end of the wedge degree, the question of the disestablish- txi en*, of the Church of England. On an eslsuracce that the matter would he dealt with by general law the Corporation agreed to withdraw the clause, and credit wgs due to the Government for baying introduced the 8111 in question, the third clause of which dealt with the matter of fess. Although he was a staunch Churchman, he woulo cordially vote for the diseqablis meat of the Churck of Erigiend to the extent shown in this clause. While credit alight be due to the Government for thGeobveiirin''mi itvts'ereeffeeecrttcaoinullyd the principles at not tit heereninlidtetrostaenoyd by anyone who had not studied every Act el Parliament referred to in its achwitile. —After a discussion the conference approved the , principle of the Bill, and also decided to petitioni Parliament to add a clause to the measure to 40- 2 move the distinction between the registratic3,Of - persons buried in consecrated and uncousee- ,tedi grounds.</p>
