It goes clampion, and as well ras ever." Oa Saturday is:rotating it also appeared to to. in !pull fet,le an.i was t.tlr, n to Newton. was in good condition and e ouid int be called a wreck.— The Chlarm on : k.ou c ionot cad it sound if it has b.-en tame en and off for six mon!hs.—Mr Uuswortta : I dio mot say sound, bah: ginerally .0 go id condition. It was wont, it r. ss,ri L ,agturu that it ehowed signs of weak- nsi The must be satisfied that there was a guilty knowledge that at e horse was lame befo:e the% cauld . If he to k okit a horaeand it fell lame on toe way h shottid act b 3 cba-ged with cruAt. ;it would be most unreasonable —Toe ease a.ainst the boo WAs diain,•s d; nth r d-fendant was tined 101. ano the Chairman advis d the Inspector to keep his eye on the sutural. A horse in tba condition described was nt-re tit tor toe kria; ker's yard than anything eise.-51t B .rd: I ask yiiu to lno‘r at it them—The Claim:tan: Tiers is no necessity to do so. THe BEGOINGIImPosiriIN. At. the Warrington Police Court on Toesday b.fere Aldei man Sutton (in the chair) and Mt. (fireball. John .Nalsh was charged on remand with being a rogue and a vagabond and going about as a coPector and gatherer oh alms unner preterces on Saturday. Jan. 30tn.-- -qun• Mo•wyban the /acts of the ease which appeared in our I st is ue md ineu the magisttaAb that it ~aci vnu -.Cr- tamed that prisoi.e: had been getting his living by beg- ging. He had been convicted rereral times, having served two months for going round begging with a ,child.ife had never been m+rried osither had he work .0 in a fac,ory. hem &amp;aid he had read a b u'; the fire RI nAwspaper.—Prisnoier who pleadel for mercy .P. 13 corn- taitted to gaol for three monrhs, and the maney found in 01= p ,6session was ordered to go towards Ilia mainteu (SECO.</p>
