      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MINOR CASES. Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>zRIDAY.—Refore the irtnyor _thq chair,. Councillor</p>
        <p>Smettulytt, Dr. Glirnalland Mr. tfepfi-ord.</p>
        <p>131t1ACK TBE FACT )RY ACT3.—The W-hitecross Corn- party. Limited, were summoned for a breach of the Factory Acts. by empl,ying three young persons after the stipulated boura,—Mr Milner appeare for the Company and admitted the ofttnce —lnsp-ctor Retook said he visited the works at 9.30 p.m. on the 13th ult., and found three young pert° s working. The hours of emp'oytnent are from six in the ni„rniiic to 4t x at night, 30th kt they had worked three and a naif ho •ra' overtime. Two of the youths were 15 veers of age and one was 17.—Mr. Milner said the youths w-re employed by a sub-contractor. An accident to a galvanising bath cocu:vd at tho works, and as they were anxioua to execute the orders they arranged to change the "tuins." Instruetions were given that the boys must cease work at eight when some othe• workmen w..uld come on. The men, hawever, did no; turn up and the boys work4d on. It was not the fault of the firm but of the sub-contractor—Mr. Hepher : You have been here before on the same charge.— The Clerk : Yes sir —The Mayor to Mr. Miler): Mr. Hepberd thinks you are an inoarrigible offender. (Laughter.) —The Clerk : Mr. Winer has been here twice before.—Mr. Hepherkt Beni him to poi withaut the option of a fine. &lt;Laughter.)—A fine of 5s and costs wail imp tsed in each case. PITCH awn loss.—Robert Higham, 142. Forster-street, and James Leigh, 6, Bowes-street were summoned for playing pitch and toss in Forate—street on the let in-t. —P.C. Clough Proved the case. —Highana Was fined 10a including costs, and Leigh who did not appear at coat until about 10.20 instead of 10 o'plecic was fined 15* ABSAULT.—Tirtiothy Sauce, of Coronation Place, was tined 5s for &amp;transiting Sarah Collins on the 3rd of July, fitatinna.Y.—Bef,re Messrs Birchall (in the chair), B. T. Fairciongh and J. W, Hayward.</p>
        <p>- Soup TRROWN OVER • POLICEMA.S.—JarnI33 Donlan, Elllver-equar , was charged with asaulting P.O. Ile.p while in ".he ox.ciri ion of hie duty in 251117er-square at nine o'clock on Prid iv, and with wilfully damaging his tunic to the amount ,of £l. He pies .ed guilty.—P.C. Heap said he was in Pinner'e-br ,w when he received a complaint from the prisoner's brother that the prisoner was in his house acting In a very disorderly mower. He (witnoss) went to the house, wren the prisoner said Now you have brought the pollee I will be looked up" Witness told h m that there was thri no cause for him to be Wired up but the prisoner threw aem of hot soup over him, spoiling his tunic.— Superint -ndent Monazhan said thit was the prisoner's sixth appearance.—He was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour. Mosnmr—Bsfor. Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Councillor Smethurst, Messrs. S. Wright, J. W. Hayward. and</p>
        <p>T. Birch, i. A WILT:NT Sow:al:R.—Thomas McKenna, a soldier. stationsi nrford Bari acks was sh.rged with being drunk and 41i.orderly in Winwick street. at 7 50 on Sunday night And with 1+834%3:Una P.C's Cooper (36) aril Monks —P.C. ecoper said that the prisoner puller his tunic off and wFaited to figh nnb.r soldier. He requ,sted him to go Away but he would not do era P.C. Monks come on the scene awl prisoner threw himself down and kicked thorn both oa the leas several times. With the assistance of the picket they got him to tin Pollee Statiun.—A tine ot as arid coats was imposed. THREE Monans FIR AgilULT.—Mart:n Monaghan, a atone num, „f K,,atoord road was charged with being drunk and disorderly hi Konteford-road at 10.40 on Saturday night and with assaulting Mary Ellen Monaghan, his wile and Mary Ills daughter at 10 o'CL'ek on the mune night. Cooper</p>
        <p>23) stated that he was on duty at Bridge Irc,ot in company with P.O. Dyson and in consegaence of information received they went toMrs Monaghan's hone ..They saw Mrs Monaghan who said she had b-en assaulted by the prisoner. He had iiio brak,n some of the woonwork of the windows at the hacK of the house ane had gone away. At 10 40 prisoner came down on the Latchford 'cut. He was drunk and on going to the intuit, he said " lam going to make a alsughter here to-night. ' He was taken to the Police Station. Mary Mien Monaghan, prisoner's wife, said that on Saturday night her husband came inte the house in company with a "corner man." The man commenced to make use of filthy language so she ordered him out. Her husband then threatened to take her life end struck her. She got him Into the backyard and he picked up a brick, and threw it at the window. The brick struck her daughter. In order to defend herself witness struck him with a poker.—Corrobora- live evidence having cern given prisoner who made his 18th appearance was seat to gaol for three months. TUESDAY—Before Alderman Sutton (in the chair) and</p>
