        <p>THROWING PEPPER INTO A WoMires MACE —Aim Roberts was summoned hr assaulting Martha Headlam at tiolborne on the 27th of June —Mr White appe arel fcr the defen lent who pleade I not guilty. —Comolainant who reildei at Edge Green stated that defendant's son threw stones at some pigeons and her husband "ran him off." Shortly afterwards defendant came to the house and commenced '' blackguard- ing" her hosoand. Witness ordered her out and defendant said ' I will blin yam and him and all." Defendant then called her foul names and went away. In a few minutes she returned and threw a hand u 1 of pepper in hr face. Witness informed a policeman ot what had o •curred, - For the defence Mr White said that complain- ant's husband very severely thrashed deienriant's boy for throwing stones at pigeons. The lad came Wale cryins with his nose bl-eding and the mother not unneturativ went to interview the men. He told her if she dal not mind tie wou'd thrash her. Complainant, at this point, came out with a laag handled broom and threatened to hie. the defendant. His client -,aid " Hit me if you dare,"and r used 11,ir hand. She denied throwing any pepper.—Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for three months. Funinus linatsa.—Two boys name I Alfred Briscoe and Joseph Bevan were charged with furiously riding horses at Newton on the 3rd inst.—P.C. Clark said the defendants were ,uriously riding horses in Comrann.roa.i. There vr-re children about at the time.—A tine of Is and costs was imposed. (fassizsni wren Clans. Frank Burgess, George Burgess and Pater Murray were charged with gaming with cards in a nublio place at Newton on the let inst.—P.O. Fox sal I tie off ince took plum on the Niesnes. Defen tants were playing at "All foura"and pa ised money several times —Uurrav who he been before the Court twice previously for similar off ,nces was fined fis and eosts, and the other defendants were mulcted in a sum of Is and cests.—James Walker, Benjamin Nutial, John Burgess. William Owen. Richard Bargess..and Charles Anderson were summoned for is similar offe .ce.—P.o Fez said defendants were playing at"banker." —wined is and costs.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ii PUBLIC HOUSE.—John Bdi was charged with being drunk on lisensed premises at Golbome on July 9h aid with refasinz to quit wh4n requeste.l to de so. Ha was also charged with assassiting Itlizsbeth Goulding. wife ~f the bcencee.-11lizabeth Goniding whose husband is the landlord of the "Sign in the Cellar " Inn, stated that the defendant came into the house in a drunken condition. She requested him to go out but he efued to do so and struck h-r in the face.—Defendant was fined 5s for the assault and Ze 6d and costs for drunkenness. _ _</p>
        <p>VISOR OFFENCIC9.—Henry Herbert. John Flynn. and B iztbeth Hart. were each fined 2s 6.1 and costs for drunken 1193 S Sarah Ann Glre was bound over to keep tie peace fo,. c eating a disturbance in Church-road, Haydock.—Jame •th p..terd WRI fined 51 and costs fir not haviqg a lighted lamp alached to he vlhicle at 1 p m. on the ZSth Jo .e 4 try 4 urnty was bound over to keen the peace for mak ng use of threats towards ,Jane Singleton at Newton on the 8 h Last —James Woods was fined 10, and elsts for making use of obscene language at Newton on the let inst.</p>
