      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>NEW COMMERCIAL INTELLIGENCE BRANCH OF THE BOARD OF TRADE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW COMMERCIAL INTELLI- GENCE BRANCH OF THE BOARD OF TRADE.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Chambrr of Conamerce (Coun- cillor Benne:t) bas received the fallowing puticulars of the ablve</p>
        <p>The Intelligence Branch of the Cemmercial Depart- ment of the Board of Trade has been established with a view to meet the constantly increasing demand for prompt and accurate intermation on commercial matt ?rs, so far as it can be met by Givernment action. In de- ciding to establish this new branch, the B e*rd of Trade have been largely influenced by the recommendatiens contained in the r-port* of a Departmental Committee composed not only of representatives of the /maw G ivernment Departments concerned, but also of promin- ent representives of commercial interests, which was map. pointed by Mr. Ritchie, the president of the 'Board, in July. 1897. The committee referred to was requested to cousider and advise (1) at to the best means of collecting and of disseminating among those interested prompt and accurate information upon comndercial subjeets, and (2) as ta the collection of sample., espezially of goods ot foreign manufacture competing with British produc- tiona, and the exhibition of such samples to manufac- terers and traiers in this c Awry. As a result of their deliberations the committee recommended under head (1) the establishment of a new office, tinder the Beard of Trade, on lines which the Board have followei generally in the creation of the Intelligence Branch, and this Branch is consequently intended to become a centre se which ieformetion on all subjeo's of commercial in crest shall be collected and focussed in a form convenient for reference. In addition, it will to the duty of the Branch, as far as ciremxist Incas permit to afford information in reply to all inquiries on commercial maters which may be addressed to it, whether written or verbal. The new office which is temporarily bossed at No 50 Parliament-street, London. S W ,was openee on toe 23 d Octob:or last; its princip.l officer (under the Cernptroller- General of the Commercial. St itistical, and L .uour De partments of the Board at Trade) being Mr. T. Worthing- ton, who recently acted as Specie! Commissioner to the Board in an inquiry into the condition aed prospects of Rd ish trade in certain South American countries. The " Intelligeuce Branch" will endeavour, en application being made to it. to supply information with regard to the following sabjects, viz :Commercial SI; f tlStiel ; Matters relating to Foreign and Colonial Tariff. ; Ex,ese anl "Consumption" duties; Fort, Elerbeur, and Tonnage dues, and other chargee on Snippine : Customs Etsgula- ti me ; Consular Fees ; FIfMS of Certificates of Origin ; Regulations concerning Commercial Travellers ; Trading Licenses; Foreign and Coloaial Contracts open to Tender; Foreign and Colonial Bounties ; Lists of Firms engaged in particular Hoes of busiemis in different localities abused ; See, Ste. An inquiry room, at which copies of official publications, directoriee, and other works of reference may be crsulted, is reserved at the officee of the branch for the conveniences of personal apelicants. The" Board of Trade Journal " will be the principal medium through which intelligence collected by the branch and intended for•general information will be con- veyed to the puolic. fhe journal, whech hes lip to the present been issued monthly at the price of 6d is now issued weekly, at the price of It is intended shortly to give effect to the recommend- ations of the committee referred to above its resprct of the collection and exhibition of sampleeeind the arrange- ments neoessary to enable this to be done are now under the consideration of the B .ad of Trade.</p>
        <p>Oddfellows' Picnic. The committee conuected wan the Juvenile Burial Society of the Grand United Order of Odd. fellows held their annual outing to Bake- well and Chatsworth House, the popular resorts in Derbyshire. The party numbered nineteen, and on arrival at Bakewell luncheon was served. The party next journeyed to Chatswortho where the historical connections with this famous house were closely inspected. The drive was continued to Haddon Hall, and by a different route the party re- turned to their headquarters at Bakewell. Dinner was served here, and, needless t 3 add, after the enjoyable drive,justice was done to the good things provided by the host of the QuHen's Arms. The re• turn home was made at 780, awl there was a con- census of opinion that the outing was one of the most enjoyable ever participated in by the com- mittee. A (Jo.lssz. P)P TIES Br..r.ree is the 3hange you want when the blood vets out of condition, and Dr. JACOB TOWNRICNVEI SARSAPARZLLA. performs the needful with splendidly pleasing officaoy 11421tYrt. TIAIr IT. V 011712,51 ^1",7.12: 187</p>
      </div>
