      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SOCIALIST LECTURES AT</head>
        <p>SOCIALIST LECTURES AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A series of lectures have been delivered at Bridge Foot during the week under the au:Tines of the War- ringt ui Branch of the Independent Labour Party. The let:river vrss Miss Ada Nield (Mrs. Chew), of R ,chdale. Her first lec-ure on Sunday morning was on " Causes of P verty." Speaking broadly she said there were three kinds of poo*rty,—mental, moral and material. She believed that much of the two former kinds of poverty ~ere the outcome and result of the latter—material— kind, and would therefore ask them to examine specially the causes of material ra ,verty. There were other ref raltra than the S ciali3te who deplored the eff..cts of poverty, at d we o as much in earnest as they in working for its at,ofifion. Notable amongst these were the advoc ites of Temperance will believed that poverty would disappear if the people became tee- total- The S,cialists differed however. Teetotalism was a good thing for the individual, but if applied uni- versally by some m racti,ous means would in all proba- bility increase poverty instead of abolishing it, since the rate of wag( s always tended towards subsistence limit, and if it were found that men could do with less owing to temperate habits—they would have to do with less. Alter dealing with other pr ,fessed causes of pnvertv,the 1-cturer concluded by saying that the root cAuse was monop dv of land an,l cad al by a non-producing class All wealth was produced by labour applied 'to raw materials an I while I lin workers maintainvd in luxury an idle cl,ss there was bound to follow, as surely as night follows day an amount of poverty which causes much suffering and misery amongst a large class of society. _</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening "The Childree's Herit,ige" was the subject. The lacnerer F aid that she was confident that her subject mil t, epeoal to everybody. In England and Wales dunce' 1898, 161 infants (under 124 months) out of every 1.000 horn, died, totalling 200.900 deaths. Infant mortality variel considerably ; in working class dietricts :n towns it reached 250 per cent ; was very I much higher still in the slums arid came down to 80 per cent in country distrie,ts. Why tnis fearful slaughter innocents? Because working men were compelled by circumstances to live in unhealthy districts where the air was impure ; and in many cases were compelled to submit to have their wives neglect the most holy duty cif woman—the nursing and care of her infant—in order to help in the struggle for bread. In the Faroe Islands and Nor wee', where infants were always breast fed, mortality was uncle: 10 per cent,while in those countries where they are brought up on artificial food the rate was 30 per cent. During the siege of Paris whilst the death nate of adults was doubled that of infants Was reduced 40 per cent ; the same thing happiined during the cotton tacnine in Lancashire, the reason being that women wore compelled to suckle their children having no food to !alive them. The ceild of the worker was handicappel from birth upwards—lack of pure air, pure food (for the average worker could not afford to buy the best kinds of ood) and, half-educated, half-developed, deprived of the child's birrhright —freedom and opportunity to expand and find jey in living, had to begin while: still a child to earn his daily bread. In London I alone there were 144,000 children under 14 years of age I earning their living—end sech a living !—A Ceristian country should he ashamed to build up its wealth out of the blood and bone of its tender children. Socialism would at least give the cl,ildren a chance. "Socialism and the Home' was the subject on Monday evening Some people hid an idea, she sid that 8 icialism was going to destroy the sacredness of home, and lay the family circle in ruins. That was a mistake. floe lecttner spoke of the homes of the wealthy classes, , and said that the Divorce Courts proved that the home- I hie of these people was anything but sacred and happy: che hotline of the mildle classes were hotbeds of shams and jealousies and meannesses ; and the homes of the workers were not worthy the name of home. Situated in ell the finiest, dirtiest districts, all of one hideously monotonous pattern, Jerry huilt, furniture chew. garret made, and above all small and poky. A room 10 feet square contained 1.000 culec leer, wh co ought tote the space for one poise(' only, was often cc upied by five or more: Criminals, etc., ii ' pris ins get 600 cubic feet, whilst very few of the wi rk• in class, s got any thing like that. In huge fists all over London end other big towns people were bring dricee into trap-like rooms, pile i pen on pen, like cattle, shut out from the sunshine all for the benefit of ground land- lords and speculators. It was not Socialism that threat- eped the home hut landlordism and c ,pitalism. .iocialism, by depriving these two of their power, would lay the feundation of homes worthy the name, out of which shoeld come crizens worthy of a great country. Oe Tuesciae evening 'Dreams and Dreamers" were spoken of. Socialists were often taint el wi h being dreamers of dreams. People of educatioe of imagination and humanity all acknowledged socia lam to be a grand and jest scheme for the deliverance of mar kind from galling and crushing conditions of life, but—it was only a dream, Well, what of it ? All the ' blessings erij red by men to day which are the result of human ski!, and ingenuity have at one time been downs. Wny not Socialiem ? Theory always preceded practice ; the dreemer always wrs the advance guard of the army of practical builders. Wedneslay evening's subject waie'The Dawn of Sod d- iem." So far from Socialism latieg a dream she cen tends d that it was fast becoming a reality of every day life, though most people were not aware of it. Secialiern meant the collective ownership and control of every- thing necessary for the materiel well-being of men. Well, in one town after another articles of use and consumption, as gaS, water, elec- tricity, eramwaye, open spaces, &amp;c , and even milk supplies were being taken over by municipa- lities and controlled by them for the people of the res- pective towns. This was to much socialism. It was only a question of time. This is the dawn. The day would in:vitably follow. Land, houses, food and clothing sueplies would pass into the control of the people out of the control of individuals a, surely as the other things named. Toe forces of evolution were helping, and nothing could stay these. The growth of educa.,ion, thanks largely to the teaching profession was lot Iping, and nothing could stop the progreei of an enlightened community. Socialism was a force already in his country, and would have to he reckoned with in the near future. On Thursday evenine the snlej• cs. was" Brotherhood." She pointed out that for 1900 years brotherhood had been preached, and was stil preached in the pulpits of the land, yet v-ie bad the spectacle of two white peoplee— our own the one—et war with each other re-day-, and in the Per East trouble and unrest and , probable blordshecl bee use Christian nations are jealous and envieus one of another. At borne in the various ' grades of life all is contusion and comparative misery. Socialists were accused of setting class against class. ' There was no need of that ! Class was already set against class and at war with each other and muit ever to while a social system based on monopoly and compe- tition was tolerated. The employing class had to com- pete and undersell each other in order to make profit out ef that which the worker had produced, and the worker had to compete with his follow worker in ercii r to get a chance to work at all. Were the results likely to engen- der the feelings which make for brotherhood ? It was mockery to simile of men being brothers while maintain- ing a 6tato of society which made the interests of all clash. Brotherhood was based on equity and justice, which only Socialism could give.</p>
