      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>• .</p>
        <p>'lll'4 ' ' 4 •</p>
        <p>• rif-</p>
        <p>Urtterg to the ctor.</p>
        <p>Colonel Ridgway and the Employers</p>
        <p>of Volunteers.</p>
        <p>.Sir,—Kindly allow me through the medium of your paper to acknowledge the generosity and pa- triotism of the employers of Warrington, in allow- ing the members of this Battalion in their employ- ment to attend camp this year for a fortnight, as owing to their number, it would be difficult for me to do so individually. it may be of interest to them, however, to know that their kindness has not been wasted, but that the whole battalion has received great benefit, both in a professional military sense and physically, from the increased training thus received, and has returned with a due sense of the increased obligation now laid on them to do their duty thoroughly both to their town and country when called upon to do no.</p>
        <p>I therefore would now tender the thanks of the officers, non-commissioned officers and men of the battalion for the hearty and cordial eupport they have received from all classes in their efforts to render themselves more efficient to perform their duties as a unit in Englann's citizen army.</p>
        <p>I renaain, yours truly, JlsTe ). CLARE RIDGWAY, Col. P.D. Hear-I -quarters, let Vol. Batt. Suuth Lane. Reg. Warrington, 6th July, 1900.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>RETURN OF WARRINGTON VOLUNTEERS FROM souni AFRICA.</p>
        <p>During the week "gentlemen in khaki" have again been familiar figures in Warrington streets. Since the war in South Africa commenced it has been a usual thing to meet khaki-clad volunteers, and militia men and regulars in Warrington.but the appearance of the men noticed this weekihas attracted more than usual attention. Their faces have been browned by the fierce iays of the sun on the South African veldt and some of them bear distinct traces of recent illness. The explanation is that about 20 members of the First South Lancashire Regiment including four of our own Volunteer Battalion, have been invalided home , from South Africa. They arrived in town on Monday evening and it is a matter of course to say chat they were gladly welcomed home by their rela- tives and friends. This was particularly the case in regard to our own volunteers, who of their own true will, have faced the perils of war and have been placed hors de combat in doing so. It is a singular 'fact that all of those who have arrived home this week have suffered not from the bullets of the Boers, but fram the rigours of the climate. Enteric fever and dysentery have been the main causes in i incapacitating them from further service, and we learn that of the first contingent of Warring:on Volunteers more than half of them have been in 'hospital with disease. The Warrington Volunteers who have returned home are</p>
        <p>Private Massey, E Company. Private Fairbrother, D Company. Private Hodge, D Company. Private Houghton, G Company. Private Massey was the first of the Warrington Volunteers to return home In conversation with a reporter of" The Examiner" he suited that he was taken ill with diarrbcea and dysentery at Johannes Kop,about 18 miles to the north of Lady- smith. Ho was instantly removed to the field hospital and from thence to the ho4piti.-1 at Mooi River. His temperature rose to 105 d,grees and he wonders now how he recovered. some of his com- rades died when their t.imperature had reached 104 degrees and the doctors told Massey that it was owing to his strong constitution that he had pulled through. Asked as to the hospital arrangements, he said they were very satisfactory and there was no lack of food or attention. The medical officers and nurses worked hard under mos; trying condititions, and deserved praise for tb(”r labours. He heard of no comp:aints on the Na;al side of inadequate hospital arrangemenA. He been granted two months furlough, and at the end of that time will return to South Afdca if he is needed.</p>
        <p>On proceeding to Wellington-street, our represt.m- tative had a talk with Private C Feirbrother who has just recovered from an ataLck of coterie fever. He state' that he and his companions returned home on the Assays. which he afterwards learned was the vessel which took out to South Africa the second contingent of Warrington Volunteers. He was attacked with fever at Ladysmith and had been a patient at the hospital at hlstcourt. Several other men of the South Lancnshires, including two of the Warrington Volunteers, had also been patients at this hospital. They were Hassell and Taylor, and be believed that both of them would arrive home in a short time. When they recovered at the hospitals all the men with very few exceptions, were sent home.</p>
        <p>Private Hodge has been attacked with dysentery, and Private Houghton. who bolongi to the Newton contingent of Volunteeri, has been a sufferer from diarrho3a. All the men are convalescent and are rapidly recovering their usual health and strength.</p>
        <p>An Earlestown Soldier's Experience in Hospital.</p>
        <p>An Earles,own soldier, L. C. Worslev, 2nd Royal Lancashire Regiment, who was invalided home after aa illness of enteric fever, speaking abow, the fie d hospital arrangements in General Buller's division, says that the provisions for the wounded and sick we better than ie ever expected, and that, unerally, the sufferers seemed to he satisfied with the arrangements. He was first attacked with rheumatism and fever at Batton's Farm, shortly after the Spion Kr)p engagement. There the attention bestowed on the patients was of the hest, with what the hospitals could give. Jellies were provided, and everyihinfr„ pfissible done to alleviate their suffeiings. He was later attacked with cr,- . .</p>
        <p>teric fever. - and after being in hospital at :Elandslaagte for six days, from there he w.s removed to Pietermaritzburg, where he was placld in the soldiers' barracks. Here they were attended by nurses, and were treated like elitlren—ia fact, the attention was better than he rcceived ia the home liropita.ls. Referring to the attempts to relieve Ladysmith, be maid that if General Buller had not been sac:eaglet in eetting through when he did, it would have killed him. He was most popular with the men, and many of them had declarad that they would rather fight under him than anybody. What ne thought was the hardest part of the campaiga was the forced marching, necessary to keep up with the Boers. They had to bivouac night ater night in torrents of rain, and for thirteen nights, they were wet through each night, either throu4h rain or through heavy new. In the course Gf their marching they had frequently to wade through water which rea‘Thed to their knees. and at Bushman's River. between Estcourt and Free, the water was up to their breasts. The men, however, accepted everything with a cheerful spirit, and so long as they were provided with a smoke were quite recon- ciled to the hard.ahips. Luxuries were, of c )(use, out of the question. Parceli sent hv friends at home had not yet reached him, and the packets of tobacco sent out for the men had, in wany cases. no b,.en deuvrud. Prob- ably owing to the transport difficulties tti-.1 goods were being kept for delivery at a more favourabla time. The Irish at Colenso.</p>
        <p>Comrades line the south bank and bkz away to cover the crossing ; bet the Boers aro so artfiilly con- cealed in trenches and behind breastworks that it is almost impossible to get a eight of one. More furious Irishmen plunge into the water and swim to meet rhe foe. Here a man is drowned, there a man is shot. One • after another comes within a few yards of the nen!) bank, only to be almost blown to pircea ny a hurricane of lead. But there is no turning hack, no givin. way. They have been ordered to effect a crossing at Bridle Drift ; and, although there is no drift, cross the river they will, ii they p?nd nine-tenths of their force. It is murder, nothing lees then murder, to ask thee- magni• ficent battalions to cross an untordable river hi Id by a well-armed enemy whom thy cannot see. much less strike at. With superior i.rtillery it might be possible. Without the protection of big gees it is a mere useless waste of blond and courage. •Still, a few accomplish the impo,aiible. 0.,e tiny band at last finds itself on the northern bank. And what does it see ? It sees row upon row of trenc e . and in front a network of barbed wire, and beyond great cannon vomiting shrapnel. Toe only cfileer who has emceed is a sergeant of the Deblina, and hi, at once alstrYlP4 eom- rn ,nd. His orders ars brief. " Fix vow kroles, bhey ! Let's make a came for ourselves." A wee orroo Erin's battle-cry brealus from the lips of th. littie knot of heroes. The words stir their warm Irish blood. What care they forideath or wounds? Thi y will make a name for themselves. They will wiite it led in tee best blood of the burghsrs. There is a trench !nil 6f Boers a little way al(ng the bank. Perchance some of Blake'a legions are in it. "Charge !" And like a whir!. , wind the Irishmen rush upon the temch. ' so terrible is the onslaught,, • ee ael fierce their bxttle-cry, that the rifiei;gen in the trench seem paralysed. Their fire becomeilnurried and</p>
        <p>aimless ; and ere they can recover nerve and confidence a score of fighting. tearing &amp;wits, some bare-headed all dripping bl.,pd and water, hurl themselvus into the entrenchment. This is the moment of moments. For this the little band bays swum the river of blood. For this they have suffered the wounds and death of friends and comrades. rney glut themselves with carnage. Thrust! And the fell s eel crashes through and through the breastbone. One runs; but a figure flies through the air alter him. It is a Dublin Fusilier with rifle and bayonet held 4t the charge; and the Boer with ono shriek falls on his face, the blood streaming from holes in back and front H x many in a were in thst trench h not known. Toe Dublios could not stop to count; and the Boer returns ware never accurate. But it ia certain that not a single man of them as left alive,— From " Cwsell's History of the Bo.r War " for July.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>MILITARY NOTES.</head>
        <p>MILITARY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant Winser, of the 3rd battalion South Lanc.ilhire Regiment and who joined that corps only last Dec Amber has been appointed a transport effieer in South Africa.</p>
        <p>Caitain Adler Ist Volunteer battalion Liverpool Regiment with headquarters at St. Ann's-street, Liverpool has been transferred to the Militia becom- ing Captain in the 3rd battalion :outh Lancashire Regiment taking over command of a company in that corps now in South Africa. Captain Adler has been not quite seven years a Volunteer officer. He was appointed becond-Lieutenant in September 1893; promotei Lieutenant in August 1891 and be- anie Captain last March. He is a very capable and energetic officer who will prove a valuable addition to the 3rd battalion South Lancashire Regiment. Lieutenant Colonel Horsbrugh, has been appoin- ted to command the 2nd battalion Royal Lancashire Reserve Regiment in which ex-soldiers of the Liverpool Regiment and the South Lancashire Regi- ment are serving. _ •</p>
        <p>Lieutenant J b Murray, on promotion to his pre— sent rank, has taken over command of the 2nd Lancashire Engineer Volunteers in succession to Colonel Pendlebury, who retired from the com- mand of the 6t. Helens corps last month. As Captain Fraser will also, it is ex- pected, be promsted Major and second in command of the regiment, Lieutenant C. T. Brown will also be advanced to the rank of ' captain in the corp. He was appointed second lieutenant in June, 1896, and became lieutenant in December 1897. He holds a P. S. certificate and has also passei the course at tilt schuol of muske- try, Hythe. It is expected too that this officer will be appointed instructor of musketry wnen Captain Fraser vacates the post on advancement to field rank in the 2nd Lancashire ft V.</p>
        <p>Colonel H. T. Brown who lately gave up com- mand of the let Cieshire Volunteer artillery, will it is expected be appointed honorary colonel of the corps in succession to Colonel Evans Lloyd who has resigned the appointment after Aolding it since July 1886.</p>
        <p>Major and Ron. Lieutenant Colonel Savage °has been promoted aubitantive Lieutenant Colonel in the let Cheshire Volunteer Artillery and has become second in command of the corp.?. - Second Lieutenants Delme-Radeliffe and Hogg have been promoted to the rank of Lieutenant in the South Lancashire Regiment. Both these officers are serving in India with the 2nd battalion of die corps and they both joined tzie regiment in August 1898.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>-•-•-- A WARRINGTON LADY AND HER COSTUME.</head>
        <p>-•-•-- A WARRINGTON LADY AND HER COSTUME.</p>
        <p>Action in the County Court.</p>
        <p>His Honour as a Judge of "Fit."</p>
        <p>Before His Bomar Judge Gwytme James at the War- rington Couoty Court on Thursday, James Critcbley, tailor, of B )Id-street, str.!d Clan Percival,ot 29, Church- street, for £4 174 61, the cost of a coat and ekirt math) to order.—Mr. A. White' represented the ptaintiff and Mr. Forsoasv defendad.</p>
        <p>Mr. White, in slating the plaintiff's case told hia Honour that in March, this' year, the defendant called upon the plaintiff and asked for patterns for a coat and skirt. The price quoted was .24 153 o.i with silk lining for tbe coat. A. tew days atterwards, the defendant called and ha.vine selected Ihe pattern, ordered a skirt to be mWe with inside box pleating!' and also a coat with a steque batik. When the defendant subieq.zently tried them on she said she did not hike toe inside box pteatings but pref-rred outside box lest- io4s similar to those she bad seen in a ie 4-made clothing shop in the town. She also asked for the coat to be made tight-fitting. These altnations were duly made and 03 tne second try-on she made no complaint respec'Aug the skirt but said the coat was still too loose. Mr. Gritchley said he would make it tieitor. It was triad on a third time and the defendant th,,n said that the skirt Wid too tight about the hips and toe plaintiff said he would let the skirt cut. She also com- plained of tbecoat being wrinkled at the back and it was decided th it this should he remediea. A velv..t collar to the coat was suguestd and agreed to which brought ap the price to 4 17s 6d. The coat and skirt were tried on again and subiiectiently they were sent to the dezendant. Oo the 2nd of April she caled with the cost and skirt and sail she didn't like th:. coat at all and refused to have either the coat or skirt. She declined to let Mr. Critchley see the cat on. James Critchley, the plaintiff, was then called and bore cu:, his solicitor's statement. ne explained in detail the alterations sugosted by the defendant on the fil7d occasions on woich el: tried toe coat and skirt on. —Orosa-txs nined, the witness stated that the defendant did bring a pictorial representat:oa of the costume she desicei. He made it in accordance witti that design. When the defendant tried it on she sail she wan,ei it tighter, he told her he could not do 30 without putting in a new back. Ho did not put in a new back but he made it a little tieiter by altering the existing back. Clara Percival, the defendant, avaa than c tiled and stated that the coat had been made fir a big4er person than herself. P. was too lar e arid creased in the tiv She went to fit on the c.istutne 10 or U. times. It also creased at the waist the sleeves were not right, and the coat too bulky in the front. Tho skirt was too tight at the hips. - -- _ _ _</p>
        <p>Anc-ie Percival ',aye corrnblrative evidencf.</p>
        <p>His Hononr adc-d the tiefo.ndAnt tl go to one of the adjinioLf rooms ui pot on the c.'stwne. Whet] sh-) Caine into court again, His flonnur closely scrutinised</p>
        <p>Mr.Farshaw contended that it had not been made in accordance with the order. The back was very tuucn creased as Hia Honour would see and was very 10-tae in iped• The sluices were no:. right. He wat convince I n w that he IMF the coat upon the young lady that it bad not beeii rni.de in accorOanCe wi!ll instructions.</p>
        <p>His Honour, in giving jud2m-rit. said in this case th. order wa+ given by the deter-lit:lnt in the Wginning of March. The c stuine stem° to hav,. been tried en a great many times, but the order wa+ that the coat should have a sacquebacl= CorrevondLig to the pa7tern shown. There was no doubt that the coat to begin with WAS made too big and numerous alterations had tel b 3 m,cle until it got down to the confition they saw it in to-day. As re- garded the skirt there was practilallyl nothing the matter with it ; 4EII it seemed a very fair fit. The de tendant complained that the front of the c at was too large, but after examining it, he c .old not say that it was. I was bet ior to have it chat way than too tight. As retarded the b.ck, there were certainly one or two crises which were a mere notliin4. It was not a fault which OUgat ti 0,11. e her to retnrn the coat. The best opinion he formed of PI WAS that it Was a reagonabie fit an i the difendant must p.y for it. He would give judgm-int for the plaintiff with costs.</p>
        <p>1.0.G.T. No. 1 Convention (S.W. Lancashire). The monthly meeting of the above cluvention was held on Saturday in the Good Templars' Hall Ashton-in-Makerfield. There was a very good attendance, representatives owl visitors being pre- sent from Warrington, Earlestown, Ashton, St. Helens, Leigh and Tyldesley. Bro George Clarke, C.C.T. (Earlestown), presided, all the executive officers being present with the exception of the C.E After the opening CerkirriolliBi the Pr ese and Credential Committees were appointed. Bro Frannie I read his monthly report, showing a fair arnount lof activity among the lodgeß. The following vkiting deputies gave reverts of visits pail to their respective lodges :—Bro Clarke (Eariehtown) " Hope of Sutton" Lodge; Bro Speakman (Ashton), "Hope of Earlestown" ; Bro Kirwin (Leigh). " Hope of Tyldeelty"; Sister Mrs. Woods Earlestown), " Onward Lodge, Ashton ; Bro E Newman (,4arlestown), "Onward " Lolge, Warrington ; Bro J Sheath ( Warringtoe), " Consolation " Lodge, Widnes. The reports were of a very encouragieg charanrer, and were accepted with tanks to the V.D The report of the Extrii- tive Committee was then preaented. dealing chiefly with open-air mission work. Meetings are to be lael,t at 'Warriogton, Whines, Sutton, and other I Waco&amp; It was decined to hold a public meeting in Warrington in August. The report of the Creiien- ! tial Committee was read and adoptei, and after the usuel votes ot thanks :he convention closed. A pablic meeting was held after tea in the same room: Bro Gorge Clarke, C CT.. presided,and gave an address, explaining the principle of Good Tem- plar:v. Solos were contributed by Sister Sheath (Werrington), Bro Nieholls (Ashton), and a recita- tion was given by Bro Roberts (Ashton). " Happy Jack " (Bro Pirr, of Leigh), gave an address on the I (lElE:king habits of the community.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EXUIINER FRUAY. JULY 13, INO.</head>
        <p>THE EXUIINER FRUAY. JULY 13, INO.</p>
        <p>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The result of the examination in Prictical, Plane, and solid Geometry inconnection with the above classes, is as followa:—S Cawley, 2nd A; W John Graham, Ist ; George Bunting, F Joseph Stringer, 2nd POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Police Court on Nionday morning, before Mr. T. J. Giileepie. Benjamin Davidson, collier, Newton' was charged with being drunk and was fined 2s 6d and CO3tB. Thomas Delaney, labourer,and Horace Daley,act)r,were each charged with being drunk on the 8-,h inst.—Delansy was fined 21 61 and costs, and Daley was seat to gaol for seven days._</p>
        <p>EARLESTOW:4 LIFEBOAT SATUBD MOVEMENT:. The first meeting of the Executive Committer) appointed to carry out the arrangements for a Lifeboat Saturday in Earleetowa and Newton was. held on Tuesday evening. Varhus matters of de- tail were dealt with, and it was decided to vigor- ously push on with the arrangements and to invite the co operation of all classes in making the demonstration a success, The date fixed for the affair was Saturday, September Bth. BOWLING AT 11 AYDOCK.</p>
        <p>In cpnnection with the Haytiock Bowling Club. a sweepstake was played on the club ground on zatur- day afternoon, for the benefit ot Mr T Boardman, toe oldest member ot the club. A. pair of bowls. jack and bag wore presented by Mr Boardman and a few friends, as the first prize. The club added 194 as consolation prizes. There were 64 entries, and the first prize was won by D Marsh, find, ss, It Whittle; 3rd, 2s 61, J Baines; and 4th, 24 Gi, J VIADUCT I NSTI TUTE TEC lINICAL INsTaucTroN CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The following results of science examinations held in connection with the above classes have come to hand :—Nfachime Construction and Draw- ing, W Davies, J F Eckersley, F Gee, 11 Williatn3on; 2nd, All P Bramah, Thomas Porter, A Purnell, Joseph Rice; Ist E, 'e Benson. It A Eaton, W Outte• ridge, R Herring. J Kynaston, J J Merrick. 'V Woodward; 2nd E. Geometrical Drawing, T Beni- son, W Bone, LI P Bratnah, W Dudgeon, Cll Hill, Henry Jolley, P Kenney, J Synaston, P J Mullins, T a Nluscreft, 'V N 1 arsh, A Purnell, J Southern, C WatsonZr Woodward. all 110483.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A COLLIER</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A COLLIER</p>
        <p>At St. Helens County Petty Sessions on Tuesday, William Finn, of Burtonwood, was charged with assaulting Margaret Edwards, eged 14 years, Mr J W Knowles, of Widnes, appeared fore defendant Complainant said that on Friday, June let, she was going home from Sutton, when she mt defendant; who asked her to hold a parcel for him. He then' comAitted the assault complained of by throwing beyito the ground. She screamwl„ and he put a h hdkerchief in her mouth. For the defence Mr. K owles urged that complainant's statement was n t corrotorated. Defendant was drunk, and did I not remember having seen the girl, The Bench fined him £2 an costs. VULCAN WORKS PICNIC CLUB.</p>
        <p>The third annual outing of this club, which embraces many ef the workmen from tile Vulcan Foundry, took place on Saturday under favourable circumstances. At half-past one, about 130 left the Wellington Hotel, Earlestown in tune waggonettes for Abbey Lakes, which they reached at 3-30. Tne drive was thoroughly enjoyed and the interval before tea was pleasantly lccupied in dancing and boating. A substantial knife and fork tea was pro- vided and ample justice done to it. A bowling match was afterwards played, the turners arid machine men, playing the fitters and erectors, the former winning by 68. The party left. for home at 9_30, woich they reachei at 11 o'clock. The arrange- ments were efficiently carried out by Mr. J. Boyer (secretary), Sir it Smith (treasurer) and a commit- tee.</p>
        <p>NEWTON ST. PETER'S CHURCH WORKERS' PICNIC. Tile annual picnic in cinnection with the workers of Sr. Peter's Church, Newton, took place on Satur- day. The parry left Newton at 8.24, in reserved carriages for Bolton Abbey, which tney reached at 11.30. Luncheon was served at the "Devonshire Arms" Hotel, and afcer thi; hal been disposed of, waggonettes were chartered for a drive to "The Strid." On arrival they alighted and walked through the woods to Bardon Bridge, and visited tile ruined tower of Bardon. Here tne conveyances were rejoined and they drove on to the opening of " The Valley of Desolation." Walking up this to the waterfall they again joined the conveyances and drove V) the Abbey. The party were conducted over the Abbey and grounds, and then drove to the hotel where dinner was partaken of. After dinner a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Gray for the excellency of his arrangements Bolton Abbey was left behind at 6 55, and Newton was reached at 930, aftr a most enjoyable day. Sr JOHN'S CHURCH, SENIOR CLASS.</p>
        <p>The annual picnic in connection with this class took place on Saturday to Chester. Leaving Earles- to wn at 7.13, the party numbering 43, accompanied by the leve T R and J C Matthews reached Chester at 825. Breakfast was partaken of at Williamson's refreshment rooms, after which a visit was paid to Dickson's Nurseries, oni of the gardeners acting as guide. The ramble through the gardens was much enjoyed, and a visit was afterwards paid to the Cathedral, where a verger took charge of the patty. The next item was dinner, and it is only fair to say that this part of the programme was entered iwo with much zest. aton Hall was next visited, and an inspection of the gardens proved perhaps the most enjoyable feature of the outing. Tea was par- taken ot at 5 20, and the remainder of the time was spent in a ramble about the city. Chester was left behind at about 9.15 and !tarlestown was reached shortly after 10 o'clock. The whole of the arrange- ments were satisfactorily carried out by the CiU3S Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>"SWLN" INN, NEWTON</head>
        <p>THE "SWLN" INN, NEWTON</p>
        <p>At the Newton Petty Se-sions on Thursday, Mr IT J Liongton made an application to the magis- trates with reference to the "Swan" Inn, Newton Common. lie ,iaid that in August last he submittfsu plans of some alterations and improvements which were agreed to. Since then the builders had been carrying out the alterations and they found that they did not work out as was expected. En the first instance no provision was made at that time for the outdoor departmont—the serving out of the outdoor suppl 9 in a special place and therefore they did not think the house was sufficiently dividen. The vault class of customers were not separated frora the others and the customers could get allover the place. In was propfosed to remedy that. defect by provid- ing a place for the outdoor department and continuing the counter so as to shut off the. vault cu,tomers from the other part of the house and confine them to the vaill,s. The superin- tendent of police suggested that what was now used as a stable should he done away with as a stable and simply used as an outhouse or coach house. His clients agreed to that, suggestion and he submitt-d plans of the proposed improvements.—The applica- tion was granted.</p>
        <p>An Earlestown Electrical _Engineer's Failure.</p>
        <p>The public examination of fie )17-ge flerbert Bent- ley, electrical engineer of Earlestown, was opened at the Warrington Bankruptcy Court. last Prifay. It appears that the nankruat comweneed business on the 17th October. 189, with £5O borrowed capital, at IS, Market-street. A month ago he re- moved to Grafton-street. The statement of affairs disciosed the fact that the liabilities amounted to £l. 47 19. 81; assets £3OB 2e 101 ; deficiency .E 839 161 10,1. Included in the assets was certain machinery which was claimed by the General Electric Company (represented by Mr. New- man) The cost price of the michinery was a little over £3OO. The causes of failure I were alleged by tlie bankrupt to be • Ihtsses of nein?, I tracts and the failure of an engine supplied to him Ito do the amount of work guaranteed by the Vt 373- dors. The engine was guaranteed to do a certain amount of horse power, but failed to do so, with the result, that hecould not drive the dynamo to Atipply the ehrr..llt to consnmers. The loes sustained was not a direct loss, but it diminished the amount of pr woe' ive profit. The cash book gave a full and true account of all receipts and payments by chf-ques and cash to the best of his belief. The pay- ments exceeled the receipts by .C 268 Os 81. lie had pail two instalments amounting to £lBO for the machinery claimed by the General Electric Com- pany.—The examination was adjourned until August 3rd so that the btrikrupt might furnish it statement of his liabilitiss and assets from the date be commenced business, and a profit and loss account from that date to the date of the necessary order.</p>
        <p>TovlnTumam. ors. Winstuw's 60.,thing N7ruo has been u•ei for over fifty vears by millions of roothnrs for their zhilriren while teething. with perfect sturess. It will relieve oonrsuffnrer immediat.Av It is pl.‘annt tn tiste ; and prmineesnatt.r.l r.”1,.t. weep. ny relieving the child from pain in-1 the lit.%) ,nernh awskes "as height as a button."Of all Uharaists is lid per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Sr. HELZNS SCHOOL CHILDREN AT BLACKPOOL. Tee 800 coiliren who heve been selected as the most deserving in the elementary schools of S. Weena alsenebted at the railway station at eight o'clock on Tuesday morning to go on the Mayor's trip to Blackpool. Each child wore a commemorative nestle!, and when the Mayor Mr. Bees.ham) and Mr Rowed .drove up to the station they were greeted by a loud outburst ef cheer- ing. At Blackpool the children who were accompanied by thirty-five teachers, were providedwite a capital tea, and free aclnissien to the Tower. SHOCKING, END TO A. SPREE AT Sr. HELENS. At St. Hetet-is Town ail!, on Saturday, Mr. S. Brig- house, couaty coroner, held the inquest on Robert Cathcart, sped twenty-two, who lived with his married Slate? at 35. Arthur-street, and who was found drowned in the Se Helens canal, near Island's-brow bridge on Thursday night. The evidence showed that on Satur- day, June 30, deceased, with two men named Gill and Knowles, were drinking in a public-house until eleven o'clock. They then took a gallon jar of beer to the heuss of Catheart'e uncle, at Island's-brow. They left this house about half-past ene on Sunday morning. De- waged was then playing an accordion. They were not sole'r whsn they left. Gill said that before getting to the bridge deceasee gave him the accordion. Gill and his companion Knowles walked on, and when they got ever toe canal bridge they missed deceased. They shouted, but got no answer, and concluded that deceased had gone back to his uncle's house. They called at deceased's sister'e house, and reported that they had left Cathcart behind. There had been no quarrel, and there were no marks on the body.—A verdict of " Acci- dental death" was returned. A Sr. HELENS RAILWAY MAN KILLED. Mr. S. Brighouse, county coronet, on Saturday, held the inquest on the body of John Toft, aged 47, a charge- man's assistant in the signal department of the London and North •Western, who was killed near the Posk No ,k sig,nal-b)x at the S. Helens goods yard the pre- v oars evening.—Henry Acourt, of 10, South John-street, ' St. Helen, cha.rgeman of the signal department, said d ceased was his assistant.. At a quarter to five on Friday rright he saw him cleaning some signal pulley s about one hundred yards from the cabin. Witness was wider the cabin when he heard a shout, and saw de- ceased under some waggons, about twelve yards away from the cabin. He was terria iy mangled, and di: din a minute or two. Witneas thought deceased bad been csmine to the signal biz tor some oil. There waste very high wind, and deceased could not bear the train, and the engine driver could not see deceased, because he was ruanine tender first, with a brake van in front. The -e) must have stru k deceare d and knocked him down. —J,len Sampson, acting traffic inspector, who had pee- pare a sketch plan of the scPne of the accident, was complimented by the coroner. Sampson had saved them a lot of time. People in much higher positions than 'lampoon came to those inquiries,and did not show half the sense Sampson had done.—A, verdict of •fr Acci- dental death "was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>NOTES BY THE- WAY AT EARLESTOWN. [BY A CORRESPONDENT.] CRICKET.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE- WAY AT EARLESTOWN. [BY A CORRESPONDENT.] CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The woather on Saturday was all that could be desired from a cricketer's point of view, and conse- quently all the fixtures of the- local, clubs were brought off. Earlestown had as their opponents Cheethum, and although playing on their Own ground, had to succumb to defeat. Cheetham have always been found by Earlestown a bard nut to crack an 1 6aturday was no exception. the home team went to the wickets first and although Hus- band piled on a score of 51 before being finally caught, his. side were all dismissed for 107. Cheetham made 141 for the loss of only 9, wickets, but it must be understood that Alderson, the home team's principal bowler was absent through indisposition; and doubtlezls this fact had some influence on the game. ltylance and Javies, however, did excep- tionally well against such batsmen and their efforta deserve every praise.</p>
        <p>In the-encounter between the second teams at Cheethain, the latter were victorious by the narrow majority of four runs. Robinson made a capital stand for 39, being unfortunately run out, but his score proved of immense advantage to-his side.</p>
        <p>Newton fairly swam pedAigburth,the scores being 164 and 80 respectively,</p>
        <p>Tall scoring was the order of the day so far as Newton was concerned,Turton beingresponsible for 44, Mitchell 47, and Carson 28. luish was to the front again in the bowling department, capturing five of the Aigburth wickets at little expense.:</p>
        <p>The meeting ot the second elevens remitted in a. victory for Newton by 175 to 129; Henderson's score of 46 was a meritorious performance, as also was Rylauce's 39;</p>
        <p>The Weeleyans journeyed to Moore to play the club of that ilk, but had to be sa,isfted with a draw altuough the, WeAleyans scored 96 to Moore 39, for 6, wicker. C Porter was top scorer with 28-, sal H Clare was a good scitid with 15. Theo Clare was very successful with the ball.</p>
        <p>The• Primitives are still hunting for points in the St. Lleteris and District Junior League, as on Satur- day they suffered another defeat, this time by Windle. It was a very near thing, however,, the scores being,. Primitives, 53,; Windie, 55.</p>
        <p>To-mosrow (Saturday) is the Earlestown Cricket Derby day, The Narlestown first eleven have set th,.!mselves the task of playing I&amp; juniors of the. district. The juniors are drawn from the Ear/es. town tipcond eleven (6), Earlsstown Wesleyans (6), and Primitive Methodists (6). If the weather be fine there-will certainly be a big crowd of specta- tors, and sport should be interesting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>MANCHEStER AND LIVERPOOL DISTRICT BANKING COMPANY LIMITED.</head>
        <p>THE MANCHEStER AND LIVER- POOL DISTRICT BANKING COMPANY LIMITED.</p>
        <p>An ordinary m,eting of the Shareholders of this Com- pany wifl be held at the Head Offie, Spring Gardess, MarieheAter, on WedneAdav next, at twelve o'c:ock noon to receiva th 'wort of the dirPctors and tiv, balanc-. sheet of the Cornp*ny, as at 30th June, 1900, and to deelsre a dividflad.</p>
        <p>The Direemrs report that tho gross profit f*r the haf. ye*r ecid.rig 30th June I,st, 4tpr providing for all bid ald dont-Attu deb's, is £204,748 43 11 to which has to 1:0 added the artio.tnt t0.0.1e.117, forwari from 30-n D,tcPmber last,viz : £24-.903 91 61; total £229,651 14s SC Fr I his sum the folowini deductions ha vii to b• male t—Tni-al expevses at the fl,-.ad Office, Lon ion Otii :e, and brancfms, including sal 'ries, stationery, taxes, kc., £76,391 is 83; arrount in of aank Pr:Tony accoun', £5,000. total £81,39/ 74 8 i ; leaving a net profit of £148,260 63 5-1 ; paid 0.1 2.d instant on the first instalment of the new shares, £2.500 Os Od ; the directors rec*minend tie declaration of a dlvidend of 154 a share, and bonus pt Si a ahare, Ng:veil will take £lOO 000 0401 ; total £102,500 Os 03 : leay.ng a balance of £45,760 6s 91 ; Of tens it is proposed to carry to toe R-s..rve Fund £20,000 Os Od and to n..xt excitant £25,769 63 9 tot al £45,70 6. 9i. The Res,rve Fund, with the above addi- tr.in, nod 2189,000 prmiomar on new shares to date, will then atnount to £1,359,000.</p>
        <p>ILoyal Order of Ancient Shepherds A.O , 1 Warrington District. t The quart?rly mei, mg of the above district was heel. under the presideucy of Bro. F S Massey, at the Thorn Tree Lodge, Thorn Inn, Appleton, on Saturday. He was supported by the Deputy Ceief, Bro. Thomas Miller ; Trustees. Bros. W Eckersley and William Hall; niitrict seeretary, P aigar ; and 20 other delegates, representing over 900 members. After the formal opening of the lodge,the Secretary at the request of the President, presented the accounts tor the post quarter, whice proved to be highly satisfactory. A substantial gain had been made to the funeral fund And on the accounts being ct,rtified by the an itors (B roe Kirkham and Adams) as correct, the f•erne were passed,—The following were elected officers for the current year, viz. :- - President 13,0. T Miller ; vice-president. Bro. John 1 •Liale,v ; district examiners, Bros. L Weir and D Harris. After the Arbitration Camr.nittee and district auditor had ben elected, Bro. William Hall gave a report of the proceedings of the A.M.C. of the Order, held at Bristol in Whit-week. lie touched upon several matters of importance and a short discussion took place. On the motion of Bro. Haley. a hearty vote of thanks was passel to him. One of the most important items on the ag Oa was the questioe of forming a fund to be called the" krmy 11.1. serve and Volunteer Fund." Prom the a.fficer'B report it appears there are at pre- sent 15 members a Tying their Queen and country in toe South African campaign. The lodge., with one exemption, cad been for several months keeping these brethren good on the books. It was, however, thought advisable to form a district fund, from wl,ich th, contributions of the whole of such nv,m- hers could be paid, and the delegates assembled de- cided to make such a levy to enable this to be done 1 whilst such members were at the front. A pleasant ! mete jug terminved by the installation of the I n -wly-elected officers, a hearty vote of thanks being i octt•s6d to the retiring Chief for his impartial con• I duct during the past year.</p>
      </div>
