      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>ASSAULT ON A SCHOOL TEACHER AT NEWTON</head>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A SCHOOL TEACHER AT NEWTON</p>
        <p>interesting Case at the Sessions.</p>
        <p>' At the Newton Petty Selsions on Thursday before Meesre. J Jack:ion and W Collingwood, Edna Burgess was eureen,ned hr aesaul'ine E litn Deem I,:one,a girl, at Newton on June 25:h.—Mr. A Smith, of Wigan appearectfnr the comptaieant, and the defendant who pleaded guilty was repreeented by Mr. J W Knowlea. Mr* Smith said that the complainant was a pupil teacher at3t.Peter'a School and it became her duty to report something that had been done by the defendant's boy, to Mr. Singleton the headmaster. This came to the knowledge ot the defendant who lived oppose to the schnol and the came to the school yard where the com- plainant was engaged in getting the beys of standard OR) together for the purpose of taking them in the school. It was necessary for the scholars to go through the boys' school yard and the 'girls' yard into the school'. Defendant curie to the gate and threat- ened what she would do to the girl when she came out, for sendieg her eon to Mr. Singleton, and also made nee of bad language. When the girl was passing along the street, the defendant went up te her and struck her a violent blow with the flat of her hand on the side of the face. She was aileut to strike another blow when a Mrs Beardman, who lived on the opposite side of the esreet, interfered. The complainant was so upset by the occur- rence that she was unable to perform her duties that afternoon. The be-dmaster's attentioe was called to her, and he found that there was a serious mark on' the side of her face, showing the vielenee of the blow. The girl was simply doing her duty in reporting the bovrand he asked the Bench to treat this assault on a different footing from ordin try assaults, because if parents were to take in bend teachers-who had a duty to perform. then the ciscipline of the' school was- at an end. This assault was an aggravated case, because it was not com- mitted away from the echo)! premises., but in the pre- sence of the small boys over whom the complainant had to exercise control.</p>
        <p>Complainavt gave evidence. When she was in the school yard defendant said she would ' sm4ck her face when she came out and knock her head against the 47411. As she was going through Ildercer-strest defendant struck her on the Ade of the face. She was stunaerl by the blow aud was unable 0 take the class that after- noon.</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles for the defence, said thatras the Bench wer aware the law ot Eagland was that two wrongs did not make one right. On the date in question de- fendant's boy was sent by the complainant to the head- master to be caned. Hia client told him V.at the boy was caned for going to a meet &amp;hop to buy sweets during the quarter of an hour's playtime that was allowed. 0 her boys did this and the defendant thought the caning was unjustifiable;</p>
        <p>Mr. Sniith : Ti caning never took place. Mi. Knowles has no right to state that which is antic; accordance with fact.</p>
        <p>The Clerk R is entitled to give bi; cllent'4 version. Mr. Smith: Not in accordance with an imaginary version.</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles: lam sure I won't do anything inten— tionally wrong. Continuing he said be hid known schoolmasters who bad Milled boys unjustifi4bly and they had been severely dealt with for doing no. The defendant, Instead of consulting a solicitor with regard to whether the boy wa3 legally caned or not took the , law into her own hands. She was never . written to.or. spoken to by anyone in connection with the school. The first intimation she rereived we', a summons, which was only taken out on the 12th July. When she received it she went to the girl to apologise but the complainant said the matter was out of her hands and she must see the sthoolmaster. She went to apologise to the school• master and be said the matter would have to bee, dealt with by the Court Defendant was a r•qpeetable woman with eight children. She lost her temper and on the spur of the moment did wrong. She would have sent a written apology which could have been read before the scriool children. She had never been in a Curt of Justice in her life and expressed' regret fr what had' oc- curred. The statuze particularly pointed nut that in frivolous cases of assault, or where it did not appear that any pecuniary punishment was merited the case could be dismissed on payment of costs. Defendant ad- mitted that she had no right to tackle the matter in this way. _ _</p>
        <p>The Bondi retired to ern id r 'll.‘?z-deciffen and nn thy le return the Cnairmsa sari they felt school teachers must be protected from assaults of this character. Dafecdant would be fined 103 and costs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith sail he was •iodred 13! the heaclmaste.r to say that it was not a fact that the defenthat'g boy wag caned neither was it a fact tr at the boys when in the playoyund were allowed to go to the sweet shop. The ordees of the echo)l were exactly to the c-mtrary and' the complainant was doing her duty in reporting the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WITH THE WARRINGTON CYCLISTS.</head>
        <p>WITH THE WARRINGTON CYCLISTS.</p>
        <p>A Pleasant time at Beeston Castle:</p>
        <p>If the members of the Warrington Cycling Club were askcd to point out the most enjsyable outing on their fixture card it is probable that toe one to Reeston Castle. would be found to be the most poputtr. For several years now, the members have spent the first Saturday in July amid the quiet and peaceful scecory which sur- rounds BePston Castle. liere, Mr. John Naylor, the este, med president of the club reeides, and witti his usual generosity the cyclists and their friends are granted the privilege of inspecting his house and grounds, and afterwards partaking of tea on the remain court. In response to an invitation from the courteous- Secretary of the Warriegton Cycting Clot), ,a reprFsenta- tive of the '• Examiner" found himself in oJinpany wirh a number of cyclists at Bank Qoay Station on Saturday afternoon. It did seem a litte anonsaloue to go a-picnicing with cyclirs by rail. I would have seemed more appropriate to have mounted a bicycle and sped along the roa, fs for twenty miles or more into the heart of Cheshire, until Weston Castle was reached. Some of the cyclists dii travel in this way, bet the majority proceeded by train. flee explanation is •not, far to seek. On this auspicious ecctsion the members are enjoined to bring their lady friends, And as the fair sex are not prepared to undertake such a long journey by road, either from lack of energy orbieyeles, the iron ' horse has to be resorted to.</p>
        <p>The rai!way journey to Beeston Castle is.. rather of a tedious kind owing to the difficulty with which this, pleasant Cheshire village is reached. It , involves a journey to Chester where a change of trains is:made eslld a long wait exporienced before ,the juirunv is resurred along the Cnelter and Crew° lino to Beeston Castle, which is within two miles of Tarporley. From the rail- way station a view of Mr Naylor's resideace is .ibtained. Standing on an eminence ic commands a. fine view oil' the surrounding country and its arclaiti.ctoral design gives it an attraction ail its own. The party immediately set their faces towards Castle Hill and were met at the gates by Mr. ad Mrs Na.vlor who heartily welcomed them. Mr. Naylor is himself an enthusiastic cyclist. He is the acknowledged representative of the Cyclists Touring Club for three counties and does a great deal of work in connection with it. His house is the M-ecca many cyclists aad each year the number visiting it in- creases.</p>
        <p>Saturday's party from Warrington numbered upwarda of siaty. The elements which always have Po much to do with tho titlre'.s9 ot mica a gathering were delight- fully benign. The sun son' forth radiamly hut the heat was tempered by a gcntle aphyr, whinh was appreciated to the full. As the party walked ahour. the grounds the scene was animated and picturesque. The bright sunny weather conduced to a brilliant display of millinery hp the ladies who were clad in light summer attire. The house was also thrown open for inspection. The numerous. small rooma are easily furuith id hut the most peculiar feature of the house is the long winding staircase leading to the upper moms, the verandah ad the tower. Once on the top the visi- tors here a disinclination to descend the na7row and crookel way, but the trouble in reaching the tower is well repaid by the splendid view which is obtained. By using the telescepe and fi,ll glasses provided, a fine panoramic view, is obtainecl of the country for miles around. Bolsters. Castle is seen in all its historic beauty whilat a little fertlipr to the South. Peckf:srton Castle sten,ts out prominently. Not a sli:Ay claimney is to he seen in any dirfiction and as one drinks in the t'eaut; of the scene he is imoressNt by nature's handiwork. Within the, tower a delightful room 33 provided and here music and song were transmitted by the phonograph. The greursda are well laid-out and trimly kept. The conservator,es, too, are delightfel houses ef thwers and at the rear is an excellent kitchen garden. Tea was served on the tennis court. Tema was salmon, fresh !rim the Dee and luscious strawberries in abundance. At the conclusion of an exclUent repast, an impromptu emcert was held en the lawn. The pano was brought outside and with Mr. Grounds as the presiling goniuS i:s notes were heard to advaneage. Mr, W. Ireland sang several songs, including" Mary," which is admirably suited to his pure-tJued tenor voice. The humour of tho entertainment was well mainttined by Mr. Emmon and Mr. Alfred Grounds. Songs were also sung by Mr. W. Wood and Mr. Pembezton, The company also engaged in several dances, and "tripped the light fantastic toe" on the green sward, until the time approaeird tor the journey homeward. Mr. William Bellian, the poprlar captain, who faithfully discharged the duties of M.C., proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Nayler for tivir hospitaii,y, and said the members of the club owed them a debt of gratitude they would never be able to repay. It was their wish that they might be spared for many years to continue their useful work, and that Mr. Naylor would long continue in office as their president. (Applause).</p>
        <p>Mr. DAVIE% better known among cyclists as" Pa DAvies." 54ic.1 it afforded him much pleasure to second the motion. With regard to the journey to limf sori, be wished moo of the " boys " had crtne by road, but he supplsed the train had been mace so mach use of because the members were accompanied by their lady friends. Re should not like it to be said that the War- rington Cycling Club was decaying,especially when they cams to ace Cresar at Rome. Re looked uponiMr.</p>
        <p>Naylor at he rlarof cycling, and on of the leaders, if not the leader, in the North of England. He hoped the neat time ftey viiedßeeeton they would travel to and fro on their aviCbioP3. (floor, hear, and laughter). Mr. PrCnA7AYCZ, in supTerting the motion said he sueposed the picieeeclines con,d harrlty be brought to a gucceuar2: josury if the •• old rain " had not said a few words to them. fie also, with the previous speakers, wiehecl to testify to the great kindness which Mr. and Mrs. Naylor had shown to them on that occasion and on other similar occueieos. They had been most hospit- ably entertalmed by their worthy President, who he trusted, woulel be pared for reany years- to fill that position, and also•thcet Mrs. Nay/Yer would continue her kind hoTitality to the members, which, he could assuse he • was very warmly appreciated. (A.pplauee.) The motion ware heartily agreed to. Mr. NAYLOR, la reniy,, said that SOrrla of the members had not come on t.114r bicycles, althoteg,h they ought to have done so. With Mre Davis, he 'Leeped they would make use of them next year if the weateer were favour- able. On that and on other occaeises they had been ex- tremely lucky on the ECOrO of the weather whenever the Cycling Club had paid them a visit, and, he could assure them that although they had many visitors there wag no institution they weree more pleased to see than the mem- bers and friends of the' WarricKtin Cyclinee Club. (Ap- plause.) On the first two visits no ladies .came, and when WI remonstrated with the members halt a dozen ladies came, and on that' °evasion the number had been considerably increased. Next year he hoped each mem- ber would bring a lady friend. (Hear hear, and ap- ple u 4).) The party afterwards left for home.</p>
      </div>
