        <p>THE FAILURE OF IVIESSRS.JAMES BARKER AND SON. _- Examination Yesterday. At the Warrington Bankrup-cy Court on Friday, be- 'ore Mr Registrar Ridg say, James Barker, of the Atlas Engineering Works, Bavdock-street, appeared for his public examination. According co the bankrupt's statement he commenced business abeue 33 years aeo in pa rteiersh!p with Peter Newall at the Atlas Works, Haydock- street trading as Newell dud Barker. They each put in £2O capital. Mr. Newell died in IEIB7, arid since that time be bad carried MI the business alone except for twelve months in 1890 when it was carried on by his landlord, Mr. Whitley, who traded under the style of the Alas Engineering Company. Be (tta. bankrupt) bad traded as James Barker and Son sine', 1890 but the son had never been a partner. The lieblities amounted to £1.492 12s 5d ; assets, £476 183 93 ; deficieney,£l,ols 13s8 I. By Mr. Johnson : Be took the business over on a rental and paid nothing for the stock. That arrange- • tnent had br en carried out to the present day.llis son had never been a partner with him neither had he received any share of the profits. He traded as James Barker and Sin because he thotieht the name of a fit m better than an individual one. No egreement bad been made with the son that he should share the prc fite or losses of the business. No profits were ever paid to him and he had never claimed them. He did not claim to he a partner and to his (the bankrupt's) knowledge had not held himself out as such. Mr. Johnson: Why did you not take your son into partnership? The Debtor: I thought I would wait and see if there was any prospect of the place progressing before I did so Mr. Johnson: The business was not successful ?—N3t very. Is it net a fact that it hae proved mere or less unsuc- cessful since you purchased it in 1892?—Well it has more or less. Have you ever made any prAits P—l hays made some profits. Have you ever made sufficient profits for your main- tenance.—Yes up to four years ago. Further examined the debtor said he attributed his failure to 10-ses in trade and by fire and be had also had some opposition to contend with. Do vote mean you had serious competition to contend with 9—l do sir. What was the result of that ?—I did not get contracts that I thought I weuld gee It reduced the volume of your businees9—Yes ; then there was a great increase in the price of material. Wes that a serious matter for you P—lt was from 1897 to 1899. Why did you not charee higher prices when they cost you more ?—We had contracts on hand and we bad to get materials to make un 'he contracts. Further examined : He first became aware of his in- solvency between two and three years ago In him statement of affairs there were deb s contracted in 1895 1896, 1897, and 1898, but he had exp. c'el to be alai, to pay them by m king the business into a limited company. His liabilities included £285 which was advanced for a hares in the proposed limbed company by Thomas Lechett. Be thought at the time that he was fully justified in using the money in the business as he ex- pected that the business would be successful. it was with Mr. Lockett's consent that he used it in that way. He kept a cash book up to the time of the fire but he had not done 80 since then. He had only used a ledger. He had not made tesriodieal statements of his liabilities and assets berause he did not know that it was requisite. He bad paid his son £2 53 a week since 1892. By Mr. Longton (who appeared 011 behalf of E Picks- vance, one of the creditors) : His son did not act as a partner. Has he had equal authority with yourself 9—Not exactly. Do you mean to say that if he gave an order as James Barker and Seri you would not have sanctioned it P—l allowed him to sign oh gees and give orders provided I had been made conversant with them before. Further qirestioned : The insurance money that he received after the fife was expended in paying accounts. Fie lead not got the receipts. He paid the mane% to Mr. Jenkins in repaymeot Of advences he had previously made and the rest was paid to c -editors. The examination was adjeurned until August 3rd.</p>
